	(373, 6, 81, 'Set tree spacing from the rootstock, the training system and the soil, rather than a single figure applied across the block.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Tree spacing', 'confirmed'),
	(374, 6, 81, 'Choose the rootstock against vigour, precocity, anchorage and the disease pressure of the site; the comparison table sets the characteristics side by side.', 'Disease management', 'Rootstock selection', 'confirmed'),
	(375, 8, 81, 'Manage the row middles as a distinct zone from the tree row: they carry the traffic, hold the soil, and feed the beneficial insects.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Row middle management', 'confirmed'),
	(376, 8, 81, 'Maintain the orchard sod deliberately -- species, mowing height and frequency -- rather than letting it revert.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Sod management', 'confirmed'),
	(377, 8, 81, 'Learn to read deficiency and toxicity symptoms in the field, and use them to direct sampling rather than to replace it.', 'Nutrient management', 'Deficiency symptoms', 'confirmed'),
	(378, 34, 81, 'Thin at blossom where the crop load is already clearly set to overrun; blossom thinning acts earlier than any postbloom spray.', 'Thinning', 'Blossom thinning', 'confirmed'),
	(380, 8, 81, 'Record every growth regulator application -- product, rate, date, conditions -- since response depends on all of them.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Record keeping', 'confirmed'),
	(381, 30, 81, 'Set hive numbers per hectare against the block, the cultivar and the weather expected during bloom.', 'Pollination', 'Hive stocking rate', 'confirmed'),
	(382, 30, 81, 'Put the pollination agreement in writing with the beekeeper: hive strength, delivery and removal dates, and placement.', 'Pollination', 'Pollination agreement', 'confirmed'),
	(383, 34, 81, 'Check the toxicity rating and the safe timing of any product applied near bloom before it goes in the tank.', 'Pollination', 'Spray timing around bees', 'confirmed'),
	(379, 61, 81, 'Use prohexadione-calcium to suppress shoot blight, timing it to shoot growth rather than to bloom.', 'Disease management', 'Fire blight suppression', 'confirmed'),
	(361, 6, 81, 'Install the support system soon after planting, particularly on dwarfing rootstocks: wind rocks the tops and breaks the new roots.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Tree support', 'confirmed'),
	(385, 46, 81, 'Work through the soil, nutritional, vigour and crop-load causes of low-calcium fruit, and correct the ones that apply to the block.', 'Nutrient management', 'Bitter pit risk', 'confirmed'),
	(392, 8, 81, 'Automate irrigation from soil-moisture sensors where the block justifies it, keeping a manual check on the result.', 'Irrigation', 'Automated irrigation', 'confirmed'),
	(393, 8, 81, 'Collect orchard weather from an on-site station or a network such as NEWA; the disease and thinning models are only as good as the data behind them.', 'IPDM', 'Weather station', 'confirmed'),
	(394, 26, 81, 'Use orchard heating for freeze protection where the block layout and fuel cost make it viable.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Frost heating', 'confirmed'),
	(395, 27, 81, 'Pulse the sprinklers where water supply will not sustain a continuous rate through the freeze.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Pulsed sprinkling', 'confirmed'),
	(396, 26, 81, 'Read the critical temperature for the bud stage the block is actually at before deciding to run frost protection.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Critical temperatures', 'confirmed'),
	(397, 15, 81, 'Deploy pheromone traps at about one per hectare, covering the warmest part of the block and last season''s hotspots, at least a week before bud break for light brown apple moth and before bloom for codling and oriental fruit moth.', 'IPDM', 'Pheromone trapping', 'confirmed'),
	(398, 26, 81, 'Check the traps daily until the first sustained moth flight, and set that date as the biofix the degree-day model counts from.', 'IPDM', 'Biofix', 'confirmed'),
	(399, 27, 81, 'Accumulate degree days from biofix using daily maximum and minimum temperatures against the pest''s lower developmental threshold, and time the first spray from the model rather than the calendar.', 'IPDM', 'Degree days', 'confirmed'),
	(400, 8, 81, 'Use the least disruptive chemistry that will do the job, and only when it is needed, so lady beetles, lacewings, parasitic wasps and predatory mites keep working in the block.', 'IPDM', 'Beneficial insects', 'confirmed'),
	(401, 8, 81, 'Rotate chemical action groups so the same active is never applied repeatedly, and use full label rates with thorough coverage; part rates and poor coverage are what breed resistance.', 'IPDM', 'Resistance management', 'confirmed'),
	(402, 8, 81, 'Meet the legal obligations that come with applying pesticides: records, withholding periods, protective equipment and drift management.', 'IPDM', 'Spray responsibilities', 'confirmed'),
	(403, 8, 81, 'Dispose of unwanted fruit properly and destroy feral and neglected trees nearby; both are reservoirs that reinfect the block.', 'IPDM', 'Orchard hygiene', 'confirmed'),
	(404, 8, 81, 'Time weed control so pests cannot use weeds as an alternative site to survive winter.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Weed timing', 'confirmed'),
	(405, 20, 81, 'Avoid overhead irrigation where it wets foliage and favours infection, unless it is being run for frost or heat management and the reasons are compelling.', 'Irrigation', 'Overhead irrigation and disease', 'confirmed'),
	(406, 7, 81, 'Young non-bearing trees do not need the bearing-tree spray schedule; monitor instead for the pests and diseases that threaten tree health.', 'IPDM', 'Non-bearing spray program', 'confirmed'),
	(407, 7, 81, 'Keep young trees out of stress with adequate nutrition and irrigation and by removing weed competition; increased compost, a sound fertiliser regime and regular monitoring are what get them to full potential.', 'IPDM', 'Young tree stress', 'confirmed'),
	(408, 7, 81, 'Run a protective schedule against scab on young trees, and remove and mulch infected leaves over winter so the infection does not carry into the next season.', 'Disease management', 'Scab in young trees', 'confirmed'),
	(411, 6, 81, 'Control weeds hard while the orchard is establishing; rapid canopy development and early cropping depend on it, and in a capital-intensive block so does profitability.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Establishment weed control', 'confirmed'),
	(412, 8, 81, 'Identify the high-priority weeds for the district and target them before they seed; marshmallow is a major one in pome fruit, and its woody taproot resists mechanical control.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Priority weeds', 'confirmed'),
	(413, 8, 81, 'Set spray volume from tree row volume rather than a fixed rate per hectare, so the dose matches the canopy actually being sprayed.', 'IPDM', 'Tree row volume', 'confirmed'),
	(414, 8, 81, 'Check the pH of the spray water: some products break down quickly in alkaline water and lose activity before they reach the target.', 'IPDM', 'Spray water pH', 'confirmed'),
	(415, 8, 81, 'Follow the regional soil and plant analysis guidelines for sampling depth, timing and interpretation, so results can be compared season to season.', 'Nutrient management', 'Soil and plant analysis', 'confirmed'),
	(416, 8, 81, 'Set the season''s fertility program from the published recommendations for apples, adjusted by the block''s own analysis results.', 'Fertilization', 'Fertility program', 'confirmed'),
	(417, 34, 81, 'Work through the pollination, honeybee and pesticide guidance together before spraying anything at bloom.', 'Pollination', 'Pesticides during bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(418, 8, 81, 'Calculate spray volume and calibrate the sprayer by the published method before the season, and re-check it when nozzles or speed change.', 'IPDM', 'Spray volume calculation', 'confirmed'),
	(419, 8, 81, 'Check a product''s toxicity to orchard predators before choosing it; the cheapest option is often the one that costs the most in lost biological control.', 'IPDM', 'Predator toxicity', 'confirmed'),
	(420, 8, 81, 'Keep applicator certification and licensing current for everyone who applies pesticides on the block.', 'IPDM', 'Applicator certification', 'confirmed'),
	(386, 50, 81, 'Run a preharvest bitter pit assessment on susceptible cultivars such as Honeycrisp, and let the result decide the storage plan.', 'Nutrient management', 'Preharvest bitter pit test', 'confirmed'),
	(391, 6, 81, 'Size the drip system to the block and calculate the application rate before scheduling anything from it.', 'Irrigation', 'Drip system design', 'confirmed'),
	(389, 53, 81, 'Control postharvest diseases through sanitation and handling first, and treatment second.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Postharvest disease control', 'confirmed'),
	(409, 53, 81, 'Treat postharvest rots as an orchard problem: many begin at flowering and fruit growth and only appear in store, so orchard protection, fruit nutrition and shed hygiene all bear on the outcome.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Postharvest rot origin', 'confirmed'),
	(421, 8, 81, 'Read the label as the legal document it is: rates, timing, re-entry and withholding periods all sit there, and it overrides any guide.', 'IPDM', 'Reading the label', 'confirmed'),
	(422, 8, 81, 'Work from the full list of essential elements, not just the ones being applied: carbon, hydrogen and oxygen come from air and water, the rest from the soil.', 'Nutrient management', 'Essential elements', 'confirmed'),
	(424, 6, 81, 'Decide the planting density and row width before anything is ordered; on flat ground 3 m rows are workable, and the whole system follows from that choice.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Intensive system planning', 'confirmed'),
	(425, 6, 81, 'Check the site factors that decide whether an intensive system will pay before committing to it; the advantages assume the site can support the density.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Site suitability for density', 'confirmed'),
	(426, 8, 81, 'Match the sprayer to the canopy: axial air-blast, multi-head wrap-around, recycling and tower sprayers each place the spray differently, and the choice shows up in both coverage and drift.', 'IPDM', 'Sprayer type', 'confirmed'),
	(427, 8, 81, 'Set up the sprayer to keep the spray in the canopy: air output, pressure and travel speed all move drift as much as nozzle choice does.', 'IPDM', 'Drift management', 'confirmed'),
	(428, 8, 81, 'Manage birds strategically rather than reactively: combine techniques, and cooperate with neighbours, since birds move across boundaries.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Bird damage strategy', 'confirmed'),
	(432, 10, 81, 'Count the flower buds on five representative trees and take the mean, then prune to leave 1.5 to 2 times the buds needed for the target crop -- never to the exact target, since frost, poor pollination and poor flower quality will each take a share.', 'Thinning', 'Crop load target', 'confirmed'),
	(433, 10, 81, 'Count the buds left after pruning as an early check that the block is actually tracking towards its crop load goal, rather than assuming the cuts achieved it.', 'Thinning', 'Post-pruning bud count', 'confirmed'),
	(434, 10, 81, 'Reduce flower bud numbers by removing one to three whole limbs and the secondary laterals on those remaining, rather than shortening everything.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Precision pruning method', 'confirmed'),
	(436, 45, 81, 'Wait until fruit reach 6 mm before judging the response to pruning, bloom and petal-fall thinning; initial set cannot be read reliably before then.', 'Thinning', 'Six millimetre assessment', 'confirmed'),
	(437, 45, 81, 'Run the carbon balance model at 6 mm, when fruit enter log growth and carbohydrate demand peaks: the greater the deficit, the more easily the tree will thin. Treat it as a warning signal, not an instruction.', 'Thinning', 'Carbon balance model', 'confirmed'),
	(438, 45, 81, 'Whatever the carbon balance indicates, temperature decides the outcome; check it before and after application rather than trusting the model alone.', 'Thinning', 'Temperature governs response', 'confirmed'),
	(439, 45, 81, 'Measure fruitlet diameters and read the fruit growth model -- the only one that shows accurately whether a fruit will set or abscise. Around 15 spurs is enough to see the trend without running the full model.', 'Thinning', 'Fruit growth model', 'confirmed'),
	(440, 8, 81, 'Rotate insecticides and miticides between Mode of Action groups; it is alternation across MoA groups, not switching product names, that slows resistance.', 'IPDM', 'Mode of action rotation', 'confirmed'),
	(441, 8, 81, 'Understand how resistance arises -- metabolic, target-site and penetration -- because it explains why part rates and repeated use of one group select for it so effectively.', 'IPDM', 'Resistance mechanisms', 'confirmed'),
	(442, 8, 81, 'Follow the published fungicide resistance strategy for each disease, and where products are tank-mixed or co-formulated apply the most stringent strategy that covers the pathogen most at risk.', 'Disease management', 'Fungicide resistance strategy', 'confirmed'),
	(443, 8, 81, 'Treat netting, tunnels and other protected structures as high resistance-risk: continuous infection pressure drives repeat applications at short intervals, which is exactly what selects for resistance.', 'Disease management', 'Protected cropping resistance', 'confirmed'),
	(444, 8, 81, 'Hold current chemical accreditation for anyone who purchases and applies pesticides without supervision; AQF Level 3 is the standard for that role in Australia.', 'IPDM', 'Chemical accreditation', 'confirmed'),
	(445, 45, 81, 'Concentrate calcium sprays in the first six weeks after fruit set. That is when the surface-to-volume ratio is high, transpiration carries water and calcium into the fruitlets, and uptake actually happens; afterwards it levels off and dilutes as the fruit expands.', 'Fertilization', 'Calcium timing window', 'confirmed'),
	(446, 45, 81, 'Chemical thinners work by stressing the tree during rapid shoot and fruit growth, so managing the soluble carbohydrate supply through that window changes how sensitive the fruitlets are to the spray.', 'Thinning', 'Carbohydrate manipulation', 'confirmed'),
	(447, 7, 81, 'Apply compost or straw mulch to young trees for surface cover and moisture retention; it breaks down to release nutrients slowly, and the reapplication interval lengthens as the orchard matures.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Compost and mulch', 'confirmed'),
	(448, 8, 81, 'Where a block has a persistent problem, take paired leaf tests from two separate areas of it and read the N:P and N:K ratios, not just the individual levels, to locate the imbalance.', 'Nutrient management', 'Paired leaf tests', 'confirmed'),
	(449, 8, 81, 'Track soil organic carbon and mineralisable nitrogen alongside the annual nutrient test; they show the energy available for microbial activity, which the nutrient figures alone do not.', 'Nutrient management', 'Soil carbon and mineralisable nitrogen', 'confirmed'),
	(451, 34, 81, 'Consider priming hives with sucrose and flower aroma to lift foraging, noting that the response differs between apples and pears because pear foraging is driven by pollen rather than nectar.', 'Pollination', 'Hive priming', 'confirmed'),
	(423, 66, 81, 'Read the leaf and fruit symptoms together: yellow and red leaf colouring, small early-maturing fruit and excessive growth each point to a different imbalance.', 'Nutrient management', 'Deficiency diagnosis', 'confirmed'),
	(430, 53, 81, 'Consider ozone in the store to suppress fungi and moulds and to hold ethylene down, which slows the ripening it would otherwise drive.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Ozone treatment', 'confirmed'),
	(452, 8, 81, 'Set the weekly irrigation from measured evapotranspiration for the district rather than a fixed schedule; tree water use climbs steadily from about 0.04 in/day in early April to several times that by midsummer.', 'Irrigation', 'Weekly water use', 'confirmed'),
	(453, 8, 81, 'Evaluate the irrigation system itself, not just the schedule: uneven pressure across slopes, worn or wrong emitters and poor distribution uniformity all cap what any schedule can achieve.', 'Irrigation', 'System evaluation', 'confirmed'),
	(454, 8, 81, 'Assess soil health across its biological, physical and chemical properties together -- active microbial communities mineralise nitrogen, build structure and compete with pathogens, which no chemical test alone will show.', 'Soil chemical management', 'Soil health assessment', 'confirmed'),
	(455, 45, 81, 'Give the thinning crew a sizing disc and re-calibrate them on it each morning: demonstrate the target diameters before they start, since accuracy drifts through the day.', 'Thinning', 'Sizing disc calibration', 'confirmed'),
	(456, 48, 81, 'Decide whether hedging is part of the system before the block is designed, since it suits a canopy of short stiff limbs carrying a few fruiting sites. Hedge at the right leaf stage to set terminal buds, push buds behind the cuts and open the canopy.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Mechanical hedging', 'confirmed'),
	(457, 8, 81, 'Spray weeds while they are small. Size at application governs control for most herbicides, and waiting costs more than the extra pass would have.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Weed size at spraying', 'confirmed'),
	(458, 36, 81, 'Set the post-bloom nutrition priorities deliberately; this is the window where the season''s fruit quality is largely determined.', 'Nutrient management', 'Post-bloom nutrition', 'confirmed'),
	(460, 8, 81, 'Monitor orchard heat stress in real time during summer and manage it for fruit colour and sunburn, rather than reacting after damage shows.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Heat stress monitoring', 'confirmed'),
	(461, 6, 81, 'Quantify the drainage risk of each area of the block and score it, then match the level of mitigation to the score rather than treating the whole orchard the same.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Drainage risk scoring', 'confirmed'),
	(462, 8, 81, 'Treat poor drainage as a whole-season problem: it produces shallow root systems that cannot cope with summer heat, shortens and weakens bloom, changes how fruitlets respond to chemical thinning, and blocks nutrient uptake at the times it matters most.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Poor drainage consequences', 'confirmed'),
	(463, 8, 81, 'Check what the existing drainage system was actually designed to do and maintain it; degraded soil structure and sloping ground both cause drainage problems that the original design never covered.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Drainage system maintenance', 'confirmed'),
	(465, 10, 81, 'Read weak, patchy and drawn-out bud break as the signature of insufficient winter chill, and trace the consequences forward through the season rather than treating it as a spring problem.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Low chill symptoms', 'confirmed'),
	(466, 10, 81, 'Where chill has been short, choose a bud break product deliberately and get the target timing and application technique right; that is what decides the result more than the choice of product.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Bud break products', 'confirmed'),
	(467, 8, 81, 'Weigh netting as a climate tool, not just a hail barrier: lower light intensity cuts fruit surface temperature and sunburn, slows soil moisture depletion, keeps leaves photosynthesising closer to optimum by reducing midday stomatal closure, and cuts wind rub.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Netting benefits', 'confirmed'),
	(468, 8, 81, 'Plan around what netting costs as well: reduced light can delay colouring -- roll out reflective mulch two to three weeks before harvest to offset it.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Netting trade-offs', 'confirmed'),
	(469, 30, 81, 'Netting reduces bee activity. Introduce hives at about 20% full bloom and leave a 1 to 1.5 m gap between the top of the canopy and the net so bees have flight paths.', 'Pollination', 'Bees under netting', 'confirmed'),
	(470, 8, 81, 'Treat excess vigour as a quality problem, not just a management one: it brings shading, poor colour, low brix, low pressure and a higher risk of internal disorders. Aim for enough vigour to hold a good leaf-to-fruit ratio and no more.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Vigour for quality', 'confirmed'),
	(471, 8, 81, 'Where vigour needs pulling back, the levers are root pruning, late pruning, girdling, summer pruning, mechanical trimming in spring or summer, prohexadione-calcium, ethephon and controlled water deficit.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Reducing vigour', 'confirmed'),
	(472, 6, 81, 'Match the variety to the microclimate before planting. Hot districts cost early-variety quality and the climate is warming; in many parts of Australia netting and secure reticulated water are prerequisites rather than options.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Microclimate matching', 'confirmed'),
	(473, 8, 81, 'Run climate and disease forecasting and work from the pest life cycles and infection risks, so sprays land inside the window rather than on a calendar.', 'IPDM', 'Disease forecasting', 'confirmed'),
	(474, 8, 81, 'Keep enough spray capacity to cover the block accurately inside tight windows, with effective coverage and the right droplet size; quality depends on it as much as on the chemistry chosen.', 'IPDM', 'Spray capacity for quality', 'confirmed'),
	(476, 45, 81, 'Apply metamitron post-fruit set, when central fruitlets are 8 to 16 mm, either as a single application at 1.1 to 2.2 kg/ha or as two applications of 1.1 kg/ha, in 1000 to 2000 L/ha of water and no more than 2.2 kg/ha for the season.', 'Thinning', 'Metamitron', 'confirmed'),
	(477, 45, 81, 'Metamitron thins by temporarily inhibiting photosynthesis -- it blocks electron transport in photosystem II -- so the weakest fruit in each cluster, those growing slowest and carrying no seed, drop first.', 'Thinning', 'How metamitron thins', 'confirmed'),
	(478, 9, NULL, 'Plan block replacement on a shorter cycle than a traditional orchard. Dwarfing rootstocks at high planting density bring a block to the end of its economic life sooner, so replanting comes round more often.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Replant interval', 'confirmed'),
	(479, 9, NULL, 'Assume the site is replant-affected and budget for it. Apple replant disease has been recorded on 25 to 70 per cent of replanted sites, and on affected soils it has been estimated to halve the profitability of the orchard that follows.', 'Disease management', 'Replant risk', 'confirmed'),
	(459, 53, 81, 'Prepare for long-term storage before picking starts: rot pressure at harvest, and the handling that follows, decide how the fruit holds in store.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Harvest preparation for storage', 'confirmed'),
	(480, 9, NULL, 'Diagnose the individual site before choosing a treatment. The organisms associated with replant disease differ from site to site, so a programme that worked on a neighbouring block may not transfer to this one.', 'Disease management', 'Replant site diagnosis', 'confirmed'),
	(481, 9, NULL, 'Choose a replant-tolerant rootstock. Over four years on a site cropped to apple for more than eighty years, rootstock was the single most important factor in overcoming replant disease, outweighing every preplant soil treatment tested.', 'Disease management', 'Rootstock for the replant', 'confirmed'),
	(482, 9, NULL, 'Set the new rows in the old grass lanes rather than on the old tree lines. Trees replanted off the previous row grew better, and the gain comes from separating new roots from old root-zone soil rather than from diluting it.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Row repositioning', 'confirmed'),
	(483, 9, NULL, 'Grow Tagetes on the site before replanting where nematodes are implicated. Apple planted after Tagetes gained significantly more shoot base diameter than apple planted after grass, through a shift in the soil nematode community.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Pre-crop before replanting', 'confirmed'),
	(484, 9, NULL, 'Build soil microbial and faunal diversity rather than relying on disinfection. Fumigants are being withdrawn in many countries, steam disinfection costs three to four times as much as chemical treatment, and preplant fumigation and compost gave little benefit over four years in trial.', 'Disease management', 'Soil biology over disinfection', 'confirmed'),
	(485, 8, NULL, 'Treat sunburn as a primary economic loss rather than a cosmetic one. Reported losses of apple fruit to sunburn range from 10 per cent to as much as 50 per cent of the crop.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Sunburn losses', 'confirmed'),
	(486, 8, NULL, 'Rank sunburn mitigation by measured efficacy. Across two seasons netting gave the greatest reduction in sunburn damage, followed by sunscreen sprays and then particle films, and none of the treatments affected yield or fruit quality.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Sunburn mitigation ranking', 'confirmed'),
	(487, 8, NULL, 'Expect sunburn mitigation to protect fruit without necessarily paying for itself. Differences between mitigation treatments did not translate into higher net returns to the grower.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Sunburn mitigation economics', 'confirmed'),
	(488, 8, NULL, 'Choose net specification as a microclimate decision, not only a hail decision. Nets cut incoming solar radiation and wind speed and alter the orchard heat balance, which can improve leaf gas exchange and water relations where radiation and temperature are extreme, provided the shading does not cost yield or quality.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Net choice as microclimate', 'confirmed'),
	(489, 48, NULL, 'Use evaporative cooling against summer heat and sunburn, separately from its use before bloom for frost protection and bloom delay.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Evaporative cooling for heat', 'confirmed'),
	(490, 30, NULL, 'Manage pollination as an active constraint on yield rather than assuming it is adequate. A meta-analysis of field studies across 532 orchard replicates found strong evidence of pollen limitation for both fruit set and seed set.', 'Pollination', 'Pollen limitation', 'confirmed'),
	(491, 34, NULL, 'Do not treat hive numbers as the whole of pollination. Honeybees made 71.9 per cent of flower visits but contributed less than their share of visitation once efficiency was accounted for, while wild bee visitation had a small but clear positive effect on fruit weight and seed set.', 'Pollination', 'Wild bee contribution', 'confirmed'),
	(492, 30, NULL, 'Do not plan on native bees substituting for managed hives in most Australian districts. Stingless bees only provide pollination service above 22 degrees Celsius, their visits fall away with distance from native forest, and their subtropical distribution excludes most apple-producing regions.', 'Pollination', 'Native bee service in Australia', 'confirmed'),
	(493, 30, NULL, 'Set pollination inputs per variety. Pollinator dependence differs between varieties, and pollinator guilds differ in how effectively they pollinate apple flowers.', 'Pollination', 'Variety pollination demand', 'confirmed'),
	(495, 6, 81, 'Design the block around a tall, narrow canopy carried as a single-row tree wall on size-controlling rootstock. The shape complements the conical form the tree takes anyway, holds canopy position consistent down the row, and leaves less structure to build and cut than a canopy trained wide.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Canopy architecture', 'confirmed'),
	(496, 6, 81, 'Specify nursery stock carrying several small lateral branches. Feathered trees start with the branching the system needs, rather than waiting for pruning to build it.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Feathered nursery stock', 'confirmed'),
	(497, 6, 81, 'Bud-graft while the bark is slipping and can be pulled back easily. Take the bud from this season''s growth with part of the leaf stalk left on as a handle, set it into a T cut in the rootstock, and wrap above and below without covering the bud itself. Cut the rootstock off just above the bud the following spring to force a single stem.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Bud grafting', 'confirmed'),
	(498, 6, 81, 'Plan the block around the wait it actually carries: a tree budded one season is cut back and grown on the next, planted the year after that, and returns a first crop about three years from planting, with full crops at about five to six.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Establishment timeline', 'confirmed'),
	(499, 6, 81, 'Weigh an interstem against a single dwarfing rootstock on measured performance rather than on the label. Interstem trees matched M.26 for size, cumulative yield and yield efficiency after eight years; M.9/MM.106 and M.9/MM.111 grew larger and cropped more heavily than MAC.9, but M.9/MM.106 survived less well than M.9, M.26 or M.9/MM.111.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Interstem trees', 'confirmed'),
	(500, 45, 81, 'Where populations are moderate to high, begin codling moth applications at 250 degree-days after biofix. The trap catch sets the biofix; the accumulation, not the catch, sets the spray.', 'IPDM', 'Codling moth spray threshold', 'confirmed'),
	(501, 45, 81, 'Alternate mode-of-action groups between generations and hold any one group to a single generation a year. Do not lean on the same product for both codling moth and aphids. Where mites are a chronic problem, adding 1% oil by volume limits the flare-up that follows an application.', 'IPDM', 'Codling moth resistance management', 'confirmed'),
	(502, 48, 81, 'Do not prune in late summer before harvest. The cuts expose fruit that grew under cover, and sunburn follows on the newly exposed side.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Late-summer pruning and sunburn', 'confirmed'),
	(503, 48, 81, 'Apply kaolin clay late in the season to shade exposed fruit. It is a white, non-toxic clay mineral, and it works by reflecting light off the fruit surface rather than by cooling it.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Kaolin clay', 'confirmed'),
	(505, 6, 81, 'Start planning and site preparation two to three years before planting, and place the tree order three years ahead where the nursery allows it. Fire-blight-resistant rootstocks exist but supply is limited, so the order is where that choice is actually made.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Pre-plant lead time', 'confirmed'),
	(506, 6, 81, 'For a tall-spindle fruiting wall, set trees three to four feet apart in the row with eleven to twelve feet between rows, giving a canopy about three feet wide -- narrow enough that light reaches the whole wall and a platform can reach across it without ladders.', 'Modify Landscape', 'High-density spacing', 'confirmed'),
	(507, 6, 81, 'Plant as early as the ground can be worked in spring, so root growth starts before the canopy makes demands on it.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Spring planting timing', 'confirmed'),
	(508, 6, 81, 'Remove all feathers below 24 inches at planting with a flush cut just outside the collar, and take off any feather larger than two-thirds the diameter of the leader, leaving a short stub for limb renewal. The stub does not need to be cut at a bevel.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Feather removal at planting', 'confirmed'),
	(509, 6, 81, 'Avoid heading cuts. Removing the apex of the leader or of a lateral pushes vegetative growth and cuts fruit-bud initiation, which is the opposite of what an establishing tree needs.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Heading cuts', 'confirmed'),
	(510, 6, 81, 'Install a wire trellis able to support the tree to nine feet, and tie the tree to it immediately -- early growth is better on a supported tree. Use a permanent tie above the first tier of feathers, left as a four-inch loop so the trunk can thicken into it.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Trellis and tying', 'confirmed'),
	(511, 6, 81, 'Once the ground has settled and the trees have started to grow, apply two to four ounces of calcium nitrate per tree in two split applications a month apart. Keep both fertiliser and herbicide off the young trunk bark.', 'Fertilization', 'Establishment nitrogen', 'confirmed'),
	(512, 6, 81, 'Maintain a four-foot herbicide strip down the tree row so the young trees are not competing for water and nutrients, and install drip irrigation at the same time. Seed the row middle to a fescue and annual rye mix at a high rate, thick enough to crowd out the broadleaf weeds that host virus vectors.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Establishment floor', 'confirmed'),
	(513, 7, 81, 'At bud swell, rub off the three shoots below the leader so nothing competes with it. Vigorous cultivars need a second pass once those shoots are three to four inches long.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Leader dominance', 'confirmed'),
	(514, 7, 81, 'Remove flower buds from young trees before they open, and do it before opening rather than after: an open flower on a young tree is a fire blight entry point, and the disease travels down a row from it. This matters most on weak-growing trees, which is a common problem in Honeycrisp.', 'Disease management', 'Flower bud removal on young trees', 'confirmed'),
	(515, 7, 81, 'Bend excessively long or upright laterals down towards horizontal rather than heading them back.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Lateral angle', 'confirmed'),
	(516, 7, 81, 'On stronger-growing trees in the second leaf, leave about fifteen fruit on the lower branches to check the vigour of those limbs, but thin the fruit above the second wire so the leader keeps growing. The leader should reach the top wire by the end of the second leaf.', 'Thinning', 'Second-leaf crop', 'confirmed'),
	(517, 7, 81, 'Dormant-prune high-density trees in February or March before the third leaf, taking out the overly vigorous limbs and leaving a short stub.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Dormant pruning timing', 'confirmed'),
	(518, 6, 81, 'Bud-graft in late summer, while both the rootstock and the scion wood are still growing and the bark will lift.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Budding season', 'confirmed'),
	(519, 10, 81, 'Mating disruption is the preferred tool, but it is a block-scale one: it is not recommended below three to five acres, works best in large, uniform, roughly square blocks, and needs supplementing with insecticide in the first years or where populations are moderate to high.', 'IPDM', 'Mating disruption block size', 'confirmed'),
	(521, 10, 81, 'Remove the reservoirs before the season starts: abandoned apple, pear and walnut trees nearby, and the props, picking bins and fruit piles left in the orchard. Pruning for coverage and calibrating the sprayer do as much for control as the material chosen.', 'IPDM', 'Codling moth reservoirs', 'confirmed'),
	(522, 45, 81, 'Where the block is organic, the accepted tools are cultural control with mating disruption plus approved oils, codling moth granulovirus, spinosad and kaolin clay. Hand-thinning infested fruit within each generation, before the larvae leave the fruit, and clearing the drops works at small scale.', 'IPDM', 'Codling moth organic options', 'confirmed'),
	(523, 46, 81, 'Treat rising trap catches under a working programme as a resistance signal, not a dose problem: combine mating disruption with the insecticides rather than increasing the rate.', 'IPDM', 'Codling moth resistance signal', 'confirmed'),
	(524, 19, 81, 'Aim the whole scab programme at the primary infections in spring. Extensive primary infection costs fruit set and makes control harder for the rest of the season, whereas secondary infection stays minor if the primary round was stopped. Exact timing and full coverage are the two levers.', 'Disease management', 'Scab primary infection', 'confirmed'),
	(525, 19, 81, 'Predict infection from the average temperature of the wet period and the hours of leaf wetness, using the Mills and La Plante table. Total intermittent wet periods until at least six hours of continuous dryness; if drying is slow and humidity stays high, extend that dry period by a three-to-four-hour margin.', 'Disease management', 'Scab wetting hours', 'confirmed'),
	(526, 19, 81, 'Where wetness periods are not being monitored, apply protective fungicides at regular intervals from green tip, and monitor wetness through spring to confirm the trees stayed covered.', 'Disease management', 'Protectant from green tip', 'confirmed'),
	(578, 15, 81, 'At half-inch green in the minimal organic programme, sulfur carries the scab protection.', 'Disease management', 'Organic programme: half-inch green', 'confirmed'),
	(520, 64, 81, 'Deploy pheromone as hand-applied dispensers or as an aerosol puffer, and hang the product shortly before biofix using historical biofix dates to time it. Sprayable pheromones are not recommended in pome fruit: the residual is very short.', 'IPDM', 'Pheromone dispenser choice', 'confirmed'),
	(537, 6, 81, 'Have the irrigation system running within three to five days of planting, and sooner if you can. The interval stretches to about a week only where 25 to 30 mm of rain falls shortly after planting.', 'Irrigation', 'Irrigation at planting', 'confirmed'),
	(538, 7, 81, 'On light soils, irrigating twice a week is the most common mistake made with drippers. A young tree that dries out even briefly takes a growth check that is not easily undone, and may not resume until the following spring.', 'Irrigation', 'Dripper frequency', 'confirmed'),
	(539, 7, 81, 'Match the emitter to the soil. Drippers work best on heavier soils, where the water-holding capacity spreads the wetted zone outwards and gives the developing canopy a wider root volume to draw on.', 'Irrigation', 'Emitter choice against soil type', 'confirmed'),
	(540, 45, 81, 'Once shoot growth has started, keep the effective root zone supplied and never let it dry out. Water is the first lever on canopy growth, ahead of nutrition, training and crop load.', 'Irrigation', 'Root zone through shoot growth', 'confirmed'),
	(541, 46, 81, 'Schedule against an allowable deficit rather than a calendar. Where the root zone holds about 30 mm of available water, irrigate before the accumulated deficit passes 30 mm and do not run below it.', 'Irrigation', 'Allowable soil water deficit', 'confirmed'),
	(542, 46, 81, 'Use the stress coefficient in the water budget to price a decision before making it. A coefficient of one meets maximum water use; entering less than one quantifies what deficit irrigation or a post-harvest cut-off would actually save.', 'Irrigation', 'Water budget stress coefficient', 'confirmed'),
	(543, 8, 81, 'Budget five to six megalitres per hectare for a mid-season cultivar such as Gala, and settle the season''s strategy against that figure before the demand arrives.', 'Irrigation', 'Seasonal water budget', 'confirmed'),
	(544, 8, 81, 'Run soil moisture to a seasonal shape rather than a fixed setpoint: raise it between budburst and flowering, ease it back through flowering to harvest while holding stress to a minimum, let it decline gradually to leaf fall, and hold off until the next budburst.', 'Irrigation', 'Seasonal moisture strategy', 'confirmed'),
	(545, 45, 81, 'Treat regulated deficit irrigation as a last resort in apples, not a routine tool: it costs fruit size. Apply it only where irrigation is genuinely short and the crop has been thinned hard, and pair an early-season deficit with heavier and earlier thinning than normal so the remaining fruit still size.', 'Irrigation', 'Regulated deficit irrigation', 'confirmed'),
	(546, 46, 81, 'Where trees are moving into drought stress, cut the crop to about 60 per cent of a normal load. Marketable fruit size can be held that way when the water to size a full crop is not there.', 'Thinning', 'Crop load under drought stress', 'confirmed'),
	(549, 36, 81, 'Make the first prohexadione-calcium application when two to five leaves are fully developed on each shoot, which in most seasons falls at or soon after petal fall.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Prohexadione-Ca timing', 'confirmed'),
	(550, 36, 81, 'Prohexadione-calcium is short-lived in the tree, with a biological half-life of ten to fourteen days. A petal-fall application has largely dissipated by the third and fourth sprays of a GA4+7 programme run at ten-day intervals, which is why it does not blunt them.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Prohexadione-Ca persistence', 'confirmed'),
	(551, 36, 81, 'Weigh russet control against size. Prohexadione-calcium at petal fall, four GA4+7 sprays from petal fall, and the two combined each lifted the share of fruit in the lowest russet class above the untreated control on Golden Delicious, and each also carried a lower mean fruit weight.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Russet control and fruit size', 'confirmed'),
	(579, 64, 81, 'At pink, apply Bacillus subtilis for fire blight only where the block has a history of the disease, and azadirachtin where European apple sawfly is present.', 'IPDM', 'Organic programme: pink', 'confirmed'),
	(547, 6, 81, 'Lay pollenizers out more intensively under hail net -- preferably one in every row at 20 to 30 m intervals -- because bees under net travel along the rows rather than across them.', 'Pollination', 'Pollenizer layout under netting', 'confirmed'),
	(553, 45, 81, 'Trees are most vulnerable to postbloom thinners at about 10 to 12 mm fruitlet size, once the reserves carried through bloom are spent. Fertilised flowers get harder to thin with every passing day, so a programme that leans on chemistry after 15 mm is working against the tree; early and aggressive beats late and repeated.', 'Thinning', 'When the window closes', 'confirmed'),
	(554, 36, 81, 'Carbaryl mimics auxin and can go on any time from 80 per cent petal fall through to 16 mm fruit size, which makes it the most forgiving window of the thinning materials.', 'Thinning', 'Carbaryl window', 'confirmed'),
	(555, 45, 81, 'Apply carbaryl combined with NAD or NAA at 8 to 12 mm fruit diameter for the best response, and expect over-thinning where daytime temperatures run above 80F.', 'Thinning', 'Carbaryl combinations', 'confirmed'),
	(556, 45, 81, 'Use one or two benzyladenine applications when king-bloom fruit are 5 to 10 mm. It works best combined with carbaryl or NAA, and needs temperatures above about 65F during the application and for several days after it.', 'Thinning', 'Benzyladenine window', 'confirmed'),
	(557, 36, 81, 'Apply naphthaleneacetamide from petal fall to 14 days past full bloom. Where the weather turns cool after bloom, hold off until the largest fruit are 2 to 3 mm and the forecast after spraying is above 50F, and preferably above 65F.', 'Thinning', 'NAD in cool weather', 'confirmed'),
	(558, 45, 81, 'The ACC thinner is labelled from king bloom onwards but works better sprayed at 15 to 20 mm fruitlet size, and a non-ionic surfactant may lift its efficacy.', 'Thinning', 'ACC thinner window', 'confirmed'),
	(559, 45, 81, 'Thinner efficacy falls away below 70F, but cool weather also masks a response that has already happened. Where a spray looks to have failed, wait for a few warm days and reassess set before adding another thinner, or the block gets thinned twice.', 'Thinning', 'Reading a slow response', 'confirmed'),
	(560, 45, 81, 'Ethephon can be tank-mixed with carbaryl, NAA or NAD to add thinning and lift return bloom, and is most effective 10 to 20 days after full bloom. High rates cut fruit size, and applied earlier than three weeks after bloom it can flatten the calyx end of Red Delicious.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Ethephon for thinning and return bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(561, 28, 81, 'Hand-thin blossoms at about the popcorn stage where bloom is heavy. It is the earliest point at which crop load can be taken down, and it works before the tree has spent reserves setting fruit it will later drop.', 'Thinning', 'Blossom thinning at popcorn', 'confirmed'),
	(562, 45, 81, 'Bring the crop to within 15 to 20 per cent of the final target within six to eight weeks of full bloom, and preferably inside six. Thinning that finishes later has already cost the following season''s return bloom.', 'Thinning', 'Crop load convergence', 'confirmed'),
	(563, 46, 81, 'The relationship between fruit number and fruit size runs at roughly two to one in the normal cropping range: to lift average size by ten per cent, take fruit numbers down by about twenty.', 'Thinning', 'Fruit number against fruit size', 'confirmed'),
	(564, 46, 81, 'Where fruit are being sized on a regular round, read a sharp drop in growth rate as the signal that the crop is beyond what the season will carry, and thin again so the remainder can size normally.', 'Thinning', 'Growth-rate check', 'confirmed'),
	(565, 8, 81, 'On mature canopies, describe crop load as fruit per square centimetre of branch cross-sectional area, taking branches or branch sections in the 2 to 5 cm diameter range.', 'Thinning', 'Branch cross-sectional area', 'confirmed'),
	(566, 8, 81, 'The fruit-per-trunk-cross-sectional-area target stops working once a block passes about 50,000 square centimetres of TCA per hectare. Past that point, set targets from the block''s own history in tonnes or tray-carton equivalents per hectare.', 'Thinning', 'When fruit per TCA stops working', 'confirmed'),
	(567, 46, 81, 'On varieties prone to biennial bearing, carry a higher crop with less risk by thinning some spurs out completely and leaving others as doubles, rather than reducing every spur to a single. What matters is the ratio of resting to fruiting sites, not the fruit per spur.', 'Thinning', 'Thinning to doubles', 'confirmed'),
	(568, 45, 81, 'Give hand thinners a reference size and have them strip anything below it, moving the reference up every few days as the crop grows. It converts a judgement call into a repeatable instruction across a crew.', 'Thinning', 'Reference-size thinning', 'confirmed'),
	(570, 21, 81, 'Run prohexadione-calcium as a two or three spray programme starting when terminal shoots are 3 to 5 cm long, repeating at three to five week intervals. Use the higher rate on large vigorous trees, and no more than three applications in a season.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Prohexadione-Ca programme', 'confirmed'),
	(571, 21, 81, 'Mix prohexadione-calcium into water at pH 7 or below, acidifying alkaline water, allow six hours for it to become rainfast, and keep three days clear of any spray containing calcium, ethylene or gibberellic acid.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Prohexadione-Ca spray conditions', 'confirmed'),
	(573, 8, 81, 'Confirm every practice and material against the certification programme that covers the block before it is used. Acceptability differs between states and certifiers, and a material listed as organic in one guide may not be permitted in another.', 'IPDM', 'Certifier confirmation', 'confirmed'),
	(574, 8, 81, 'Treat organic production as a whole-systems approach rather than a substitution of approved products for conventional ones. Site, cultivar, canopy and orchard floor carry more of the load in an organic block than the spray programme does.', 'IPDM', 'Whole-systems organic approach', 'confirmed'),
	(575, 6, 81, 'Soil fumigation for replant disease is not available in an organic block. Pre-plant treatments with seed meals from the mustard family have been effective in its place, so the replant decision moves to site preparation.', 'Disease management', 'Replant without fumigation', 'confirmed'),
	(576, 8, 81, 'Antibiotics have not been available to certified organic apple and pear growers since October 2014, and streptomycin is disallowed. That single change is what makes bloom-period fire blight the hardest problem in an organic block with susceptible cultivars and a history of the disease.', 'Disease management', 'No antibiotics for fire blight', 'confirmed'),
	(577, 10, 81, 'Dormant to silver tip in the minimal organic programme: copper against fire blight, and horticultural oil against European red mite.', 'IPDM', 'Organic programme: dormant to silver tip', 'confirmed'),
	(569, 61, 81, 'In late spring, take out or stub back the annual shoots that run past 25 to 30 cm or arise from the top of a branch. Left in place they shade the fruiting wood and pull the tree towards a biennial pattern.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Vigorous shoot removal', 'confirmed');
INSERT INTO "public"."operation" VALUES
	(580, 34, 81, 'At bloom, run Bacillus subtilis against fire blight on an interval set by the infection model rather than the calendar: depending on what the model shows, that can mean reapplying every two to seven days.', 'Disease management', 'Organic programme: bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(581, 36, 81, 'At petal fall, sulfur again for scab -- combined with oil where thinning is also wanted -- Bacillus thuringiensis for lepidopteran larvae, and kaolin clay for plum curculio only where there is pressure.', 'IPDM', 'Organic programme: petal fall', 'confirmed'),
	(582, 45, 81, 'Seven to ten days after petal fall: Bacillus amyloliquefaciens for sooty blotch and flyspeck, tank-mixed with a low-rate copper for fire blight only under fast-drying conditions; sulfur rather than copper for preventive scab cover; kaolin clay as the general insecticide; and codling moth granulosis virus timed from trap captures and accumulated degree days.', 'IPDM', 'Organic programme: fruit set', 'confirmed'),
	(583, 46, 81, 'Through summer: sulfur only where scab lesions are actually present, kaolin clay as the general insecticide, and horticultural oil as a miticide with care -- summer oil is phytotoxic, particularly over sulfur residue. Bacillus thuringiensis covers the leafrollers but not codling moth; granulosis virus, spinosad, azadirachtin and pyrethrum are timed for codling moth and apple maggot from traps and degree-day models.', 'IPDM', 'Organic programme: summer', 'confirmed'),
	(584, 19, 81, 'Elemental sulfur has to be on the tree before infection, and rain strips it off. Coverage needs renewing far more often than a conventional fungicide with real rain-fastness, which is what sets the spray interval in an organic block.', 'Disease management', 'Sulfur is protectant only', 'confirmed'),
	(585, 45, 81, 'Sulfur does not control rust diseases on apple, and in the southern part of a growing region it is also weak on flyspeck, bitter rot, black rot and white rot through mid-summer. Cover those with something else rather than raising the sulfur rate.', 'Disease management', 'What sulfur will not do', 'confirmed'),
	(586, 19, 81, 'Liquid lime sulfur is more effective than elemental sulfur and is used to burn out scab lesions once they appear, but it has no activity during the incubation period between about 70 hours after infection and the appearance of the lesion. It is extremely caustic, an eye and respiratory irritant, and equipment needs thorough cleaning after use.', 'Disease management', 'Liquid lime sulfur', 'confirmed'),
	(587, 45, 81, 'Keep the number of sulfur and lime sulfur sprays to the minimum the season needs. Both suppress photosynthesis, and the cost of that shows up as yield.', 'Disease management', 'Sulfur and yield', 'confirmed'),
	(588, 19, 81, 'Leave seven to ten days between a horticultural oil spray and any sulfur or lime sulfur. Applied inside that window they are phytotoxic.', 'Disease management', 'Sulfur and oil interval', 'confirmed'),
	(589, 15, 81, 'A full-orchard copper spray between silver tip and quarter-inch green cuts overwintering fire blight inoculum and holds apple scab for about a week. That window is where copper earns its place in the programme.', 'Disease management', 'Prebloom copper window', 'confirmed'),
	(590, 20, 81, 'Copper applied between half-inch green and bloom usually russets the fruit, and between bloom and roughly early July it can blacken the lenticels. Both windows are injury, not efficacy, decisions.', 'Disease management', 'Copper injury windows', 'confirmed'),
	(591, 46, 81, 'Copper applied later in July gives excellent sooty blotch and flyspeck control on red cultivars, but can discolour yellow ones severely. Newer low-rate formulations are labelled for summer use against several diseases; trials have controlled rust, rots, Brooks spot and sooty blotch with them, at the cost of increased russet.', 'Disease management', 'Summer copper against cultivar', 'confirmed'),
	(592, 34, 81, 'Bacillus subtilis is labelled for fire blight, apple scab and powdery mildew, and gives partial fire blight control applied to blossoms before, or within 24 hours after, an infection period. It is not reliable against fungal diseases under humid temperate conditions, so do not carry it as the scab programme.', 'Disease management', 'Bacillus subtilis on blossoms', 'confirmed'),
	(593, 45, 81, 'Bacillus amyloliquefaciens strain D747 is labelled for fire blight, sooty blotch, flyspeck and the summer rots, and suppresses apple scab. Trials show good efficacy against the shoot stage of fire blight when it is combined with a low-rate copper in summer sprays.', 'Disease management', 'Bacillus amyloliquefaciens', 'confirmed'),
	(594, 19, 81, 'Potassium bicarbonate has no post-infection activity, so it works only applied ahead of infection and repeated. European studies have managed apple scab with it where it was combined with, or rotated against, wettable sulfur.', 'Disease management', 'Potassium bicarbonate', 'confirmed'),
	(595, 45, 81, 'Hydrogen peroxide with peroxyacetic acid kills fungi and bacteria on contact and leaves no residual, so it has to land between spore deposition and infection to do anything. Field use on apples carries a russet risk.', 'Disease management', 'Contact sanitisers', 'confirmed'),
	(596, 6, 81, 'Rust in an organic block is a siting problem before it is a spray problem, because sulfur will not control it. Remove eastern red cedars and ornamental junipers within about 500 feet, or establish new blocks away from cedar habitat.', 'Disease management', 'Rust hosts and orchard siting', 'confirmed'),
	(597, 36, 81, 'Kaolin clay works as a particle film, so it has to be there before the insects are. Apply it dilute -- at least 100 gallons of water per acre -- and build a complete base layer ahead of target insect activity, starting at late bloom where plum curculio is the target, to stop migrating adults colonising the trees.', 'IPDM', 'Kaolin base layer', 'confirmed'),
	(598, 48, 81, 'Later kaolin applications suppress apple maggot and second-generation codling moth, but used past early July the residue at harvest becomes a problem in itself. Weigh the two before extending the programme.', 'IPDM', 'Kaolin late in the season', 'confirmed'),
	(599, 34, 81, 'In an organic block the crop-load decision is made at bloom, because there are no organic post-bloom thinners. The approved bloom thinners are lime sulfur, superior oil, stylet oil and fish oils, and the pollen tube growth model is what makes their timing repeatable.', 'Thinning', 'Organic thinning happens at bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(600, 36, 81, 'Azadirachtin, from neem seed, acts as a repellent, an antifeedant and an insect growth regulator rather than a contact killer, so it needs repeated applications at short intervals to hold a population. Do not treat foraging bees or open water with it.', 'IPDM', 'Azadirachtin', 'confirmed'),
	(601, 36, 81, 'The clarified neem oil label is broad but thinly substantiated. Use it against European apple sawfly rather than as a stand-alone scab fungicide, keep it out of tank mixes with oil-incompatible products, and account for the persistent film it leaves and the non-target hazard that comes with it.', 'IPDM', 'Clarified neem oil', 'confirmed'),
	(624, 10, 81, 'For green apple aphid, biological control with sprays of insecticidal soap, approved narrow-range oils or azadirachtin. Rosy apple aphid is harder: an organic block needs a dormant flowable emulsion applied at delayed dormant to silver tip to get any control at all.', 'IPDM', 'Organic options: aphids', 'confirmed'),
	(602, 45, 81, 'Bacillus thuringiensis reaches caterpillars that feed exposed on the surface, not those already inside the fruit, which is why it is a first-choice material for obliquebanded leafroller and no answer at all for codling moth. Start coverage earlier and respray at shorter intervals than a conventional insecticide would need, and it can go on up to harvest without a residue concern.', 'IPDM', 'Bacillus thuringiensis', 'confirmed'),
	(603, 46, 81, 'Use an organically accepted spinosad formulation -- not every formulation is -- and note that the bait formulation works at a low rate against fruit flies, which changes both the cost and the coverage needed.', 'IPDM', 'Spinosad', 'confirmed'),
	(604, 46, 81, 'Pyrethrins give short-term knockdown and nothing more, so they belong on top of a programme rather than under it. Apply them in the late evening, when the material degrades slowly and foraging has stopped.', 'IPDM', 'Pyrethrins', 'confirmed'),
	(605, 46, 81, 'The combined azadirachtin and pyrethrin products are for the insects that neither material handles alone. Do not apply them, or allow drift, onto blooming crops or flowering weeds while bees are foraging.', 'IPDM', 'Azadirachtin with pyrethrin', 'confirmed'),
	(606, 45, 81, 'Insecticidal soap kills soft-bodied arthropods on contact and does nothing it does not directly wet, so coverage is the whole decision.', 'IPDM', 'Insecticidal soap', 'confirmed'),
	(607, 10, 81, 'Horticultural oil works physically, by suffocation, which is why the main application belongs at dormant or prebloom when the target is exposed and the tree is not. Summer oil is a variety-dependent decision, not a routine one.', 'IPDM', 'Horticultural oil as a suffocant', 'confirmed'),
	(608, 45, 81, 'Cydia pomonella granulovirus has to be eaten by a newly hatched larva before it enters the fruit, so applications are timed to egg hatch and repeated through each generation rather than applied on a fixed interval.', 'IPDM', 'Codling moth granulovirus', 'confirmed'),
	(609, 46, 81, 'Rotate Chromobacterium subtsugae against granulovirus or Bacillus thuringiensis rather than repeating any one of them, and keep Burkholderia rinojensis metabolites to trial blocks until there is more evidence behind them.', 'IPDM', 'Rotating the newer biologicals', 'confirmed'),
	(610, 10, 81, 'Mating disruption carries much of the codling moth programme in an organic block. Size and shape the block for it, add border sprays where immigration pressure is high, and confirm with the certifier which pheromone materials are acceptable before ordering.', 'IPDM', 'Organic mating disruption', 'confirmed'),
	(611, 45, 81, 'Where flyspeck is the summer target, liquid lime sulfur gave good control in a Hudson Valley trial on either a long or a short interval, with the rate traded against the interval -- a higher rate less often, or a lower rate more often. The guide carries both combinations.', 'Disease management', 'Lime sulfur against flyspeck', 'confirmed'),
	(612, 34, 81, 'With streptomycin gone, organic fire blight management is an integrated programme rather than a spray: cultivar and rootstock choice, inoculum reduction, vigour control, blossom removal on young trees, infection forecasting with Maryblyt or Cougarblight through NEWA, and lime sulfur or biocontrol materials applied at bloom on what the model shows.', 'Disease management', 'Organic fire blight programme', 'confirmed'),
	(613, 19, 81, 'The Reynoutria sachalinensis extract is a plant defence promoter with translaminar but not systemic activity, so it has to be applied preventatively on a ten to fourteen day schedule. It is not effective against apple scab; it is labelled for fire blight suppression and for sooty blotch, flyspeck and powdery mildew, and the evidence behind those is still thin enough to keep it to trial blocks.', 'Disease management', 'Reynoutria extract', 'confirmed'),
	(614, 8, 81, 'No organically accepted material carries a disease programme on its own. Combine them with conservation of the biological control already in the block and with cultural reduction of inoculum -- the leaf litter, the cankers and the mummies -- or the spray interval becomes the only lever left.', 'Disease management', 'Materials, biology and sanitation together', 'confirmed'),
	(615, 34, 81, 'Yeast formulations are among the biological options being evaluated for fire blight control at bloom, and belong in the same trial-block category as the other biocontrol materials rather than in the standing programme.', 'Disease management', 'Yeast formulations for fire blight', 'confirmed'),
	(616, 8, 81, 'The New England guide''s apple spray table marks organically acceptable materials with a section symbol, so the full material list for any pest can be read straight off the standard table rather than from the organic pages alone.', 'IPDM', 'Organic options in the spray table', 'confirmed'),
	(617, 8, 81, 'The standard removes tools before it offers any. No herbicides and no clean cultivation; no synthetic nitrogen or phosphorus fertilisers and no muriate of potash; no fast-acting calcium such as slaked lime or quicklime, which is caustic to soil organisms; no genetically modified cultivars or rootstocks, and planting stock that is itself organically produced.', 'IPDM', 'Organic standard: what is ruled out', 'confirmed'),
	(618, 34, 81, 'Thinning sprays and growth regulators are not permitted under organic standards. Crop load therefore has to be settled at bloom and by hand, and the vigour that a growth regulator would have checked has to come out of rootstock choice, pruning and the orchard floor instead.', 'Thinning', 'No thinning sprays or growth regulators', 'confirmed'),
	(619, 6, 81, 'Choose cultivars and rootstocks adapted to the location and to the production system. In an organic block this is the largest single decision available, because most of the corrective tools that would compensate for a poor match later are not permitted.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Cultivar and rootstock for an organic block', 'confirmed'),
	(620, 8, 81, 'Keep permanent ground cover wherever it can be kept, with a species-rich flora and fauna. Competing vegetation is controlled, but in a way that protects the soil and maintains a lasting natural cover rather than clearing it.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Organic ground cover', 'confirmed'),
	(621, 8, 81, 'Prune to a naturally spreading shape and moderate growth, keeping the tree in physiological balance, and take out diseased, dead and broken limbs that harbour pests and diseases. Pruning does sanitation work in an organic block that a spray would otherwise do.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Pruning under organic standards', 'confirmed'),
	(622, 8, 81, 'Holdings are inspected at least once a year by an independent accredited body. Most certifiers require the whole farm to convert rather than a block, with the conversion period extendable to a maximum of five years, and a private body may set standards stricter than the statutory minimum but never lower.', 'IPDM', 'Certification and conversion', 'confirmed'),
	(623, 8, 81, 'The restrictions follow from five aims: food free of artificial and health-damaging pesticide residues, high and maintained soil fertility, natural cycles kept as closed as is feasible, biodiversity promoted and conserved, and no synthetic aids or genetically modified organisms. Decisions that are hard to settle from the rules are usually settled by reading them against these.', 'IPDM', 'What the organic standard is aiming at', 'confirmed'),
	(625, 6, 81, 'Woolly apple aphid is managed in an organic block by resistant varieties and rootstocks together with biological control, which puts the decision at planting: the Malling series MM.106 and MM.111 are resistant to it.', 'IPDM', 'Organic options: woolly apple aphid', 'confirmed'),
	(626, 10, 81, 'Biological control and approved oil sprays are the organic tools for San Jose scale, which is rarely a pest in an organic orchard anyway; lime treatments are acceptable against Italian pear scale. Check with the certifier which narrow-range oil products qualify.', 'IPDM', 'Organic options: scales', 'confirmed'),
	(627, 45, 81, 'Bacillus thuringiensis and an organically accepted spinosad formulation cover obliquebanded leafroller, omnivorous leafroller, orange tortrix and apple pandemis; approved narrow-range oil is also acceptable for fruittree leafroller, and kaolin clay for pandemis.', 'IPDM', 'Organic options: leafrollers', 'confirmed'),
	(628, 45, 81, 'Biological control and sprays of an organically accepted spinosad formulation are the primary means of controlling leafminers in a certified organic block, and low populations are usually held by natural enemies alone.', 'IPDM', 'Organic options: leafminers', 'confirmed'),
	(629, 34, 81, 'Cultural control with sprays of an organically accepted spinosad formulation is what is available for western flower thrips. Do not disc the cover crop at a time that would drive the thrips up into the trees.', 'IPDM', 'Organic options: thrips', 'confirmed'),
	(630, 46, 81, 'Mites in an organic block are held by biological and cultural control, approved narrow-range oils and resistant varieties. Natural enemies keep spider mites below damaging levels in many orchards, and European red mite only occasionally becomes troublesome, so the first decision is usually to leave them alone.', 'IPDM', 'Organic options: mites', 'confirmed'),
	(631, 45, 81, 'For lygus bug and the native stink bugs, cultural and biological control are the whole of the organic toolkit. For brown marmorated stink bug few organic guidelines exist at all, and a protectant such as kaolin clay reduces fruit damage only where coverage is thorough.', 'IPDM', 'Organic options: true bugs', 'confirmed'),
	(632, 8, 81, 'Five diseases carry organic apple production: apple scab, European canker, powdery mildew, apple replant disease, and brown rot with blossom wilt. Host resistance, rootstock and scion choice, planting location, biological control agents, soil amendments, cultural management, postharvest treatment and disease modelling are the levers available against them.', 'Disease management', 'Organic disease priorities', 'confirmed'),
	(633, 10, 81, 'Against European canker the organic programme is sanitation carried out properly: prune cankers out in winter, take the mummies off the tree and from the ground beneath it, remove infected fruit as soon as symptoms appear, and take waste fruit out of the orchard rather than leaving it in the rows.', 'Disease management', 'Canker sanitation', 'confirmed'),
	(634, 6, 81, 'Every wound is a canker infection site, so nursery handling shows up years later. Maiden trees -- single-stemmed, one to two metres -- reduce wounding on canker-susceptible varieties, while the Knip-boom technique wounds the tree at 70 to 100 cm to force its tier of side shoots. Lifting nursery trees, cold-storing them over winter and planting in spring has itself been found to increase canker once the trees are out in the orchard.', 'Disease management', 'Canker and nursery handling', 'confirmed'),
	(636, 8, 81, 'Apply compost to the topsoil of the tree rows once a year at 5 to 10 tonnes per hectare. Over twelve years that practice, with grass in the alleys, left the organic orchard topsoil with 32 per cent more soil organic carbon than the integrated orchard beside it, along with greater macroporosity and more stable aggregates -- the physical result of the carbon, not just the carbon.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Compost on the tree row', 'confirmed'),
	(637, 8, 81, 'Expect the trade-off to be real and to be large. Across orchards in Spain, Germany and Sweden, organic blocks averaged 48 per cent lower fruit production than IPM blocks -- a wider gap than organic management shows in other crops -- while carrying greater species richness of beneficial arthropods. Species richness and production were largely uncorrelated, so the biodiversity gain does not pay for itself through pest control alone.', 'IPDM', 'What organic management costs and returns', 'confirmed'),
	(638, 45, 81, 'In the European organic orchards studied, pest control ran on natural extracts such as neem and pyrethrum, on micro-organisms and viruses, and on mating disruption. The stated priority for improving those systems is developing pest control that does not cost pollination service.', 'IPDM', 'What the organic programme runs on', 'confirmed'),
	(639, 7, 81, 'An apple orchard is a net carbon source while it is establishing and becomes a net sink at about eight years old. Sequestration capability peaks at around eighteen years and declines after that, as the proportion of long-residence woody biomass rises but growth slows -- which puts carbon on the same clock as the block''s productive life.', 'Modify Landscape', 'When the block becomes a carbon sink', 'confirmed'),
	(640, 9, 81, 'Carbon sequestered in the standing trees is released when the block comes out, so the replanting decision carries a carbon cost as well as a yield one. The published orchard estimates come from a small number of sites in one season and are not transferable to a different climate without local measurement.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Carbon at the end of the block', 'confirmed'),
	(641, 6, 81, 'Site selection is the primary frost-control technique available; everything else is mitigation. Choose gently sloping land with good air drainage, so cold air moves down and away rather than pooling in the block, and keep early-blooming cultivars off south-facing positions where they will start sooner into a later frost.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Air drainage and frost', 'confirmed'),
	(642, 6, 81, 'Determine effective soil depth before committing to the site, and prefer well-drained soils. Depth and drainage set the root volume the block will ever have, and neither is adjustable once the trees are in.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Soil depth and drainage', 'confirmed'),
	(643, 6, 81, 'Till to about 20 cm before planting, and deep-rip 15 to 25 cm below the normal tillage depth to break compacted layers, working on dry soil where possible. On shallow or poorly drained ground, push the topsoil into ridges within the tree row to gain depth and shed water.', 'Perform Tillage', 'Pre-plant soil preparation', 'confirmed'),
	(644, 6, 81, 'Correct a non-optimum soil pH before the trees go in. Afterwards the amendment has to move down through an occupied root zone, which is slower and less complete than mixing it through beforehand.', 'Soil chemical management', 'Correct pH before planting', 'confirmed'),
	(645, 6, 81, 'Orient rows north to south where the site allows it, so both sides of the canopy take direct light through the day.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Row orientation', 'confirmed'),
	(646, 6, 81, 'On a replant site, take out as much of the old root system as the machinery will lift before anything else. Where fumigation follows, apply it in autumn into warm moist soil at roughly 10 to 18 C -- below about 42 F it works poorly -- and decide whether it is needed at all from a seedling bioassay rather than from the site history alone.', 'Disease management', 'Replant site preparation', 'confirmed'),
	(647, 6, 81, 'Where root-lesion nematodes are part of the replant problem, a marigold cover crop grown before planting suppresses them, which gives a non-fumigant option on sites where fumigation is unavailable or unwanted.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Marigold before replanting', 'confirmed'),
	(648, 6, 81, 'Start planning two to three years before planting. Rootstock orders go in two to three years ahead and scion orders eighteen months to two and a half years ahead; the exact cultivar and rootstock combination should be ordered preferably two years in advance, because it is the combination, not either half, that is scarce.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Establishment lead times', 'confirmed'),
	(649, 6, 81, 'Specify virus-tested rootstocks and scion material, certified true-to-type budwood, and clones tested locally rather than only in their region of origin. Source rootstocks from production areas indexed for apple stem-pitting, stem-grooving, mosaic and chlorotic leaf spot virus, and buy from a nursery that will stand behind trueness to name.', 'Disease management', 'Nursery stock health status', 'confirmed'),
	(650, 6, 81, 'Match the rootstock to the density rather than the reverse. MM.106 does not belong in intensive plantings above roughly 1,500 trees per hectare, except on poor but well-drained soil where growth is weak; MM.111 suits replant sites and not intensive plantings; B.118 is for spur strains, weak soil or replant situations.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Rootstock against planting density', 'confirmed'),
	(651, 6, 81, 'Any rootstock of M.26 size or smaller needs support for the life of the block, and M.9 and G.202 need it to be very good. Where G.30 carries Gala, the reported system is two wires, one at about 36 to 40 inches and a second at eight to nine feet.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Rootstocks that must be supported', 'confirmed'),
	(652, 6, 81, 'Read the rootstock against the soil''s water-holding capacity as well as its vigour: Bud 9 on light sandy soil needs good irrigation to perform, and will show the shortfall before a more vigorous stock would.', 'Irrigation', 'Rootstock and water supply', 'confirmed'),
	(653, 6, 81, 'Check the combination, not just the two halves. G.16 requires a scion of known virus-free status, and some scion and rootstock pairings are specifically not recommended -- Auvil Early Fuji on G.16 among them.', 'Disease management', 'Rootstock and scion compatibility', 'confirmed'),
	(654, 6, 81, 'Semi-vigorous rootstocks spread picking by seven to ten days against a fully dwarfing stock, which is a labour decision as much as a horticultural one, and they settle trees into early and regular cropping.', 'Harvest management', 'Rootstock and the harvest window', 'confirmed'),
	(655, 6, 81, 'Set the bud union 6 to 8 cm above the soil at planting so it sits 4 to 6 cm above it once the ground settles. The union is the dwarfing mechanism: buried, the scion roots and the rootstock stops controlling vigour.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Bud union height', 'confirmed'),
	(656, 6, 81, 'The union height is a system decision within that rule. Tall spindle plantings set unions 4 to 6 inches above the soil to hold vigour down; M.26 and MM.111 are set no more than 1 to 2 inches above the final soil line; where the union is used deliberately to check vigour it may be carried to 20 to 25 cm, and to only 10 to 15 cm for spur types. With a mechanical planter, set the trees an inch or two deep and adjust to depth by hand.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Union height by system and rootstock', 'confirmed'),
	(657, 6, 81, 'Plant in autumn where winters are not excessively cold, since roots keep growing at about 5 C and upwards. In humid areas plant late winter to early spring at the earliest weather that allows it, rather than waiting for settled conditions.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Planting season', 'confirmed'),
	(658, 6, 81, 'Put new trees under sprinklers for 24 hours before they go in, so they plant fully hydrated. Afterwards do not overwater: the root system is small and a saturated hole costs more than a dry one.', 'Irrigation', 'Tree handling around planting', 'confirmed'),
	(659, 6, 81, 'Fit tree guards at planting and keep spray, herbicide, machinery and animals off the young trunk. Bark damage in the first years is the injury most likely to cost the tree outright.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Tree guards at planting', 'confirmed'),
	(660, 6, 81, 'Where phosphorus is placed at planting, mix it well through the zone the new roots will occupy rather than banding it, so the emerging roots meet it without meeting a concentrated band.', 'Fertilization', 'Phosphorus in the planting zone', 'confirmed'),
	(661, 7, 81, 'Avoid heavy or late nitrogen on young trees. It pushes soft growth that does not harden before winter and delays the tree settling into cropping.', 'Fertilization', 'Nitrogen on young trees', 'confirmed'),
	(663, 6, 81, 'Choose varieties that broaden the harvest window rather than concentrating it, and do not plant a late variety where the season will not finish it -- GoldRush being the standing example.', 'Harvest management', 'Varieties and the picking programme', 'confirmed'),
	(664, 6, 81, 'Within a variety, plant the better-coloured strains for new blocks of Fuji, Royal Gala types and Cripps Pink. The strain decision is made once and carried for the life of the planting.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Strain selection within a variety', 'confirmed'),
	(665, 6, 81, 'Where vigour is more than a single leader will carry, adopt a multi-leader form along the row and split the vigour between two or more leaders. Note that bending and tying work rises steeply above about 3,000 trees per hectare, so the form and the density have to be decided together.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Multi-leader tree form', 'confirmed'),
	(667, 24, 81, 'A rotation is only a rotation if it has a limit. Cap any single-site group at two consecutive sprays before moving to a different mode of action, and tank-mix it with a protectant rather than running it alone.', 'Disease management', 'Consecutive-spray limits', 'confirmed'),
	(691, 6, 81, 'On light soils, postpone cutting back budded rootstocks until after the last spring frosts. The cut forces the bud into growth, and growth that starts before the frosts have finished is what gets caught.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Cutting back budded rootstocks', 'confirmed'),
	(668, 45, 81, 'The published limits differ by class and are worth holding to individually: at most two consecutive applications of a Group 3 sterol inhibitor or a Group 7 before switching; at most two or three consecutive strobilurin sprays, then at least two applications of a non-strobilurin; and premix products of those groups capped at four applications a season with no more than two in sequence.', 'Disease management', 'Group limits by fungicide class', 'confirmed'),
	(670, 45, 81, 'Where DMI performance has slipped, do not fall back on a low-rate EBDC plus DMI combination. Tank-mix the single-site material with a full-rate protectant such as an EBDC or captan instead, so the protectant is carrying the load rather than topping it up.', 'Disease management', 'Where DMI resistance is suspected', 'confirmed'),
	(671, 15, 81, 'Use dodine with caution where scab pressure is heavy: it is where resistance has repeatedly been selected, and heavy pressure is where a slip shows up as a crop loss rather than a lesson.', 'Disease management', 'Dodine under pressure', 'confirmed'),
	(673, 45, 81, 'Rotate insecticide mode of action between pest generations, and use only one chemical class within a generation. Rotating within a generation exposes the same individuals to two materials; rotating between them exposes different ones to each.', 'IPDM', 'One mode of action per generation', 'confirmed'),
	(674, 36, 81, 'Check the mode-of-action group, not the product name. All neonicotinoids count as one material when rotating; diamide products do not rotate against each other across successive generations of the same species; and rotate away from IRAC Group 28 between consecutive generations rather than repeating it.', 'IPDM', 'Rotations that are not rotations', 'confirmed'),
	(675, 45, 81, 'Season limits follow the active ingredient across products. Abamectin applied at petal fall counts against the allowance for any premix containing it later in the season, and abamectin itself should be rotated with other materials and applied no more than twice a year.', 'IPDM', 'Season allowances that overlap', 'confirmed'),
	(676, 15, 81, 'Keep sulfur and oil two weeks apart in either direction, and do not apply sulfur within about ten days of oil or captan, or when temperatures are above 85 F. Near a sulfur application, hold oil below about 80 F.', 'IPDM', 'Sulfur, oil and captan intervals', 'confirmed'),
	(677, 24, 81, 'After an oil spray, wait at least five to seven days and until two new leaves have unfolded before applying a captan or sulfur spray at tight cluster to open cluster.', 'Disease management', 'Re-entry after an oil spray', 'confirmed'),
	(678, 10, 81, 'Run a jar test before any oil tank mix. If the materials separate, precipitate, form a gel, foam excessively or throw oil droplets, the mix is physically incompatible and belongs in the jar rather than the tank.', 'IPDM', 'Jar test before an oil tank mix', 'confirmed'),
	(679, 15, 81, 'Check the pH of the spray mix and hold it close to 7.0 when copper is in it, and read the labels for incompatibility before tank-mixing anything with copper. Copper is where water chemistry stops being a detail.', 'Disease management', 'Spray water chemistry with copper', 'confirmed'),
	(681, 45, 81, 'Where alternate-row-middle spraying is used, do not let the interval between half-sprays exceed about seven days from the second or third cover onward. Past that, half the canopy is running unprotected for longer than the residual lasts.', 'IPDM', 'Alternate-row-middle interval', 'confirmed'),
	(682, 45, 81, 'Set water volume against the target, not the sprayer. Twenty to fifty gallons per acre is often not adequate for leafroller sprays, which have to reach larvae inside rolled leaves, and abamectin needs at least forty gallons per acre to work.', 'IPDM', 'Water volume against the target', 'confirmed'),
	(683, 7, 81, 'Add ammonium sulfate where a prohexadione-calcium product is mixed in hard water; the calcium and magnesium in the water otherwise tie up the active ingredient before it reaches the tree.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Growth regulator in hard water', 'confirmed'),
	(684, 10, 81, 'Size sprayer capacity so the whole block can be covered within a couple of days at most. A spray programme timed to an infection model is only as good as the machine''s ability to finish inside the window the model gives.', 'IPDM', 'Sprayer capacity against the block', 'confirmed'),
	(685, 45, 81, 'Choose a floor management system that fits the site rather than defaulting to bare ground. Weeds are not the same thing as a managed ground cover, and the aim through the season is suppression in the early-summer window when competition actually costs the tree, not year-round eradication.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Suppress rather than eradicate', 'confirmed'),
	(686, 8, 81, 'Select ground-cover species for the inter-row and for the tree line against different criteria: the inter-row carries traffic and feeds beneficials, while the tree line is competing directly with the tree for water and nitrogen.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Inter-row and tree-line separately', 'confirmed'),
	(687, 50, 81, 'Residual herbicides need water to move into the weed seed zone: apply pendimethalin before weeds establish and count on rain or irrigation within about 21 days, and put rimsulfuron onto moist soil ahead of rain, which needs roughly half an inch within three weeks.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Activating a residual herbicide', 'confirmed'),
	(688, 7, 81, 'Withhold pronamide from blocks less than a year old where they were autumn-planted, or less than six months where spring-transplanted, and set the rate from soil texture and organic matter -- at or above about four per cent organic matter the rate changes.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Herbicide restrictions on young blocks', 'confirmed'),
	(689, 56, 81, 'Apply the postharvest urea spray at the first signs of leaf fall, and follow with a light ground spray to reach the fallen leaves the canopy spray missed. Where dilute sprays are applied by airblast the separate ground spray can be omitted, because the canopy spray already reaches the floor.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Postharvest urea timing', 'confirmed'),
	(690, 6, 81, 'Bud at least 10 cm above ground, with 15 cm the better standard, and take it to 25 to 35 cm for a vigorous scion on a dwarfing rootstock such as M.9 -- the extra height adds dwarfing where the scion would otherwise overrun the stock.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Budding height on the rootstock', 'confirmed'),
	(672, 8, 81, 'Apply a minimum sulfur programme each year even on a scab-resistant cultivar. The resistance is a gene that a shifting pathogen population can overcome, and an unsprayed resistant block is where that selection happens fastest.', 'Disease management', 'Scab-resistant cultivars still need cover', 'confirmed'),
	(692, 6, 81, 'Where a dwarf tree is wanted on a seedling root system, a dwarfing interstock such as M.26 gives the size control while the seedling root supplies the anchorage and soil tolerance.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Dwarfing interstock', 'confirmed'),
	(693, 6, 81, 'Match the tree type to the density: large-calliper feathered trees suit plantings around 2,000 trees per hectare, while bench-grafted trees suit densities above about 3,000, where the tree is expected to build its structure in place. Bench grafts also have a short lead time on a chosen rootstock, which matters when a planting decision is made late.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Feathered trees against bench grafts', 'confirmed'),
	(694, 46, 81, 'Read sunburn risk from orchard air temperature against a fruit-surface threshold of about 46 C: fruit come under risk at roughly 28 C air temperature in an unnetted block and about 33 C under netting, which is the difference the net makes expressed as a decision rule.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Air temperature as the sunburn trigger', 'confirmed'),
	(695, 45, 81, 'Take the tissue sample from recently matured leaves in the middle of a non-bearing shoot or a non-bearing spur, from mid-canopy. A leaf from a fruiting spur is reading against the fruit as well as the tree, which is a different question.', 'Nutrient management', 'Which leaf to sample', 'confirmed'),
	(696, 45, 81, 'A mid-season leaf test is usable, but it has to be interpreted against late-season reference standards rather than read as if it were one. The value of taking it early is that there is still a season left to act in.', 'Nutrient management', 'Reading a mid-season leaf test', 'confirmed'),
	(697, 21, 81, 'Balance the soil cations in the order calcium, then magnesium, then potassium. It is the ratio between them, not the absolute level of any one, that governs what the tree can take up.', 'Soil chemical management', 'Soil cation balance', 'confirmed'),
	(698, 10, 81, 'Zinc is applied as an annual foliar spray, and in young orchards on new ground it can go on dormant, directly onto bark and buds. Zinc sulfate is the exception to spray-when-you-can: do not apply it above about 85 F.', 'Nutrient management', 'Zinc as a dormant spray', 'confirmed'),
	(699, 45, 81, 'A single annual maintenance foliar boron spray holds tissue boron in the 0.5 to 1.0 mg/kg range. Timing is the argument: mid-season rather than pre-bloom, and autumn in preference to spring, with a postharvest spray an option on late-ripening cultivars though not widely adopted.', 'Nutrient management', 'Boron maintenance and timing', 'confirmed'),
	(701, 6, 81, 'Put phosphorus in while the soil of a new block is being prepared, when it can be mixed through the profile the roots will occupy. On a mature block already high in soil phosphorus, the timing of a P application is not critical.', 'Fertilization', 'Phosphorus at establishment', 'confirmed'),
	(702, 7, 81, 'Keep the establishment fertiliser programme until significant cropping begins -- usually about year three -- then revert to the normal producing-orchard programme. The switch is triggered by the crop, not by the calendar.', 'Fertilization', 'When the young-tree programme ends', 'confirmed'),
	(704, 10, 81, 'Pick one chill model and stay with it. Utah chill units suit temperate winters; where winters are warm enough that moderate temperatures partly negate accumulated chill, a dynamic-type model represents the season better than an hours count.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Choosing a chill model', 'confirmed'),
	(706, 10, 81, 'Record accumulated winter chill each year alongside the block''s budburst and flowering dates. After a few seasons that record, not a published requirement figure, is what predicts budburst timing and tells you whether this winter was short.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Chill against the block''s own record', 'confirmed'),
	(707, 10, 81, 'Decide whether to use a dormancy-breaking spray, and when, from the chill actually accumulated this season rather than from the calendar or last year''s programme. In a low-chill year it is the intervention that makes bud break even; in a normal one it is an unnecessary cost.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Timing a dormancy-breaking spray', 'confirmed'),
	(708, 10, 81, 'In low-chill subtropical production, trees are defoliated to prevent dormancy setting in at all, rather than chilled through it and then broken out. It is a different production model, not a variation on the temperate one.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Where chill never accumulates', 'confirmed'),
	(709, 15, 81, 'Evaporative cooling by sprinkling can hold bloom back where the frost risk is worth the water, working off the difference between wet-bulb and air temperature rather than off the air temperature alone.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Delaying bloom by sprinkling', 'confirmed'),
	(710, 34, 81, 'Each thinner has its own temperature optimum, which is why one programme cannot be carried between districts: NAA works at 20 to 25 C, carbaryl across 15 to 25 C, ethephon at 20 to 25 C, and ammonium thiosulphate is far more effective at 20 C than at 14 C.', 'Thinning', 'Temperature optima by thinner', 'confirmed'),
	(711, 34, 81, 'Carry rates across districts with care. Where conditions are warm, NAA has been used at about half the rate applied in cooler regions for the same effect -- the same label, a different orchard.', 'Thinning', 'Rate against district temperature', 'confirmed'),
	(714, 6, 81, 'Where pollinizers are planted in solid rows, alternate two rows of pollinizer with four of the main cultivar, and drop to every third row for cultivars that are less self-fruitful. Whatever the pattern, no tree should sit more than about 100 feet from a pollinizer.', 'Pollination', 'Pollinizer arrangement in the block', 'confirmed'),
	(717, 6, 81, 'The training system is chosen against the cultivar and the site vigour, not by preference: spindle, bi-axis, Y-trellis, upright fruiting offshoot and Tatura are all in commercial use. Bi-axis in particular needs vigour to fill two leaders, so avoid it on poor or low-vigour soils and on low-vigour rootstocks.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Training system by cultivar and vigour', 'confirmed'),
	(718, 21, 81, 'On a cultivar that runs to vigour, the levers are used together: minimise winter pruning, pull back nitrogen and water, and summer-prune or de-leaf before harvest. Winter pruning alone pushes the vigour it was meant to remove.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Vigour control on a vigorous cultivar', 'confirmed'),
	(719, 45, 81, 'Some cultivars respond to thinners far more strongly than the district average, and are thinned with great caution and at reduced rates. Establish where a new cultivar sits before running the standard programme on it.', 'Thinning', 'Cultivars that thin easily', 'confirmed'),
	(720, 34, 81, 'Tap 20 flower clusters on the sunny side of each of five tagged trees over a white container and count what falls. Treat at two to four dimpling bugs per 100 flower clusters, and let the previous season''s damage decide whether monitoring starts early or at the standard time.', 'IPDM', 'Apple dimpling bug threshold', 'confirmed'),
	(722, 27, 81, 'Thrips come up from the floor, so treat the floor as part of the control: replace broad-leaved ground cover with grasses, and time any thrips treatment to coincide with a herbicide knockdown rather than driving them into the trees ahead of it. If thrips persist after spraying, suspect western flower thrips.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Thrips and the orchard floor', 'confirmed'),
	(723, 15, 81, 'Mating disruption only works if it is in place before the flight starts. Codling moth dispensers go up no later than pink, and where oriental fruit moth is in the district, no later than the start of peach flowering.', 'IPDM', 'Mating disruption deadline', 'confirmed'),
	(724, 15, 81, 'Horticultural mineral oil is applied to European red mite eggs where they actually sit -- spurs, laterals and the main limbs -- and within the label timing, which runs up to about half green tip. After that the eggs are hatching and the target has moved.', 'IPDM', 'Oil against mite eggs', 'confirmed'),
	(725, 45, 81, 'Where an infestation is confined to known trees -- woolly aphid being the standing case -- spot-spray those trees rather than putting a cover spray across the block. Flag the infested trees and keep the written record, so the next round goes to the same places.', 'IPDM', 'Spot-spray rather than cover-spray', 'confirmed'),
	(726, 45, 81, 'At low to moderate fruit fly pressure, bait spraying replaces the cover spray: a measured spot of prepared bait onto the foliage of every second tree in every second row in a low-density planting, which puts a fraction of the material into the block. The guide carries the volume per spot and the pattern for higher densities.', 'IPDM', 'Bait spraying at low pressure', 'confirmed'),
	(727, 34, 81, 'Every insecticide considered for the bloom period carries a bee-impact rating, and the rating governs when it may be applied rather than whether it works. Read the rating before the timing decision, not after it.', 'IPDM', 'Bee-impact ratings at bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(728, 7, 81, 'In small blocks, poultry will work weevils out of the orchard floor at a maximum stocking of about 50 birds per hectare. Above that they damage the floor they were brought in to clean.', 'IPDM', 'Poultry against weevils', 'confirmed'),
	(729, 45, 81, 'Do not run cover sprays to a calendar. The spray goes on ahead of an infection event identified from the weather and the model, which in a dry stretch means fewer sprays than the calendar would have had, and in a wet one means more.', 'Disease management', 'Against calendar cover sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(730, 34, 81, 'Where antibiotics are used at bloom, the window is narrow: they go on within the 24 hours before the flowers are wetted. Kasugamycin gives forward control applied two to three days ahead of rain and only partial control within twelve hours after it.', 'Disease management', 'Fire blight antibiotic window', 'confirmed'),
	(731, 34, 81, 'A biological blossom treatment needs time to establish on the stigma before the infection event: apply to every tree row at least 12 to 24 hours ahead of rain, not into it.', 'Disease management', 'Biological fire blight materials ahead of rain', 'confirmed'),
	(732, 34, 81, 'Copper at bloom is a real option where antibiotics are unavailable. In replicated trials three applications of soluble copper cut fire blight strikes from 77 to 5.5 per 100 clusters in New York and from 71 to 17 in Pennsylvania, with fixed copper giving 77 to 27 and 71 to 8; soluble formulations are favoured in wetter districts.', 'Disease management', 'Copper at bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(733, 36, 81, 'Secondary bloom keeps the fire blight window open after the main one closes. Protect for about three weeks after petal fall, with follow-up applications every two to three days through a risk period, rotating mode of action across them.', 'Disease management', 'Secondary bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(735, 10, 81, 'Where mildew infection is light, cut the infected terminal buds out during dormant pruning. Examine designated trees and count the infected dormant buds while doing it -- that number is the season''s starting inoculum and sets how hard the programme has to run.', 'Disease management', 'Mildew bud removal and counting', 'confirmed'),
	(736, 45, 81, 'Powdery mildew cover sprays run at 10 to 14 day intervals through the susceptible period; the interval is set by how fast new growth is being produced, since it is unprotected shoot tissue the fungus takes.', 'Disease management', 'Mildew cover spray interval', 'confirmed'),
	(737, 7, 81, 'Prioritise powdery mildew control on young trees trained as central leader or palmette. They are producing the new growth the fungus needs, and the structure being built now is the one the block will carry.', 'Disease management', 'Mildew on young trees', 'confirmed'),
	(721, 64, 81, 'Spray thrips at six to eight per blossom, which is where they arrive after warm dry weather in the pink to full-bloom window. Deploy two sticky traps per block or five per hectare, hung in the lower third of the tree.', 'IPDM', 'Thrips threshold and trapping', 'confirmed'),
	(738, 56, 81, 'Choose the scab infection model from the autumn inoculum assessment: where inoculum is low, a model such as MacHardy''s is sufficient; where risk is high, run the Mills tables together with ascospore monitoring so the programme is timed on measured release rather than on weather alone.', 'Disease management', 'Choosing a scab model by inoculum', 'confirmed'),
	(739, 56, 81, 'Assess the block''s scab position in autumn by sequential monitoring -- ten shoots on each of ten trees, counting the leaves carrying lesions. It is a fraction of a research sample and enough to place the block in an inoculum category.', 'Disease management', 'Autumn scab assessment', 'confirmed'),
	(741, 6, 81, 'Where Phytophthora has been a problem, the decisions are made at planting: choose M9 or M26, plant shallowly with the graft union well clear of the soil, and do not rip to root-prune a vigorous rootstock. Avoid replanting into the spaces vacated by trees that died of it.', 'Disease management', 'Phytophthora at establishment', 'confirmed'),
	(742, 6, 81, 'Where native trees are being cleared for a new block, ringbark them and leave them standing at least six months before removal, and leave the ground under pasture for two or three years so the small roots rot. Both steps are aimed at the root pathogens that survive in undecomposed root material.', 'Disease management', 'Clearing native trees before planting', 'confirmed'),
	(754, 46, 81, 'Keep the canopy open enough that fruiting sites sit at 50 per cent or better of available light. Colour at harvest is largely decided by that number months earlier, and no picking decision recovers it.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Canopy light for colour', 'confirmed'),
	(756, 8, 81, 'The first crop-load decision is the genetics and the orchard system. Steep branch angles, dense shading canopies and standing vigour make a crop impossible to manage whatever is sprayed at it; flatter branch angles, light into the canopy and carbohydrate reserve make the same crop routine.', 'Thinning', 'Crop load starts with the system', 'confirmed'),
	(757, 10, 81, 'Prune to remove potential flowers and fruit before they form. Every fruit taken off at pruning is one that never draws on reserves, never needs a spray decision, and never has to be picked off by hand.', 'Thinning', 'Pruning as the first thinning', 'confirmed'),
	(758, 34, 81, 'Understand the scale before judging a spray: bringing a normal bloom to a commercial crop means removing well over 90 per cent of the potential fruitlets. A thinning result that looks drastic is usually the correct one.', 'Thinning', 'The scale of thinning', 'confirmed'),
	(760, 34, 81, 'Start ammonium thiosulphate blossom sprays at 40 to 50 per cent of blossom open and repeat at intervals through bloom; in a two or three treatment programme the follow-ups fall one to two days after full bloom.', 'Thinning', 'ATS blossom thinning window', 'confirmed'),
	(572, 67, 81, 'Do not harvest for eight weeks after a prohexadione-calcium application; plan the last spray of the programme against the picking date, not the other way round.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Prohexadione-Ca withholding period', 'confirmed'),
	(759, 10, 81, 'Where chemical thinning is unreliable, floral buds can be removed by hand in the dormant or early season -- artificial bud extinction -- to a set density such as five or three floral buds per square centimetre of branch cross-sectional area. It converts crop load from a spray outcome into a countable one, and repeated over seasons it supports a fruit-set prediction for the block.', 'Thinning', 'Artificial bud extinction', 'confirmed'),
	(749, 50, 81, 'Where AVG is used to hold fruit on the tree, apply it closer to 28 days before harvest than 21, and plan on it delaying harvest by one to two weeks. Applied late it does less, and the harvest plan has already been built around the delay.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'AVG timing before harvest', 'confirmed'),
	(755, 67, 81, 'Direct marginal fruit to processing rather than storing it. Recovering value from fruit of marginal storage viability happens by sending it somewhere it keeps its value, not by holding it and finding out.', 'Harvest management', 'Marginal fruit to processing', 'confirmed'),
	(761, 45, 81, 'Benzyladenine is a temperature decision before it is a rate decision: apply it post-bloom only where temperatures are expected to exceed about 18 C, and hold the application if the forecast will not deliver that during and after the spray.', 'Thinning', 'Benzyladenine and the forecast', 'confirmed'),
	(762, 45, 81, 'Do not use ethephon as a post-blossom thinner once fruit are at 20 to 30 mm, do not use it for return bloom on varieties prone to early-season drop, and avoid the higher rates generally -- the margin between thinning and stripping is narrow.', 'Thinning', 'Where ethephon does not belong', 'confirmed'),
	(763, 45, 81, 'Predict the thinning response from relative fruit growth rate rather than waiting for drop: a fruitlet growing at less than about half the rate of the fastest fruit on the tree is one that will abscise. That reading is available days before the fruit falls.', 'Thinning', 'Reading the thinning response early', 'confirmed'),
	(764, 45, 81, 'Target chemical thinner sprays to the upper tree. That is where the crop over-sets, where light is strongest, and where a whole-tree rate over-thins the bottom before it corrects the top.', 'Thinning', 'Aim the thinner at the top of the tree', 'confirmed'),
	(765, 45, 81, 'Track fruit size weekly on the same 20 fruit through the sizing period. The same fruit measured repeatedly shows the growth curve; different fruit each week shows the sample.', 'Thinning', 'Weekly fruit measurement', 'confirmed'),
	(766, 7, 81, 'Carry 5 to 7 fruit per square centimetre of trunk cross-sectional area on young trees from the second leaf. A thinning gauge held against the trunk converts that into a fruit number for the crew, and the notched disc does the same per branch.', 'Thinning', 'Crop load on young trees', 'confirmed'),
	(767, 38, 81, 'In the on year, NAA or ethephon promote floral initiation for the following season; in the off year, gibberellin formulations are used to inhibit it, so the two crops are pulled towards each other rather than reinforcing the alternation.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Bioregulators against alternate bearing', 'confirmed'),
	(768, 50, 81, 'Keep NAA stop-drop sprays to the lower end of the registered rate range, and off the block in hot weather. At high rate or high temperature the spray advances ripening as much as it holds the fruit; the guide carries the rate and temperature limits.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Stop-drop limits', 'confirmed'),
	(769, 50, 81, 'AVG is a planned application, not a rescue treatment for a block already dropping. In a hot season its timing relative to harvest has to be brought forward rather than applied later to compensate.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'AVG is not a rescue', 'confirmed'),
	(770, 7, 81, 'Prune a young tree as little as the structure allows. Every cut removes leaf area that would have built the tree and pushes a vegetative response in its place, which delays the cropping that finally slows the tree down.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Prune young trees as little as possible', 'confirmed'),
	(771, 21, 81, 'Take many small cuts in preference to a few large ones. The same volume removed in small pieces produces far less regrowth than the same volume removed in a few large wounds.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Many small cuts, not a few large ones', 'confirmed'),
	(772, 10, 81, 'On an overly vigorous tree, delay dormant pruning until bloom or past it. Pruning at that point takes stored reserves out with the wood and produces markedly less regrowth than the same cuts made in mid-winter.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Delaying pruning on a vigorous tree', 'confirmed'),
	(773, 21, 81, 'Heading cuts push laterals, which is a problem on a vigorous tree and the point on a spur-type that will not branch on its own. On spur-type Delicious they are the standard way of getting the framework built.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Heading cuts where laterals are needed', 'confirmed'),
	(774, 21, 81, 'Where a tree has gone spur-bound, remove about 30 per cent of the growing points on each spur complex and add heading cuts. The aim is renewal rather than reduction: too many weak spurs produce small fruit and nothing to replace them with.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Spur pruning a spur-bound tree', 'confirmed'),
	(776, 21, 81, 'Do not use heavy pruning to lift a weak block. Pruning redistributes what the tree has; where vigour is genuinely low the limiting factor -- water, nutrition, root health, crop load -- has to be found and corrected first, or the cuts simply reduce the tree further.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Pruning will not fix low vigour', 'confirmed'),
	(777, 8, 81, 'Hold tree height to no more than about twice the clear alley width. Past that the lower canopy is shaded by the row opposite for part of the day, and no amount of pruning inside the tree recovers it.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Tree height against alley width', 'confirmed'),
	(778, 7, 81, 'Aim to have tree height equal to the between-row spacing, and around 60 per cent light interception, by the end of the fourth growing season. A block that has not filled its space by then is carrying the cost of the planting without the yield it was designed for.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Filling the space by the fourth leaf', 'confirmed'),
	(779, 7, 81, 'Keep six to eight of the lightest branches on a young tree and reduce to about four by the fourth or fifth leaf, taking out the strongest rather than the weakest. Branch renewal is what keeps the tree productive; branch retention is what makes it into a problem.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Branch selection on a young tree', 'confirmed'),
	(780, 7, 81, 'Bend branches down by 10 to 15 degrees a year rather than pulling them to position in one pass, and do not take them beyond about 60 degrees from vertical. Train a branch into a pendant position once it is nearly the length it should be, not before.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Bending by degrees', 'confirmed'),
	(781, 34, 81, 'Root prune bearing trees at 70 to 100 cm from the trunk, on two sides, in roughly the upper 25 cm of soil, around full bloom. Distance, depth, one side or two, timing, rootstock and soil all move the response, so the specification is the treatment.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Root pruning specification', 'confirmed'),
	(782, 45, 81, 'Adjust crop load down on root-pruned trees. The treatment has cut the tree''s capacity to size fruit as well as its capacity to grow shoots, and a full crop on a root-pruned tree produces small fruit.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Crop load on root-pruned trees', 'confirmed'),
	(783, 34, 81, 'Trunk girdling at bloom or petal fall reduces shoot vigour by 70 to 80 per cent, which is why it is reserved for blocks where vigour is genuinely excessive. Where fruit set is satisfactory -- around 30 per cent -- delay the girdle to the natural drop period instead.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Trunk girdling for vigour', 'confirmed'),
	(784, 21, 81, 'For controlling annual extension growth, prefer prohexadione-calcium to ethephon. Both check shoot growth, but ethephon carries effects on fruit and on return bloom that prohexadione-calcium does not.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Prohexadione-Ca ahead of ethephon', 'confirmed'),
	(785, 7, 81, 'Hold a weed-free strip one to one and a half metres wide along the tree row from late dormancy through the growing season. On a young tree the competition in that strip is the difference between filling the space and not.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Weed-free strip on young trees', 'confirmed'),
	(786, 7, 81, 'Start watering young trees regularly once green tissue appears and maximum temperatures pass about 20 C. A young tree has neither the root volume nor the reserves to carry a check, and the check comes earlier than the irrigation schedule usually assumes.', 'Irrigation', 'Watering young trees by temperature', 'confirmed'),
	(787, 34, 81, 'Where a young tree''s growth is inadequate, remove the flowers while leaving the spur and vegetative bud leaves in place. The earlier it is done the stronger the growth response, and the leaves left behind are what funds it.', 'Thinning', 'Flower removal to force growth', 'confirmed'),
	(788, 6, 81, 'Where fewer than about five usable feathers remain after selection, take them all off and rebuild the framework from the leader. A tree carrying two or three surviving feathers grows lopsided around them.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Feathers below the useful number', 'confirmed'),
	(789, 6, 81, 'Follow planting with a light irrigation to settle the soil around the roots. It is a settling operation rather than a watering one, and a heavy application at this point does the opposite.', 'Irrigation', 'Light irrigation after planting', 'confirmed'),
	(791, 30, 81, 'A pollinizer has to start just ahead of the target variety and stay in flower across the first half to two-thirds of its bloom. Overlap at the end of bloom is worth little: the first flowers to open are the ones that set the crop.', 'Pollination', 'Bloom overlap the pollinizer must give', 'confirmed'),
	(796, 6, 81, 'Where in-row spacing is about 1.5 m or less, plant pollinizers as whole-tree sites rather than interplanting them between trees. At that spacing an interplanted tree is a gap in the fruiting wall rather than an addition to it.', 'Pollination', 'Whole-tree sites in close plantings', 'confirmed'),
	(798, 30, 81, 'Plan on a minimum of four or five strong colonies per hectare. Colony strength matters more than colony count: a weak hive forages a fraction of the area a strong one does.', 'Pollination', 'Colony strength and number', 'confirmed'),
	(799, 30, 81, 'Set hives in a sunny sheltered position, and towards the base of the slope on sloping ground -- bees work uphill more readily than down. Provide a regular clean water supply near the hive, or the foragers will find one off the block.', 'Pollination', 'Where to put the hives', 'confirmed'),
	(800, 30, 81, 'Bring hives in at 5 to 10 per cent of blossom open, so the colonies orient to the orchard rather than to whatever is flowering outside it, and take them out at the early stages of petal fall on two-year and older wood.', 'Pollination', 'When hives come in and go out', 'confirmed'),
	(801, 30, 81, 'Under hail net, leave the nets open over flowering wherever it is practical. Where they must stay closed, put the hives inside the netted area, distribute them well through the block, raise the density where clearance is limited, and mark each hive position with something distinctive standing above the foliage so the bees can navigate back to it.', 'Pollination', 'Hives under netting', 'confirmed'),
	(802, 34, 81, 'Fruit removed in the first three or four weeks after bloom is what decides next season''s flowering. Thinning after that window corrects this year''s crop and does very little for the following one, which is how a biennial pattern gets locked in.', 'Thinning', 'The window that sets return bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(803, 34, 81, 'Scoring or ringing the bark of the trunk or a large scaffold limb within a few weeks of bloom improves fruit set by holding carbohydrate above the cut. It is a strong intervention on a strong tree and a damaging one on a weak tree.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Scoring or ringing for fruit set', 'confirmed'),
	(804, 45, 81, 'Harvest fruit size can be predicted well before harvest by measuring fruit diameter through the season and projecting the growth curve forward. It turns the size outcome into something that can still be acted on by thinning or irrigation.', 'Harvest management', 'Projecting harvest fruit size', 'confirmed'),
	(805, 9, 81, 'When an old block comes out, remove the root pieces as well as the trees: anything longer than about 30 to 40 cm or thicker than 4 to 5 cm carries the replant problem forward. Cut roots with a disc to break them up, and burn the old trees away from the ground that will be replanted rather than on it.', 'Disease management', 'Removing the old block cleanly', 'confirmed'),
	(806, 9, 81, 'Between removal and replanting, keep the soil where it is: interceptor drains, temporary contour grades or rough ploughing, and a cover crop established over the bare ground. A replant site that has lost its topsoil to a winter has a second problem on top of the first.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Holding the ground between blocks', 'confirmed'),
	(795, 6, 81, 'The common patterns are one pollinizer in twenty for a large block and one in ten where pollination is harder -- under net, or in a variety known to set poorly. At close in-row spacing a one-in-ten ratio leaves no tree more than about three metres from a pollinizer, which is the figure the ratio is really chosen for.', 'Pollination', 'Choosing the pollinizer ratio', 'confirmed'),
	(809, 34, 81, 'Choose the inter-row cover crop against the pests the block is likely to carry, not only against the soil. The wrong species feeds the pest and the right one feeds its natural enemies, and both effects run all season.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Cover crop chosen against the pests', 'confirmed'),
	(811, 6, 81, 'Do not carry planting into late spring. Past that point the top starts into growth before the root system has made contact with the soil, and the tree spends its first season recovering rather than establishing.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Do not plant late', 'confirmed'),
	(812, 6, 81, 'A tree planted too deep can be raised carefully, but only within about two weeks of planting. After that the roots have set and lifting does more damage than the depth would have.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Correcting a tree planted too low', 'confirmed'),
	(813, 6, 81, 'Water the trees in at planting, by hand, rain or irrigation. The purpose is to close the air gaps around the roots rather than to supply water, which is why it matters even on wet ground.', 'Irrigation', 'Watering trees in', 'confirmed'),
	(814, 7, 81, 'Give young trees nitrogen in small frequent doses, applied to the soil or through fertigation, rather than in a few large ones. A small root system cannot take up a large application, and what it cannot take up leaches past it.', 'Fertilization', 'Nitrogen in small frequent doses', 'confirmed'),
	(815, 8, 81, 'Balance crop load against what the tree can actually yield, at every age. Overcropping costs fruit size this season, return bloom next season, and on a young tree, the structure it was still building.', 'Thinning', 'Never overcrop a tree of any age', 'confirmed'),
	(816, 15, 81, 'A curative fungicide is defined by its window, not by its label: it has to go on as soon as possible after the infection period and within the material''s after-infection interval. Applied outside that window it is a protectant, and an expensive one.', 'Disease management', 'Using a curative fungicide', 'confirmed'),
	(817, 36, 81, 'Where no degree-day model is running, first cover sprays for the internal-feeding moths go on about seven to ten days after complete petal fall, with subsequent intervals not exceeding about a fortnight. Where a model is available, time from accumulated degree days after trap detection instead -- it is the more accurate of the two by a wide margin.', 'IPDM', 'First cover timing without a model', 'confirmed'),
	(819, 8, 81, 'For optimum light interception, hold mature tree height at about 0.8 to 0.9 times the row spacing -- roughly 2.8 to 4.0 m of tree for rows at 3.5 to 4.5 m. Taller than that and the row shades its neighbour; shorter and the block is not using the ground it occupies.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Height against row spacing', 'confirmed'),
	(820, 6, 81, 'Keep planting distances inside about 2.5 m in the row and 4.5 m between rows. Past those the canopy cannot close the space it was given, and yield per hectare falls away regardless of how well the individual tree performs.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Upper limits on planting distance', 'confirmed'),
	(821, 6, 81, 'Increase planting density by 20 to 30 per cent on an old orchard site. Replant-affected trees grow more slowly, and the extra trees are what recovers the yield the site would otherwise lose in the establishment years.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Density on a replant site', 'confirmed'),
	(822, 8, 81, 'Where light interception needs to rise, plant more trees at the leaf density that already works rather than growing denser canopies on the trees in the ground. Extra leaf layers inside a canopy shade each other; extra canopies do not.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Add trees rather than leaf layers', 'confirmed'),
	(823, 7, 81, 'On a vertical-axis tree, carry about 12 to 16 fruiting branches. Fewer and the tree over-crops what it has; more and the branches compete and none of them renew.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Fruiting branch number on a vertical axis', 'confirmed'),
	(824, 7, 81, 'On a Y-trellis, the arm angle trades yield against balance: about 60 degrees gives the best yield, while 65 to 70 degrees gives the better balance between cropping and manageable vigour.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Arm angle on a Y-trellis', 'confirmed'),
	(826, 21, 81, 'On a horizontal cordon, cincture at the point where a vertical shoot is wanted -- around 30 cm intervals in the systems built this way -- so the bud below the cut takes up as a renewal shoot.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Cincturing for a vertical shoot', 'confirmed'),
	(827, 45, 81, 'In a planting that has closed up more than intended, summer pruning is what recovers light to the fruiting wood within the season. It is a correction rather than a system, and a block needing it every year is telling you the spacing or the rootstock was wrong.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Summer pruning in a crowded planting', 'confirmed'),
	(828, 7, 81, 'Permanent netting over a young planting -- grey in particular -- shifts growth towards the tree in the first two years after planting, which is a reason to net early rather than after the block is bearing.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Netting on a young planting', 'confirmed'),
	(829, 8, 81, 'A netted block is a different orchard and has to be managed as one: light, temperature, humidity, pollination and spray behaviour all change under net. Where fruit set falls after netting, look at the pollination system before looking at the flowers.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Managing a netted block differently', 'confirmed'),
	(830, 7, 81, 'Thin second-leaf trees hard under net -- around two fruit per square centimetre of trunk cross-sectional area -- so the extra growth the net produces goes into building the tree rather than into a crop it cannot yet carry.', 'Thinning', 'Crop load on a netted second leaf', 'confirmed'),
	(831, 8, 81, 'Assess the financial impact of a hail event by when in the season it falls, not by the damage alone. The same storm costs a different amount in November than in March, and that timing is what the netting investment is actually being weighed against.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Costing a hail event', 'confirmed'),
	(832, 27, 81, 'Where a pesticide has to go on prebloom, choose the material with the lower toxicity to bees and the shorter residual, and move plant-systemic applications back to the half-inch green window rather than pink or white bud. A systemic applied close to bloom is still in the flower when the bees arrive.', 'IPDM', 'Prebloom sprays and bees', 'confirmed'),
	(810, 6, 81, 'Where trees arrive before the ground is ready, stand them upright in a bin with the roots thoroughly covered in moist sawdust and the tops moistened daily, and only while they are fully dormant. A tree that starts into growth in the bin has already lost the season.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Holding trees before planting', 'confirmed'),
	(833, 36, 81, 'Where a systemic insecticide is used near bloom, apply it only after most blossoms have dropped, restrict the choice to the most bee-safe compounds in the class, and withhold entirely while bees are visiting the treated area -- including flowering weeds on the orchard floor.', 'IPDM', 'Systemic insecticides around bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(834, 46, 81, 'Overhead sprinkling cools fruit directly by evaporation and is the intervention available when the heat has already arrived, as distinct from netting and reflective materials, which have to be in place before it.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Overhead sprinkling against heat', 'confirmed'),
	(835, 15, 81, 'The first scab fungicide goes on at green tip. In a block with genuinely low overwintering inoculum the start can be delayed, but that is a decision taken from an autumn assessment, not from the look of the spring.', 'Disease management', 'Scab programme start', 'confirmed'),
	(836, 15, 81, 'Run the primary scab programme at roughly 7 to 10 day intervals, tightening to 5 to 7 days under pressure, and spray more often in a cool wet spring than a warm dry one. The interval is set by growth and rainfall, not by the calendar.', 'Disease management', 'Scab spray interval', 'confirmed'),
	(837, 10, 81, 'The dormant to silver tip fungicide and bactericide spray goes on above about 45 F. A fixed copper here is the choice where scab or fire blight was severe the previous season, and it is doing inoculum reduction rather than protection.', 'Disease management', 'Dormant to silver tip spray', 'confirmed'),
	(838, 15, 81, 'Apply copper at green tip where no dormant copper went on, or where significant rain has fallen since it did. Copper is the material with the shortest memory in the programme: what the rain removed is simply gone.', 'Disease management', 'Copper at green tip', 'confirmed'),
	(839, 24, 81, 'Time the prebloom sprays off the growth stages rather than the calendar: about seven days after half-inch green, then seven to ten days after tight cluster. Each interval covers the tissue that emerged since the last one.', 'Disease management', 'Prebloom spray timing', 'confirmed'),
	(840, 24, 81, 'The anilinopyrimidine class performs best in cool conditions -- below about 70 F -- which makes it the prebloom choice in a cold spring where other single-site materials are weakest.', 'Disease management', 'Cool-weather fungicide choice', 'confirmed'),
	(841, 36, 81, 'A sterol-inhibiting fungicide applied within about 96 hours of a scab infection period still gives control of that infection. Past that window it is a protectant, and the infection it was bought to stop is already established.', 'Disease management', 'The post-infection window', 'confirmed'),
	(842, 36, 81, 'Use complete sprays rather than alternate-row-middle from bloom through petal fall, and switch back from alternate rows whenever rain events are frequent. Where alternate rows are run, the block still needs at least two half-sprays, or one complete spray, per interval.', 'Disease management', 'Complete sprays through the critical period', 'confirmed'),
	(843, 45, 81, 'Place the classes across the season deliberately: sterol inhibitors early, where scab and mildew are the targets, and hold the Group 7 materials for the late-season and preharvest diseases they are strongest against. Spending a class early leaves nothing for the disease it was needed for.', 'Disease management', 'Where each class belongs in the season', 'confirmed'),
	(844, 34, 81, 'The risk condition is an average daily temperature around 60 F or higher together with a wetting event on open flowers. Run a decision-support model rather than the thermometer alone -- the models integrate the accumulated warmth the bacterial population has had, which the day''s temperature does not show.', 'Disease management', 'Judging fire blight risk', 'confirmed'),
	(845, 34, 81, 'Where a bactericide programme is run at bloom, begin at about 10 per cent bloom and repeat at three to six day intervals, planning on a minimum of two applications. One application covers the flowers open on that day and nothing that opens after it.', 'Disease management', 'Bactericide programme at bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(846, 45, 81, 'Apply a bactericide within 24 hours of a hailstorm at any stage of the season. Hail opens wounds across the whole canopy at once, and it is the one event that makes a post-bloom application worthwhile.', 'Disease management', 'Fire blight after hail', 'confirmed'),
	(847, 21, 81, 'A prohexadione-calcium programme that hardens shoot tips off ten to fourteen days early suppresses shoot blight, because the succulent tip the bacterium needs is no longer there. Target it at vigorous young trees that have nearly filled their space, and add mid-season applications where vigour continues; on a settled block it is not worth the cost.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Growth control as shoot blight control', 'confirmed'),
	(848, 24, 81, 'Begin mildew management at tight cluster -- at green tip where the block carries pressure from last season -- and carry it through to about second cover. Where biological materials are used they are preventive and rotated, applied against the threshold rather than against visible mildew.', 'Disease management', 'Powdery mildew programme', 'confirmed'),
	(849, 27, 81, 'Treat quince rust as economically important where a wetting event runs more than 48 hours with a mean temperature above 50 F. There is no curative action available once a rust lesion is visible, so the whole decision sits before infection.', 'Disease management', 'Quince rust risk condition', 'confirmed'),
	(850, 45, 81, 'Start the sooty blotch and flyspeck programme at roughly 220 accumulated hours of leaf wetness after petal fall, then hold cover at about 14-day intervals through summer. Accumulated wetness is the trigger; the calendar only tells you when to start counting.', 'Disease management', 'Sooty blotch and flyspeck accumulation', 'confirmed'),
	(851, 45, 81, 'Where scab lesions are already visible, a material with anti-sporulant activity cuts secondary spread from them. It does not cure the lesion; it stops the lesion supplying the next infection.', 'Disease management', 'Suppressing sporulation on visible lesions', 'confirmed'),
	(852, 24, 81, 'Copper can be carried up to tight cluster only where surface russeting of the fruit is not a concern for the variety and the market. Beyond that point the russet cost is the limiting factor, not the efficacy.', 'Disease management', 'Copper and russet', 'confirmed'),
	(857, 56, 81, 'Where scab pressure ran high through the season, a broad-spectrum fungicide after harvest reduces what overwinters into next spring. It is the cheapest scab spray of the programme because it is the one that lowers next year''s starting inoculum.', 'Disease management', 'Post-harvest broad-spectrum cover', 'confirmed'),
	(858, 6, 81, 'Systemic materials for Phytophthora crown and collar rot work before symptoms appear, not after. Dose a trunk drench by trunk diameter, time a soil application to rain or irrigation that will carry it to the root zone, and where the risk is known at planting, a pre-plant root dip is the cheapest application there is.', 'Disease management', 'Phosphonate and phenylamide timing', 'confirmed'),
	(859, 6, 81, 'Do not try to grow through replant disease by choosing a more vigorous rootstock. The vigour is expressed above ground while the problem stays in the root zone, and the block ends up both replant-affected and too vigorous to manage.', 'Disease management', 'Vigour is not a replant treatment', 'confirmed'),
	(861, 27, 81, 'Hang delta traps with a rubber septum lure at about one per five acres, in the upper third of the canopy where the moths fly. A trap hung low reads a different population from the one doing the damage.', 'IPDM', 'Pheromone trap deployment', 'confirmed'),
	(862, 27, 81, 'Check traps daily until the first adult is caught and record that date as the biofix, then move to twice-weekly checks. The date is the input every degree-day model runs on, so it is the one reading that has to be right.', 'IPDM', 'Finding biofix from the trap line', 'confirmed'),
	(863, 27, 81, 'Compare trap counts against a published economic injury level rather than against last week''s count -- for tufted apple bud moth that level is around 2.4 adults per trap by tight cluster. A rising count below the level is information, not a spray decision.', 'IPDM', 'Trap counts against an injury level', 'confirmed'),
	(864, 36, 81, 'Check about two hundred fruit per block periodically through the season for fresh feeding injury. It is the count that separates damage already done from damage still happening, which is what the spray decision turns on.', 'IPDM', 'Fruit inspection for fresh feeding', 'confirmed'),
	(866, 15, 81, 'Concentrate early two-spotted mite monitoring on the inner, lower part of the tree. That is where the population builds before it is visible anywhere else, and where an early treatment is still cheap.', 'IPDM', 'Where to look for mites early', 'confirmed'),
	(867, 30, 81, 'Monitor apple sawfly adults with white sticky panels. The colour is the point -- the adults are drawn to white, which is what makes the panel a monitoring tool rather than a general trap.', 'IPDM', 'Sticky panels for sawfly', 'confirmed'),
	(868, 30, 81, 'Some thresholds are not a fixed number. For apple dimpling bug the action threshold falls as the fruitlet develops, computed from days since a reference stage, so the same count justifies a spray later in the window and not earlier. Do not spray this pest by the calendar or by crop stage alone.', 'IPDM', 'A threshold that changes with the stage', 'confirmed'),
	(869, 27, 81, 'Watch what is flowering outside the block. Dimpling bug builds on native and ornamental flowering species and on stonefruit blossom before it moves into apple, so those are the early indication -- and thrips arriving in weed flowers and early-flowering species are the same signal.', 'IPDM', 'Watching the surrounding vegetation', 'confirmed'),
	(870, 27, 81, 'Where a thrips spray does not work, look at resistance, application error and orchard hygiene before changing product -- and where thrips persist afterwards, suspect western flower thrips rather than the species you treated for.', 'IPDM', 'When a thrips spray fails', 'confirmed'),
	(871, 45, 81, 'Do not apply an insecticide against codling moth outside the stage it acts on. Material aimed at egg or newly hatched larvae applied when neither is present costs the money, the residue and the selection pressure, and controls nothing.', 'IPDM', 'Spray the stage, not the pest', 'confirmed'),
	(872, 36, 81, 'Time a systemic against the first leafminer generation while the larvae are still sap-feeding, and time later generations about two weeks after the pheromone trap-catch peak. The same material applied to a tissue-feeding larva does much less.', 'IPDM', 'Leafminer timing by generation', 'confirmed'),
	(873, 45, 81, 'For lightbrown apple moth, set the degree-day model biofix at the start of the rise in moth captures after petal fall rather than at the first individual caught. The first catch in a mixed population is noise; the rise is the generation.', 'IPDM', 'Setting a biofix after petal fall', 'confirmed'),
	(874, 30, 81, 'Time a trunk borer spray to peak egg hatch. The larva is only exposed between hatching and entering the trunk, which is a window of days, and after it the material cannot reach it at all.', 'IPDM', 'Borer sprays at peak egg hatch', 'confirmed'),
	(875, 45, 81, 'Apply the selective mite materials while populations are still low -- petal fall to first cover, or within about a month of bloom. They act on eggs and immature stages, so they are preventive materials being sold as miticides, and applied to a built population they fail.', 'IPDM', 'Mite materials while numbers are low', 'confirmed'),
	(876, 15, 81, 'Time the dormant oil to the pest it is aimed at: green tip where scale is the main target, delayed to half-inch green or pink where mites are. Apply above about 32 F for at least 24 hours either side of the spray, and hold oil until green tip rather than applying it truly dormant.', 'IPDM', 'Oil timing against the target', 'confirmed'),
	(877, 24, 81, 'Horticultural spray oils applied prebloom keep scale populations in check without touching the natural enemies that hold them there. It is the cheapest scale programme available and the one that costs the least in the rest of the block.', 'IPDM', 'Prebloom oil against scale', 'confirmed'),
	(878, 7, 81, 'Some pests are only worth treating on trees that cannot absorb the damage. Spring beetles are controlled on newly planted trees and trees up to about two years old, or in a year of unusual abundance; blister mite on young trees carrying an early infestation. On an established tree the same population is not worth a spray.', 'IPDM', 'Treat only where the tree cannot carry it', 'confirmed'),
	(860, 50, 81, 'Stop acting on infection warnings once the season has moved past the conditions the model was built for. Late in the season the model is still producing numbers, but the tissue it assumes is susceptible has hardened off.', 'Disease management', 'When the warnings stop applying', 'confirmed'),
	(856, 53, 81, 'A low-concentration chlorine dip followed by brushing reduces surface inoculum on fruit going into store. It is sanitation of the fruit surface, not a fungicide treatment, and it does nothing about infections already inside.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Sanitising dip and brushing', 'confirmed'),
	(879, 45, 81, 'Treat the part of the block that carries the pest. Plum curculio moves in from woods and fencerows, so the three or four border rows carry the damage and the spray; pear slug and woolly aphid sit on identified trees and are spot-sprayed or strip-sprayed rather than covered.', 'IPDM', 'Where the pest actually is', 'confirmed'),
	(880, 45, 81, 'Where the woolly aphid parasitoid is established in the block, strip-spray rather than cover-spray, so the wasp population survives in the untreated rows and recolonises the treated ones.', 'IPDM', 'Protecting a working parasitoid', 'confirmed'),
	(881, 15, 81, 'Egg parasitoid releases are an option against the leafroller complex, and they set a constraint on everything else: the release only pays if the broad-spectrum materials that would kill it are kept out of the programme.', 'IPDM', 'Releasing egg parasitoids', 'confirmed'),
	(882, 6, 81, 'Host-plant resistance is available for woolly apple aphid through the rootstock, which moves the decision to planting and removes a recurring below-ground problem that no spray reaches well.', 'IPDM', 'Rootstock resistance to woolly aphid', 'confirmed'),
	(883, 7, 81, 'A soil drench for woolly apple aphid is used on young trees -- up to about seven years old -- where the root population is what limits establishment. On a mature tree the volume needed and the residue make it a different decision.', 'IPDM', 'Soil-applied systemics on young trees', 'confirmed'),
	(885, 45, 81, 'Shorten spray intervals and add applications into first and second cover where the weather is cool and wet, or where the pest injured the block in a previous season. Both conditions extend the exposure period the programme has to cover.', 'IPDM', 'Shorten intervals in cool wet weather', 'confirmed'),
	(886, 10, 81, 'Where overwintering mealybugs are found on wood and bark, defer the spray until large numbers of young nymphs emerge in spring. The crawler stage is the exposed one; the overwintering stage is protected under wax and bark.', 'IPDM', 'Deferring a dormant spray', 'confirmed'),
	(887, 30, 81, 'Put fruit fly monitoring traps out before the accumulated-degree-day threshold for early activity, not when damage appears. A trap set after the flies arrive records the infestation instead of preventing it.', 'IPDM', 'Trapping before the activity threshold', 'confirmed'),
	(890, 45, 81, 'Where a block was affected the previous season, find and treat the underlying cause -- vigour, crop load, water, calcium supply -- rather than repeating the calcium programme alone. Bitter pit is a symptom of how the tree is being run.', 'Nutrient management', 'Treat the cause of bitter pit', 'confirmed'),
	(888, 8, 81, 'Lethal control of pest birds only reduces a population where more are removed than are being replaced, which is rarely achievable. Trapping after breeding catches many juveniles but leaves the breeders; trapping during breeding removes fewer birds but the ones that matter.', 'IPDM', 'Bird control that works', 'confirmed'),
	(889, 42, 81, 'Count seeds per fruit at fruit set. A low seed number signals poor pollination, and poorly pollinated fruit carry a higher bitter pit risk later -- which makes the pollination result an early nutritional warning rather than only a yield one.', 'Nutrient management', 'Seed count as a bitter pit signal', 'confirmed'),
	(910, 45, 81, 'Time the codling moth programme off accumulated degree days from biofix: a granulovirus application lands at roughly 230 to 240 degree days, a broad-spectrum material at about 250, and the second application of that generation around 550 to 600. The material sets the number, and the number sets the date.', 'IPDM', 'Codling moth degree-day windows', 'confirmed'),
	(911, 36, 81, 'Stop applications against first-generation plum curculio at about 310 degree-days. Past that point the generation is over and any further application is aimed at nothing.', 'IPDM', 'When first-generation plum curculio stops', 'confirmed'),
	(912, 36, 81, 'For oriental fruit moth the response scales with the catch: one application for the first generation, nothing for the second where catches never exceed about three moths per trap per week, a single application at three to five, and control measures once catches reach around seven.', 'IPDM', 'Trap thresholds by generation', 'confirmed'),
	(914, 27, 81, 'Sample 20 flower clusters per tree -- four clusters at each of five locations -- tapping a shoot carrying at least four clusters three or four times over a white one-to-four litre container, and do it in the morning. Same protocol every week or the counts are not comparable.', 'IPDM', 'Flower cluster tapping protocol', 'confirmed'),
	(915, 27, 81, 'Tap-sample the wattles, roadside vegetation and ornamentals around the block to establish the resident population before it moves in, and watch weather fronts as the incursion trigger. The block''s own counts tell you when it is too late.', 'IPDM', 'Sampling the vegetation around the block', 'confirmed'),
	(916, 27, 81, 'Once western flower thrips has been identified in the block, deploy blue sticky traps to carry the monitoring through the season. The species identification changes the monitoring method as well as the material.', 'IPDM', 'Blue traps once thrips are identified', 'confirmed'),
	(918, 6, 81, 'Set the planting back from wooded or shrubby borders -- on the order of 20 metres -- where the migrating pests come from. The setback is the cheapest border-row treatment available and it is applied once.', 'IPDM', 'Set the block back from the border', 'confirmed'),
	(919, 6, 81, 'Where dagger nematode transmits viruses on the site, plant the orchard ground to a non-host such as a grass. The nematode is not the problem; what it carries is, and a non-host ground cover breaks the chain.', 'Disease management', 'Ground cover against virus vectors', 'confirmed'),
	(920, 34, 81, 'Select active ingredients on the mode-of-action group and the beneficial-impact rating together. A material chosen only on efficacy can cost the block its predator population and generate the mite flare that the next spray then has to address.', 'IPDM', 'Choosing an active on two ratings at once', 'confirmed'),
	(921, 34, 81, 'Time nutrient applications to the periods when the roots are actually active -- the flush after bloom and the one after harvest -- rather than spreading them evenly through the season. Nutrient applied when the root system is quiescent is nutrient applied to the soil.', 'Nutrient management', 'Time nutrition to root activity', 'confirmed'),
	(922, 45, 81, 'Concentrate the calcium programme in the early covers, roughly first through third. The fruit takes up calcium while it is still small and transpiring hard; later applications land on a fruit that has largely stopped importing it.', 'Fertilization', 'Calcium in the early covers', 'confirmed'),
	(923, 46, 81, 'Through cell enlargement, top up what the tissue tests show is short: supplementary calcium where levels have fallen away, nitrogen through calcium nitrate fertigation, and potassium as a thiosulphate where the crop is pulling the block down. Each is a response to a measurement rather than a routine.', 'Fertilization', 'Supplementary nutrition during cell enlargement', 'confirmed'),
	(924, 45, 81, 'In a nitrogen-excess situation the correct application is none. Extra nitrogen at this point costs colour, firmness and storage life, and adds the vigour that then has to be pruned back out.', 'Fertilization', 'Withholding nitrogen', 'confirmed'),
	(925, 55, 81, 'An autumn foliar urea application on low-nitrogen trees puts nitrogen into reserves while the leaves are still able to move it, which is what funds next spring''s growth before the roots are active.', 'Nutrient management', 'Autumn foliar urea', 'confirmed'),
	(926, 21, 81, 'Give a young block regular nitrogen through spring and early summer, when it is building the framework it will crop on. This is the one stage where pushing growth is the objective rather than the problem.', 'Fertilization', 'Spring nitrogen on a young block', 'confirmed'),
	(927, 36, 81, 'Balance nutrition from soil and tissue analysis read together. The soil test says what is available and the tissue test says what the tree actually took up, and a decision made on either alone is missing half the question.', 'Nutrient management', 'Soil and tissue together', 'confirmed'),
	(917, 64, 81, 'Start mite leaf sampling from the outer canopy from pink onward, using an 8 to 10 times hand lens, and record predatory mites on the same leaves. A mite count without the predator count cannot support a decision to leave the population alone.', 'IPDM', 'Mite sampling from pink', 'confirmed'),
	(913, 50, 81, 'Keep scab cover intervals to no more than about three weeks in the lead-up to harvest. Late infections do not show as lesions before picking; they show as pinpoint scab in store.', 'Disease management', 'Scab cover into harvest', 'confirmed'),
	(909, 53, 81, 'Do not use an orchard fungicide as a postharvest treatment. Where a postharvest problem needs treating, use a material registered for that use -- the residue rules, the contact times and the water are all different.', 'Disease management', 'Postharvest products are not orchard products', 'confirmed'),
	(928, 8, 81, 'Take routine leaf samples from trees chosen to represent the block -- consistent vigour, consistent crop -- and use the same trees each year. A sample from whichever trees were convenient measures the sampling, not the block.', 'Nutrient management', 'Selecting the trees for a leaf sample', 'confirmed'),
	(930, 46, 81, 'Where a controlled deficit is used during cell expansion -- from around 45 days after bloom to harvest -- it is run against a measured soil water deficit rather than by feel, and the size cost is accepted deliberately in exchange for the water or the quality gain.', 'Irrigation', 'Deficit irrigation in the expansion phase', 'confirmed'),
	(931, 34, 81, 'Cross-pollination has to reach the king blossom in each cluster, because it sets the largest fruit and sets it first. That makes bloom overlap with king blossom emergence the timing that matters, not overlap with the cluster as a whole.', 'Pollination', 'The king blossom is the target', 'confirmed'),
	(932, 34, 81, 'Stock two to three hives per hectare as a minimum and three to five in a high-density planting, raising both the number and the condition of the colonies as tree and blossom density rise. A hive count set for a traditional planting is short in a modern one.', 'Pollination', 'Hive density against planting density', 'confirmed'),
	(933, 34, 81, 'Assess pollination by counting flower visitation against a target rate rather than by counting hives. Hives are what is paid for; visits are what sets the crop, and the two come apart under poor weather, competing forage or a weak colony.', 'Pollination', 'Measuring the service, not the input', 'confirmed'),
	(934, 34, 81, 'Keep weed blossom -- dandelion, mustard, wild radish -- out of the orchard through bloom. A flowering orchard floor is direct competition for the foragers that were brought in for the trees.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Competing blossom on the floor', 'confirmed'),
	(935, 34, 81, 'Keep pesticide use to the minimum while hives are on the property, not merely off the flowers. The colony forages beyond the block and the beekeeper is carrying the risk of everything applied while it is there.', 'IPDM', 'Pesticides while hives are on the property', 'confirmed'),
	(936, 34, 81, 'Leave overhead clearance between the tops of the trees and the net, and limit tree height to preserve it. Bees need flight space above the canopy; a canopy grown into the net removes the volume they work in.', 'Pollination', 'Clearance under the net', 'confirmed'),
	(937, 34, 81, 'On a pitched net, open the inter-row seams over the flowering period. It restores the flight paths and the light at the one time of year when both decide the crop.', 'Pollination', 'Opening a pitched net at flowering', 'confirmed'),
	(938, 8, 81, 'Place nets high above the canopy rather than close to it. The extra height costs structure but buys the air volume, the light and the flight space that a tight net takes away.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Placing nets high', 'confirmed'),
	(939, 34, 81, 'A gibberellin and cytokinin treatment applied within about 24 hours of a frost can set parthenocarpic fruit on flowers whose ovules were killed. It is a rescue with a short window -- after it, the decision has already been made by the frost.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Post-frost rescue treatment', 'confirmed'),
	(940, 50, 81, 'An auxin applied before harvest holds fruit on the tree for roughly 10 to 14 days, and a maturation-delaying treatment is applied about a month before the expected harvest date. Both extend the picking window rather than the storage life.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Delaying abscission with auxin', 'confirmed'),
	(941, 6, 81, 'A rootstock recommendation is only valid for a scion and a region together: a stock that performs well generally can be specifically not recommended under one cultivar in one production region. Check the combination against local trial results, not the rootstock''s general reputation.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Rootstock, scion and region together', 'confirmed'),
	(943, 6, 81, 'Specify the support with the rootstock and scion, not after them. A precocious scion on a semi-dwarfing stock has been carried on two wires -- one at around 36 to 40 inches and a second at eight to nine feet -- and the wire positions are part of the system rather than a detail of installation.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Support specified with the combination', 'confirmed'),
	(945, 6, 81, 'Assess site suitability formally before committing: identify the limiting factors the site carries, decide how each will be overcome, and price that work into the establishment. A limiting factor identified after planting becomes a permanent operating cost.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Assessing site suitability', 'confirmed'),
	(947, 34, 81, 'Read the pollination before setting the thinning. Where pollination was poor, reduce the thinning or hold the first blossom spray until enough flowers have been fertilised; a caustic blossom thinner applied over a light set removes the crop the season had.', 'Thinning', 'Thinning against the pollination result', 'confirmed'),
	(948, 34, 81, 'Thin the outside rows and headlands harder than the interior -- they set more because they carry more light -- and on east-west rows, treat the two sides on different days at different rates for the same reason. A single rate across a block over-thins one part of it.', 'Thinning', 'Thinning is not uniform across a block', 'confirmed'),
	(949, 34, 81, 'On a heavily spurred block, bring the blossom thinner forward and time it more tightly. The spurs open together and the window in which the spray does what is intended is correspondingly shorter.', 'Thinning', 'Spurred blocks thin earlier', 'confirmed'),
	(950, 45, 81, 'Where the crop is still above target after the first postbloom spray, repeat at seven to ten day intervals until the target is reached or about 60 days after full bloom, whichever comes first. Past that point the fruit will not abscise and the remaining correction is by hand.', 'Thinning', 'Repeating a postbloom thinner', 'confirmed'),
	(929, 67, 81, 'Where aerial or canopy scanning is used to map nutrition, calibrate it against plant tissue analysis and the leaf condition on the ground first. The scan extends a calibrated measurement across the block; it does not replace the measurement.', 'Nutrient management', 'Calibrating a scan against tissue', 'confirmed'),
	(946, 8, 81, 'Where the variety is wrong but the block is sound, graft over the existing trees -- the step-graft method -- rather than pulling and replanting. The root system, the support and the irrigation stay, and the new variety crops years earlier than a replant would.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Grafting over an established block', 'confirmed'),
	(952, 45, 81, 'Thinning converts into fruit size only where water and nutrition can carry the fruit that were left. On a block short of either, the thinning has removed yield without buying size.', 'Thinning', 'Thinning only pays with water and nutrition', 'confirmed'),
	(953, 45, 81, 'Where hail or frost has damaged the crop beyond salvage, an ethylene-releasing product at around 40 to 50 days after full bloom removes it. Taking the crop off deliberately returns the tree to growth and protects next season''s bloom; leaving damaged fruit on does neither.', 'Thinning', 'Stripping a damaged crop', 'confirmed'),
	(954, 8, 81, 'Calibrate the fruit-per-square-centimetre target against the block''s own cropping record rather than adopting a published figure. The same number produces a different crop on a different rootstock, soil and canopy, and the block''s history is the only calibration that accounts for all three.', 'Thinning', 'Calibrating the crop load target', 'confirmed'),
	(956, 45, 81, 'Trunk scoring is timed to about two and a half weeks after full bloom, when the fruitlets are competing hardest and holding carbohydrate above the cut changes the outcome. The same cut before bloom does something different.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Trunk scoring timing', 'confirmed'),
	(957, 45, 81, 'Where a ring girdle is used rather than a score, the published specification is two overlapping horizontal rings cut to about 2 cm deep and spaced apart on the trunk. Depth and spacing are the treatment; a deeper or continuous ring is an injury.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Ring girdling specification', 'confirmed'),
	(958, 10, 81, 'Cut root suckers off at ground level in the dormant season, then treat the regrowth once it is a few inches long with a sucker-suppressing auxin product, and repeat the treatment the following year. Cutting alone selects for more suckers.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Root suckers', 'confirmed'),
	(959, 7, 81, 'A gibberellin and cytokinin branching spray is directed at one-year-old wood, applied when shoots are one to three inches, and only to high-vigour trees -- a weak tree does not have the reserves to build the branches it forces. Whole-tree treatment belongs on young non-bearing trees only.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Branching spray targeting', 'confirmed'),
	(960, 7, 81, 'Check the spray mixture and acidify it if it tests alkaline: the branching materials break down in alkaline water before they reach the bud. Multiple applications are used where a single one does not produce the number of branches wanted.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Branching spray mixture', 'confirmed'),
	(961, 21, 81, 'Time the first prohexadione-calcium application to one to three inches of shoot growth, or to three or four developed cluster leaves. Applied later it is working against extension that has already happened.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'First growth-regulator application', 'confirmed'),
	(962, 21, 81, 'Expect more growth suppression on a heavily cropped, drought-stressed or dwarfed tree, and less on a cropless, well-watered vigorous one -- which is where more than the usual number of applications is used. Monitor the most vigorous trees in the block to time the additional sprays rather than treating on a fixed schedule.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'How much suppression to expect', 'confirmed'),
	(963, 34, 81, 'Where more vigour control is wanted, an ethylene-releasing product is used as a companion to the second and subsequent growth-regulator sprays, so return bloom is not suppressed along with the shoot growth.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'A companion for return bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(964, 21, 81, 'Prune to the habit the cultivar has. A basitonic, spur-type habit takes light heading-back to keep renewal coming; an acrotonic, weeping habit is headed back to lift the fruiting wood off the ground. The same cuts on the wrong habit produce the opposite result.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Prune to the growth habit', 'confirmed'),
	(965, 34, 81, 'Before pruning, assess how much of the block''s flowering sits on spurs and how much on terminal long shoots. That ratio decides whether renewal pruning is removing fruiting wood or creating it.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Reading where the flowers are', 'confirmed'),
	(966, 7, 81, 'Do not head the leader on a second- or third-leaf tree. In the second leaf, remove nothing larger than about half the diameter of the central axis; in the third, take out limbs above about two-thirds the leader diameter and cut broken branches back to a spur. The tree is still building the axis everything else hangs on.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Second and third leaf limits', 'confirmed'),
	(967, 7, 81, 'Set winter height with a cut through one-year-old wood rather than into older wood -- around 2.7 m in the systems built this way. Cutting into older wood at the top produces the vigorous regrowth that has to be removed again next season.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Winter height cut', 'confirmed'),
	(968, 45, 81, 'Choose the summer pruning date by the response wanted: earlier cuts produce regrowth, later ones do not, and the effect on next season''s flowering moves with the date. Compare it against what a dormant cut would have done before deciding it is worth the pass.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Summer pruning date sets the response', 'confirmed'),
	(970, 45, 81, 'Treat root pruning as a rescue treatment for a block that has run away, not as an annual operation. It is the strongest vigour tool available and the least reversible one.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Root pruning is a rescue', 'confirmed'),
	(971, 45, 81, 'Plot fruit growth curves across treatments and seasons rather than only recording final size. The shape of the curve separates a crop-load effect from a water effect from a temperature effect, which a single harvest measurement cannot do.', 'Harvest management', 'Reading fruit growth curves', 'confirmed'),
	(972, 6, 81, 'Where trees are held before planting, store them at around 4 C for the first four to six weeks. That holds them dormant without drying them, and it is the condition the rest of the planting plan assumes.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Cold storage before planting', 'confirmed'),
	(973, 8, 81, 'Design and manage the tree form to intercept about 60 per cent of midseason sunlight. That figure is what the density, the rootstock, the row spacing and the canopy shape are all chosen to reach -- below it the block is not using the site, and much above it the lower canopy starts losing light to the row above.', 'Pruning & Training', 'The light interception target', 'confirmed'),
	(974, 6, 81, 'Design a high-density planting to intercept a high proportion of the available radiation within its first three years. The years before the canopy fills are carrying the establishment cost with none of the yield, and shortening them is most of what the density is buying.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Reaching the target early', 'confirmed'),
	(975, 6, 81, 'The working density band for a dwarfing planting is roughly 2,000 to 3,000 trees per hectare, and above about 1,900 trees per hectare on a dwarfing rootstock the block can reach the light interception target. Very high densities -- of the order of 5,000 trees per hectare -- are not recommended: the cost rises faster than the light captured.', 'Modify Landscape', 'The density band that works', 'confirmed'),
	(976, 8, 81, 'Fill the canopy volume with evenly spaced branches and fruiting spurs, holding extension shoots to around 30 cm or less. Light interception comes from evenly distributed fruiting wood, not from long shoots that shade what is beneath them.', 'Pruning & Training', 'What fills the canopy volume', 'confirmed'),
	(977, 8, 81, 'Where rows have to run east to west, hold leaf area index at or below about 2.0. That orientation shades one side of the canopy for much of the day, and the extra leaf layers a north-south row can carry simply darken it.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Leaf area index on east-west rows', 'confirmed'),
	(978, 8, 81, 'Bring the vigour tools in -- summer pruning, trunk girdling, root pruning, a controlled deficit -- once light interception passes about 60 per cent and leaf area index is approaching 3.0. That combination is the point at which additional growth is being added inside a canopy that is already full.', 'Pruning & Training', 'When to start pulling vigour back', 'confirmed'),
	(979, 45, 81, 'Diagnose canopy density from the shadow the tree casts at midday. A speckled pattern of light and dark inside the shadow is the canopy letting light through to the fruiting wood; a solid shadow means the interior is dark, and small shadows with the floor in full sun mean the block is not intercepting what it could.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Reading the canopy from its shadow', 'confirmed'),
	(980, 6, 81, 'Bring a new variety into the business on a small test planting -- of the order of twenty trees -- before committing a block to it. The trial costs one row and answers questions about the variety on this site that no published description can.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Start a variety on a test planting', 'confirmed'),
	(982, 8, 81, 'Moving from a standard variety to a higher-colour strain of it takes on the order of five years to work through, counting the propagation, the establishment and the first commercial crops. Start the change before the market difference is urgent.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Changing to a higher-colour strain', 'confirmed'),
	(983, 7, 81, 'Some varieties need to be held off cropping longer than the general rule while they build structure. Cropping them on the standard schedule stunts the tree and the block never reaches the size it was designed for.', 'Thinning', 'Do not crop a slow-starting variety too soon', 'confirmed'),
	(985, 46, 81, 'Through cell enlargement the target is to keep soil water in the readily available range rather than to refill after a deficit. It is the stage where a check costs final fruit size directly, and the size is not recovered later.', 'Irrigation', 'Readily available water through cell enlargement', 'confirmed'),
	(986, 10, 81, 'Irrigation is not generally needed over dormancy, but the soil moisture should be checked at the start of spring rather than assumed. A block going into bud break on a dry profile starts the season behind.', 'Irrigation', 'Irrigation over dormancy', 'confirmed'),
	(987, 46, 81, 'Run a simple water budget alongside a soil moisture probe. The budget projects what the block will need and the probe says what actually happened; either alone produces a schedule that drifts from the soil.', 'Irrigation', 'A budget and a probe together', 'confirmed'),
	(988, 46, 81, 'Irrigating above crop evapotranspiration demand -- to the order of 150 per cent -- does not add growth or size. It leaches nitrogen past the root zone, keeps the soil oxygen-poor and increases the disease risk in the root zone.', 'Irrigation', 'Above crop demand is not more growth', 'confirmed'),
	(989, 8, 81, 'After a net goes up, re-diagnose problems rather than reaching for the pre-net explanation. Where fruit set falls in a netted block, the pollination system is the first thing to examine, and the whole management programme -- irrigation, nutrition, spray behaviour, crop load -- has to be readjusted to the modified environment.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Diagnosing a problem under net', 'confirmed'),
	(990, 8, 81, 'Weigh a hail net against the loss it prevents in the seasons it prevents it, not against an average year. The published assessments frame it as an investment decision at around a tenth of the crop value at risk, which is what makes the timing of a hail event as important as its severity.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Costing a hail net', 'confirmed'),
	(993, 15, 81, 'Where scab is run on a degree-day model, start the accumulation at bud break of the earliest varieties, or at a fixed regional date if that comes first. Whichever is used, hold the same convention between seasons or the accumulations are not comparable.', 'Disease management', 'Scab degree-day biofix', 'confirmed'),
	(994, 45, 81, 'The primary scab programme has an end as well as a start: once ascospore release is finished and the model shows the primary season closed, the interval lengthens. Continuing at primary-season intensity through summer spends material on a risk that has passed.', 'Disease management', 'When to ease off the scab programme', 'confirmed'),
	(995, 15, 81, 'Treat green tip as a combined operation rather than a spray: the copper application, the orchard sanitation that reduced the leaf litter, and the programme that follows all work on the same inoculum. Any one of the three on its own carries the season.', 'Disease management', 'Green tip as a combined operation', 'confirmed'),
	(984, 8, 81, 'Irrigate more frequently on a dwarfing rootstock. The root system is smaller and shallower than the canopy it carries, so it runs out of readily available water sooner than a more vigorous stock on the same soil and the same schedule.', 'Irrigation', 'Dwarfing rootstocks need water more often', 'confirmed'),
	(981, 8, 81, 'Where a variety is underperforming but the block is sound, rework it to another variety by grafting rather than replanting. The judgement is about the variety, not the orchard, and the orchard is the expensive half.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Reworking a poor performer', 'confirmed'),
	(996, 36, 81, 'Where a disease risk is high for a defined period rather than event-driven -- a susceptible variety in a wet district through the critical weeks -- a block of three applications at weekly intervals covers it more reliably than waiting for individual infection events to be identified.', 'Disease management', 'A block of sprays where risk is constant', 'confirmed'),
	(997, 56, 81, 'Where the season was a bad one for scab, the post-harvest urea treatment matters more, not less. It is the intervention that lowers the inoculum going into next spring, and the years it is most tempting to skip are the years it pays for itself.', 'Disease management', 'Post-harvest urea after a bad year', 'confirmed'),
	(999, 36, 81, 'Tarnished plant bug and the native stink bugs are treated at pink and again at petal fall where they are present. Outside those two timings the damage is already in the fruit and the spray is aimed at the next generation rather than this crop.', 'IPDM', 'Early-season plant bugs', 'confirmed'),
	(1000, 36, 81, 'Decide whether oriental fruit moth needs its own programme from two things: heavy damage in a previous season, or a block adjacent to stone fruit. Away from both, the codling moth programme already covers it and a separate one is a cost with no return.', 'IPDM', 'When oriental fruit moth is worth spraying', 'confirmed'),
	(1001, 45, 81, 'Hold mites at or below the established threshold rather than eliminating them. A block with no mites has no predator population either, and the flare that follows the next broad-spectrum spray has nothing to check it.', 'IPDM', 'Mites are managed to a threshold, not to zero', 'confirmed'),
	(1002, 10, 81, 'A paraffinic oil application after harvest is available where an infestation got away during the season. It works on the overwintering stage while the tree is bare, and it lowers what has to be dealt with at bud break.', 'IPDM', 'Paraffinic oil after harvest', 'confirmed'),
	(1004, 8, 81, 'Move the fertiliser programme from higher to lower nitrogen as the block matures. What was building a canopy in the establishment years is producing vigour, poor colour and soft fruit in a mature one, and the change is gradual rather than a switch.', 'Fertilization', 'Nitrogen falls as the block matures', 'confirmed'),
	(1005, 34, 81, 'Accumulate growing degree days for the district from bloom and carry the running total through the season. It is the number that says whether this season is ahead of or behind normal, which is what the harvest date, the labour booking and the storage plan are all being set against.', 'Harvest management', 'Seasonal heat accounting', 'confirmed'),
	(1007, 34, 81, 'Settle the thinning plan -- material, rate, water volume and the timing variations for different blocks -- a couple of weeks before the window opens. Inside the window there is no time to decide anything, and the decisions made under time pressure are the ones that go wrong.', 'Thinning', 'Lock the plan down before the window', 'confirmed'),
	(1008, 34, 81, 'Two conditions stop a blossom thinner going on: rain forecast within about 24 hours, which washes a caustic material off before it works, and temperatures below about 15 C, at which the hormone thinners do very little. In either case the spray is deferred rather than reduced.', 'Thinning', 'Conditions that hold a spray back', 'confirmed'),
	(1009, 34, 81, 'The blossom thinners are substitutable within the window: where conditions do not suit a caustic material, a hormone thinner is used in its place and the reverse. The substitution is made on the forecast, not on preference.', 'Thinning', 'Substituting one thinner for another', 'confirmed'),
	(1010, 45, 81, 'Where the first season''s thinning result was unsatisfactory, repeat the programme the following year at a tighter interval -- of the order of seven to ten days between applications -- rather than raising the rate. Interval is the safer lever.', 'Thinning', 'Repeating an unsatisfactory programme', 'confirmed'),
	(1011, 34, 81, 'Calculate the crop load target from the market rather than from a rule of thumb: divide the tonnage goal by the desired average fruit weight to get the fruit number per hectare, then per tree. That number is what the thinning is aimed at, and it changes when either the yield goal or the size goal changes.', 'Thinning', 'From tonnage goal to fruit number', 'confirmed'),
	(1012, 46, 81, 'Crop load moves fruit size both ways. On a small-fruited block, take yield off to lift size; on a block running too big for the market, carry more crop to bring it down. The second direction is the one most programmes forget they have.', 'Thinning', 'Managing size in both directions', 'confirmed'),
	(1013, 7, 81, 'A notched disc or thinning gauge carries a factor that is adjusted for cultivar, target fruit size and tree vigour, and reduced on young trees relative to mature ones. Handing a crew the default setting on a young block leaves more crop than the trees can size.', 'Thinning', 'Adjusting a thinning gauge', 'confirmed'),
	(1015, 34, 81, 'The pollen tube growth model is run from a public weather network service using the block''s bloom dates and hourly temperatures, and the caustic bloom thinner goes on before the tubes reach the ovule. It converts bloom thinning from a judgement into a scheduled operation.', 'Thinning', 'Running the pollen tube model', 'confirmed'),
	(1016, 34, 81, 'Gibberellin inhibits flower bud initiation, which is used in two places: to hold a juvenile tree vegetative while it is still building structure, and in the on year of an alternate-bearing block to level the following crop. Timing differs by wood -- on spurs at or shortly after bloom, later on one-year-old shoots.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Gibberellin to suppress flowering', 'confirmed'),
	(1017, 45, 81, 'Do not use gibberellin on cultivars prone to pygmy fruit. The material that suppresses flowering on one cultivar produces a fruit defect on another, and the constraint is varietal rather than dose-related.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Where gibberellin does not belong', 'confirmed'),
	(998, 64, 81, 'For a pest that arrives from outside the block faster than monitoring can detect it, the published approach is a single prophylactic spray at the vulnerable stage -- late pink for apple dimpling bug -- followed by threshold-driven decisions for anything further. The first spray buys the time that monitoring then uses.', 'IPDM', 'Prophylactic first, threshold after', 'confirmed'),
	(1018, 45, 81, 'On a heavily loaded tree, an ethylene-releasing product combined with a foliar nutrient spray promotes return bloom. The load is what suppressed the flower initiation, and the treatment is aimed at the following season rather than at this crop.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Return bloom on a heavily loaded tree', 'confirmed'),
	(1019, 8, 81, 'On an older, taller intensive planting, recalibrate and move to a small tower sprayer rather than continuing with the machine the block was planted with. An air-blast pattern set for a young canopy does not reach the top of a mature one, and the top is where the crop is.', 'IPDM', 'Sprayer against canopy height', 'confirmed'),
	(1020, 45, 81, 'Where one vigour tool is not enough, they stack: a post-bloom ethylene-releasing spray, a summer auxin application and root pruning are used together on a block that has run away. Each acts on a different part of the growth cycle, and the combination is stronger than a heavier dose of any one.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Stacking vigour treatments', 'confirmed'),
	(46, 30, NULL, 'Apply a spinosyn or other bloom-registered insecticide from early through full bloom for leafroller and thrips, timing the application around petal fall so efficacy is kept and bee exposure minimised. The product registered for that use differs by country and season; take it from the guide.', 'IPDM', 'Bloom period insects', 'confirmed'),
	(111, 38, 81, 'Apply a return-bloom bioregulator -- an auxin such as NAA, or an ethylene-releasing product -- approximately six to eight weeks after petal fall to encourage flower bud formation for the following season.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Return bloom enhancement', 'confirmed'),
	(232, 46, 81, 'Apply an auxin or an ethylene-releasing bioregulator to encourage flower bud formation for next season.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Return bloom enhancement', 'confirmed'),
	(157, 34, 81, 'Use organically accepted alternatives: a plant defence activator, a low-rate soluble copper, or a Bacillus-based biofungicide.', 'Disease management', 'Organic options', 'confirmed'),
	(233, 46, 81, 'Suppress suckers with a sucker-inhibiting auxin product applied to the regrowth, or with a herbicide directed off the trunk.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Sucker control', 'confirmed'),
	(324, 8, 81, 'Check vegetative growth on a vigorous block with prohexadione-calcium, or with an ethylene-releasing product where shoot extension is running ahead of cropping.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Growth control', 'confirmed'),
	(808, 6, 81, 'Mound the planting row by moving topsoil in from the centre of the alley. It gives the new trees a deeper rooting zone and drainage the flat ground does not have, using soil already on the site. Keep the drainage lines clear so grassed waterways can still carry surface water off the block.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Mounding the planting row', 'confirmed'),
	(464, 10, 81, 'Measure the chill actually accumulating at the site against a named model, holding the same start and stop convention between seasons -- 1 March and 50 per cent leaf fall are the two in common use -- and read it alongside the heat units accumulated afterwards, since both govern bud break. A chill figure only means something against the window it was accumulated over.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Chill accumulation', 'confirmed'),
	(77, 15, 81, 'Apply dormant oil or miticide if overwintering eggs are present.', 'IPDM', 'European red mite', 'confirmed'),
	(78, 15, 81, 'Scout and apply insecticides at green tip before leaf curl.', 'IPDM', 'Rosy apple aphid', 'confirmed'),
	(431, 53, 81, 'Keep ethylene from building up in the room; it accelerates ripening and shortens the storage life of everything held with it.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Ethylene management', 'confirmed'),
	(751, 53, 81, 'Hold storage oxygen one to one and a half per cent above the carbon dioxide level, with carbon dioxide below one per cent, and establish the atmosphere without delay after harvest rather than letting the room stand in air.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Controlled atmosphere setpoints', 'confirmed'),
	(330, 67, 81, 'Run a maturity programme with repeat sampling as harvest approaches, not a single test.', 'Harvest management', 'Maturity programme', 'confirmed'),
	(333, 67, 81, 'Read background colour as a maturity index alongside the destructive tests.', 'Harvest management', 'Ground colour', 'confirmed'),
	(334, 67, 81, 'Set picking and handling to limit bruising; damage done here cannot be undone in store.', 'Harvest management', 'Handling', 'confirmed'),
	(387, 67, 81, 'Plan picking around excessive heat: it is a worker safety issue before it is a fruit quality one.', 'Harvest management', 'Worker heat safety', 'confirmed'),
	(429, 67, 81, 'Expect bird pressure to peak as fruit colours and sugars rise, and have the deterrents in place before that point, not after damage appears.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Bird damage at harvest', 'confirmed'),
	(450, 67, 81, 'Pick on scientifically valid maturity measurements: good post-harvest management can do nothing with fruit that was harvested too early or too late.', 'Harvest management', 'Maturity measurement', 'confirmed'),
	(475, 67, 81, 'Guard both ends of the harvest window. Immature early picks and over-mature late picks reach the consumer from the same season, and inconsistency -- not any single defect -- is the complaint growers hear most.', 'Harvest management', 'Maturity consistency', 'confirmed'),
	(504, 67, 81, 'Read the starch test against where the fruit is going. On the 1 to 8 scale, pick at 3 to 5 for long-term storage and at 6 to 7 for the fresh market. The 1 to 8 chart was developed for McIntosh and carries across many cultivars; Honeycrisp has its own 1 to 6 chart, so the value means nothing without the chart it was read from.', 'Harvest management', 'Starch test thresholds', 'confirmed'),
	(527, 67, 81, 'Sample each cultivar or strain and each block separately, since maturity varies between blocks and within a tree. Mark five to eight trees representative of the block for crop load and vigour, avoiding the borders, and take two to three fruit from the periphery of each -- periphery fruit ripen ahead of the inside of the canopy.', 'Harvest management', 'Maturity sampling design', 'confirmed'),
	(528, 67, 81, 'Begin sampling four to five weeks before the anticipated harvest date, weekly at first and more often as maturity advances. Use days after full bloom to anticipate that date, recording full bloom by block and cultivar each spring, and expect the estimate to sit five to twenty days from the optimal date.', 'Harvest management', 'Maturity sampling window', 'confirmed'),
	(529, 67, 81, 'Collect the sample at the same time of day each week, take fruit of similar size free of disease and insect damage, and measure within two hours of picking before temperature changes move the readings.', 'Harvest management', 'Sample handling', 'confirmed'),
	(530, 67, 81, 'Pick at a firmness of at least 15 lb for storage beyond three months, and 13 to 15 lb where the fruit will be held one to two months. Watercore inflates the reading and larger fruit is softer than small, so keep the sample uniform in size.', 'Harvest management', 'Firmness thresholds', 'confirmed'),
	(531, 67, 81, 'Use a 7/16-inch plunger, pare a disc of skin from the blush and non-blush sides midway between stem and calyx, and press to the scribed line at 7.9 mm over about two seconds -- pressing faster reads high. Average the two sides for the fruit value and keep the same person testing a given lot.', 'Harvest management', 'Firmness method', 'confirmed'),
	(532, 67, 81, 'Where a DA meter is used, harvest for long-term storage at an index of about 0.60 to 0.70 and for short-term storage at about 0.35. The index tracks skin chlorophyll-a and falls as the fruit ripens, and it can be read on fruit still hanging.', 'Harvest management', 'IAD thresholds', 'confirmed'),
	(533, 67, 81, 'Harvest fruit for long-term storage as the background colour turns from green to yellow, and hold the yellow-to-cream change for fruit going into short-term storage. This reads most clearly on cultivars such as Gala and Fuji.', 'Harvest management', 'Background colour targets', 'confirmed'),
	(534, 67, 81, 'Aim for roughly 12 to 14% soluble solids at harvest, adjusting by cultivar and market. Read the figure in context: it rises in hot, bright seasons, falls with heavy rain, heavy irrigation or a heavy crop, and runs lower on shaded fruit and weak spurs within the same tree.', 'Harvest management', 'Soluble solids target', 'confirmed'),
	(535, 67, 81, 'Read acidity as a rate of change across repeated samples from the same trees rather than against a target value: cultivars differ too widely in how fast they lose acid, and in the level they eat well at, for a fixed number to mean much.', 'Harvest management', 'Acidity as a rate', 'confirmed'),
	(536, 67, 81, 'Decide harvest on several indices together -- background and surface colour, firmness, starch, soluble solids and the trend in acidity. No single index explains the true maturity of an apple, and which ones matter most depends on the cultivar, the block and where the fruit is going.', 'Harvest management', 'Combined indices', 'confirmed'),
	(548, 67, 81, 'Where stem-bowl cracking is the risk, harvest before starch clearing reaches 3.5. Cracks develop as the fruit matures, so the maturity window closes earlier than the eating-quality indices alone would suggest.', 'Harvest management', 'Starch clearing and stem-bowl cracking', 'confirmed'),
	(635, 67, 81, 'Size the brown rot problem before the season, not after: pre-harvest losses of up to 45 per cent have been reported in organic pome-fruit orchards, and yield loss in unsprayed orchards ranges from 50 to 90 per cent.', 'Disease management', 'Brown rot losses without fungicides', 'confirmed'),
	(669, 67, 81, 'Do not lean on a Group 12 fungicide repeatedly, or in successive years, as the primary method of postharvest rot control. The population that matters here goes into store with the fruit and is selected on year after year.', 'Disease management', 'Postharvest rot and Group 12', 'confirmed'),
	(680, 67, 81, 'Some exclusions are absolute: do not tank-mix dodine with lime or other alkaline products, and do not combine an anilinopyrimidine with captan 50 WP below about 10 gallons of spray volume per acre.', 'Disease management', 'Known tank-mix exclusions', 'confirmed'),
	(740, 67, 81, 'Inspect 100 fruit per tree on five trees per block at harvest, with a one-minute foliage inspection on five trees alongside it. That pairing gives both the damage that reached the crop and the pressure still standing in the block.', 'Disease management', 'Harvest inspection sample', 'confirmed'),
	(743, 67, 81, 'Select for storage on the combination, not on one index: the desired background colour, starch conversion around 25 to 35 per cent, and firmness in range. Fruit outside that combination is a short-term or a processing line, decided at picking rather than discovered in store.', 'Harvest management', 'Storage candidate selection', 'confirmed'),
	(744, 67, 81, 'Base the harvest decision on at least three maturity parameters, sampled repeatedly through the approach to harvest and recorded across successive samplings. It is the trend across samples, not any single result, that identifies the optimum date.', 'Harvest management', 'Three parameters, repeated', 'confirmed'),
	(745, 67, 81, 'Where the block will be picked more than once, skim the better-coloured fruit early for long-term storage and leave the poorer-coloured fruit to develop. Select picks by size on the same principle, taking the smaller fruit later so it sizes into the market''s preferred profile.', 'Harvest management', 'Skim picking by colour and size', 'confirmed'),
	(746, 67, 81, 'Do not pick wet or cold fruit. Both carry damage into the bin that does not show until the fruit is out of store.', 'Harvest management', 'Do not pick wet or cold fruit', 'confirmed'),
	(747, 67, 81, 'Exclude watercore fruit from the pressure-test sample. It reads firmer than it is, and a sample carrying it will send the block to picking later than it should be.', 'Harvest management', 'Watercore in the firmness sample', 'confirmed'),
	(748, 67, 81, 'Where picking is directed by colour, test the crew for colour blindness. A picker who cannot separate the colour break is picking to a different standard from the one the block was assessed on.', 'Harvest management', 'Colour vision in the picking crew', 'confirmed'),
	(790, 67, 81, 'Plan the block for three to five picking passes where colour or size is being selected on. The number of passes is a planting-system decision as much as a harvest one, because it is the canopy that determines whether the later passes have anything worth taking.', 'Harvest management', 'Multiple picking passes', 'confirmed'),
	(818, 67, 81, 'Harvest blocks with a history of watercore ahead of the others. Watercore develops on the tree and the fruit does not store, so the block''s value is in getting it picked and moved rather than in holding it for a better window.', 'Harvest management', 'Pick watercore-prone blocks first', 'confirmed'),
	(853, 67, 81, 'Track rainfall through the harvest period and re-protect against fruit rots after each accumulation of roughly one to two inches. Fruit picked from an unprotected canopy after late-season rain carries the rot into store.', 'Disease management', 'Re-protecting through harvest', 'confirmed'),
	(865, 67, 81, 'The end-of-season codling moth assessment is at least five trees with 100 fruit examined on each. That sample is what tells you whether the programme worked, and it is the number the following season''s programme is built from.', 'IPDM', 'Detailed codling moth fruit inspection', 'confirmed'),
	(884, 67, 81, 'Pick up and destroy dropped fruit through the season. Dropped fruit carries larvae that would otherwise complete development on the orchard floor and return as the next generation.', 'IPDM', 'Picking up the drops', 'confirmed'),
	(892, 67, 81, 'Take a minimum of 20 fruit for starch and firmness. Where a smaller working sample is used, measure ten and add a further ten whenever the reading comes back marginal -- the extra ten cost minutes and the decision they inform costs a season.', 'Harvest management', 'Maturity sample size', 'confirmed'),
	(893, 67, 81, 'Choose the maturity indices by where the fruit is going, and use the results to allocate blocks between long-term and short-term storage. Fruit for export is only picked once the tests and the red-area colour both meet the export standard.', 'Harvest management', 'Indices chosen by destination', 'confirmed'),
	(894, 67, 81, 'Measure firmness at harvest and again after about ten days at room temperature. The pair of readings shows how fast the fruit is softening, which is what predicts its behaviour in store; a single reading does not.', 'Harvest management', 'Firmness now and in ten days', 'confirmed'),
	(895, 67, 81, 'Harvest as close to ripe as possible while the fruit still carries enough reserves to survive storage, and keep over-mature fruit out of long-term storage entirely. The two ends of that sentence are the whole harvest decision.', 'Harvest management', 'As ripe as the store allows', 'confirmed'),
	(896, 67, 81, 'Pick early varieties selectively and often -- a three to four day turnaround between passes. They move through their window faster than the crew can cover the block once.', 'Harvest management', 'Turnaround on early varieties', 'confirmed'),
	(897, 67, 81, 'Keep every drop in the harvest chain to no more than 10 to 15 cm, pad the harvesting equipment and keep sharp edges out of it. Bruising is cumulative down the chain and none of it is visible when it happens.', 'Harvest management', 'Drop height and equipment', 'confirmed'),
	(898, 67, 81, 'Do not disturb rotting hanging fruit while picking -- moving it spreads spores across the fruit being handled. Take it out and destroy it after harvest instead, and remove bird-damaged fruit as soon as it is seen.', 'Disease management', 'Rotting fruit at picking', 'confirmed'),
	(900, 67, 81, 'An ethylene-releasing product advances colour and maturity, and carries four constraints together: apply only where colouring conditions are already good, no earlier than about three weeks before the normal harvest date, inside the temperature window the material needs to be absorbed, and only over an area that can be picked within two to three days.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Advancing colour and maturity', 'confirmed'),
	(901, 67, 81, 'An AVG treatment delays maturity but should be kept off blocks with a history of poor colour development, because it delays colouring with everything else. Where a growth regulator is not wanted, trunk girdling advances maturity instead.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Delaying maturity, and its cost', 'confirmed'),
	(1006, 67, 81, 'Start harvest when the clear area in the starch pattern moves outside the core, not when it first appears. The pattern is read as a position rather than a percentage, and that position is what the cultivar chart is calibrated on.', 'Harvest management', 'Reading the starch pattern for the start', 'confirmed'),
	(1021, 47, 81, 'Boron can go on after harvest, but only while the leaves are still green: the leaf is the route in, so the application has to happen between picking and leaf discolouration. Once the canopy is shutting down there is nothing left to take it up, and the remaining option is to supply it through the solid fertiliser programme instead.', 'Nutrient management', 'Boron goes on while the leaves are green', 'confirmed'),
	(1022, 47, 81, 'Do not shut the water off at harvest. Irrigation continues after picking to carry the post-harvest nitrogen into the root zone and to keep the canopy photosynthesising into reserves. Where water is short it can be reduced, but not to the point that the trees drop their leaves early, which ends reserve accumulation for the season.', 'Irrigation', 'Irrigation after the fruit is off', 'confirmed'),
	(1023, 47, 81, 'The powdery mildew programme runs from tight cluster to terminal bud set, not to harvest. Once shoot growth has finished there is no new susceptible tissue being produced, so continuing to spray past that point protects nothing; the decision to stop is taken on the shoots, not on the calendar.', 'Disease management', 'Terminal bud set ends the mildew programme', 'confirmed'),
	(1024, 47, 81, 'Where pruning is done after harvest, plan it around the weather forecast rather than around labour availability. Most Nectria infections enter at leaf scars and pruning cuts, infection severity is driven by wet weather, and a wound becomes less susceptible the longer it has been open -- so it is the days immediately after the cut that decide the risk.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Keep post-harvest cuts out of wet weather', 'confirmed'),
	(1025, 47, 81, 'Where the leaves are brought down deliberately to speed up litter breakdown, the material is a metal-based class -- a copper chelate, or a zinc or iron salt -- applied in the post-harvest period. Two cautions come with it: going too early takes the canopy out while it is still working, and these materials carry a phytotoxicity risk at the rates that will actually defoliate.', 'Nutrient management', 'Timing an induced leaf drop', 'confirmed'),
	(1026, 47, 81, 'Partial leaf removal about two weeks after harvest is being trialled as a crop-load treatment rather than a tidying operation: the aim is to change the carbohydrate position the tree carries into dormancy and so improve fruitlet sensitivity in the spring chemical thinning window. It is under investigation, not established practice, and the cited trial is where the evidence stands.', 'Thinning', 'Autumn defoliation as a crop-load decision', 'confirmed'),
	(1027, 52, 81, 'For red and partially coloured varieties, reflective mulch is laid between the rows three or four weeks before harvest, where it lifts canopy light levels into the lower and inner fruit. Laid later it has no time to work, and the value it adds is in fruit that would otherwise be picked short of colour.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Reflective mulch goes down before colour is set', 'confirmed'),
	(1028, 52, 81, 'By this stage the levers left for colour are leaf plucking, late summer pruning and reflective mulch, and none of them will move a late variety far. Treat a block that is short of colour now as information for next season''s light and crop-load plan rather than as a problem to be sprayed out of.', 'Pruning & Training', 'What is still available for colour, and what is not', 'confirmed'),
	(1029, 52, 81, 'Draped hail netting is put on after petal fall and taken off immediately before harvest. It sits directly on the canopy, so leaving it on through picking obstructs the crew and risks rubbing the fruit it was there to protect. Overhead systems carried on posts and wires are not removed this way.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Draped netting comes off before picking', 'confirmed'),
	(1030, 52, 81, 'As harvest approaches, the pre-harvest interval decides what can still go on, and for some residual herbicides that interval runs to 150 days in pome fruit -- long enough that a spring application has to be planned against the harvest date rather than the weed. Check the interval on the formulation before the pass, not after.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Pre-harvest intervals close the spray window', 'confirmed'),
	(1031, 52, 81, 'The early varieties are packed before the late ones are picked, so their outturn data is available while there is still a season to act on. Analyse yield and packout block by block as it arrives, and let it set the picking and storage decisions for the fruit still on the tree.', 'Harvest management', 'Read the early packout while the late fruit is still hanging', 'confirmed'),
	(1032, 42, 81, 'Where the nets were opened over blossom for pollination, close them as soon as flowering finishes. The fruitlets become vulnerable to hail as soon as they are set, and crops have been lost because closing was left a few days too long. The pollination gain is only worth taking if the closing is planned with it.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Close the nets as soon as blossom is over', 'confirmed'),
	(1033, 42, 81, 'Where hail lands on newly set fruit, thinning becomes the tool for removing the damaged fruitlets. Decide the extra thinning from the damage assessment and against the effect on final fruit size, and take the damaged fruit off early where that is practical -- hail-damaged fruit is more likely to break down in storage, so carrying it to harvest spends size on fruit that will not pack.', 'Thinning', 'Hail in the fruit-set window changes the thinning plan', 'confirmed'),
	(1034, 42, 81, 'Fruit size is set first by cell number and only then by cell expansion, and the cell-division phase runs in the weeks straight after petal fall. Cytokinin applications made into warm conditions in this window act on cell division; the same application later in the season is acting on a cell number that is already fixed.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Cell division is where fruit size is decided', 'confirmed'),
	(1035, 42, 81, 'Assess shoots carrying mildew-infected terminal buds immediately after flowering, across a minimum of ten trees per block. Terminal buds stay open to infection for as long as the lateral keeps growing, and the infected buds carry the disease through to next spring, so this count is what next season''s mildew programme should be built on.', 'Disease management', 'Count the mildewed terminals now, plan next spring with it', 'confirmed'),
	(1036, 17, 81, 'The same timing is not equally useful against every disease. Rated across the season, green tip and white bud is the most effective window there is for scab, only moderately effective for fire blight, and the least effective of any timing for powdery mildew. Spend the pass on scab and plan the mildew and fire blight work for pink and bloom, where those ratings are highest.', 'Disease management', 'What the green-tip spray is actually worth', 'confirmed'),
	(1037, 17, 81, 'The group 9 fungicides lose effect as it warms, and the labels put a ceiling on it -- above about 70F they are not worth applying. Their place is the cool end of the prebloom programme, which is where bud break usually sits; a warm spell at this stage is a reason to choose a different group rather than to raise the rate.', 'Disease management', 'A temperature ceiling on the group 9 fungicides', 'confirmed'),
	(1038, 17, 81, 'Where no delayed-dormant spray was applied, the leafroller decision passes to monitoring: sample 100 fruit clusters per block between green tip and pink bud and treat on any larvae found. The threshold is that low because the larvae are feeding in the developing cluster, where a miss is fruit damage rather than foliage damage.', 'IPDM', 'Skipping the dormant spray moves the work to sampling', 'confirmed'),
	(1039, 17, 81, 'Do not put oil on within 24 to 48 hours of freezing temperatures, or while it stays below about 35F after a freeze. Oil works by covering and suffocating, and the same film on tissue that is freezing or thawing causes injury. Bud break is exactly when both the oil window and the frost risk are open at once.', 'IPDM', 'Oil and frost do not mix', 'confirmed'),
	(1040, 59, 81, 'The branching paint has to be on before the buds begin to swell and before any shoot emerges. Once green tissue is showing, that option has closed for the season and the decision moves to a sprayed cytokinin-type regulator instead.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Branching paint is already too late here', 'confirmed'),
	(1041, 59, 81, 'The sprayed branching treatment goes on once the buds have broken and leaf tissue is showing on the one-year-old wood, and it works best into warm conditions. A cytokinin-only product is mixed without a surfactant; the cytokinin-plus-gibberellin products take a non-ionic one. Applying before there is tissue to absorb it wastes the pass.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Spray branching once there is tissue to take it up', 'confirmed'),
	(1042, 61, 81, 'Prohexadione-calcium formulations do not share a start timing. One carries a pink-bud recommendation; another still says to begin at one to three inches of shoot growth. Because the programme is built by repeating from its first application, starting on the wrong one shifts every spray after it -- so the timing comes off the label of the formulation actually being used.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Read the start timing off the formulation, not the active', 'confirmed'),
	(1043, 25, 81, 'Put the monitoring out at bud burst, not when damage appears: yellow sticky traps through the block for thrips, carried to shuck fall, and pheromone traps read weekly for the leafroller flight. Both are there to give a first date and an activity level, and a trap hung after the flight has begun cannot give either.', 'IPDM', 'Bud burst is when the trap lines start', 'confirmed'),
	(1044, 65, 81, 'After a frost, cut and assess the king bloom and the side blooms as separate populations. The king is the most developed flower in the cluster and is the more susceptible at every stage of spring development, so a cluster can read as damaged while the side blooms are still viable -- and the crop that is left is a side-bloom crop, which sets later and smaller.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Assess frost damage on the king and the side blooms separately', 'confirmed'),
	(1045, 65, 81, 'Where bloom is prolonged rather than compressed, the cytokinin-plus-gibberellin treatment is split into two half-rate passes instead of one: the first from early king bloom to early petal fall on the side blooms, the second three to twenty-one days later when the rest of the canopy has come into flower. A single pass on a drawn-out bloom reaches only part of the crop.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Split the application when bloom draws out', 'confirmed'),
	(1046, 16, 81, 'The annual nitrogen is split into two equal doses rather than applied once: the first goes on at the swollen-to-breaking bud stage together with the potassium, the second immediately after bloom. Both are broadcast over the herbicide strip along the tree row, which is where the feeding roots are, rather than over the mown sod between rows.', 'Fertilization', 'Split the nitrogen, and put the first half on now', 'confirmed'),
	(1047, 60, 81, 'Once the leaves are fully expanded the canopy has a measurable volume, and the spray should be set against it rather than against a fixed rate per hectare. At this stage a conventional high-volume pass gives excessive coverage and a high contamination risk, while canopy-adjusted treatments at a quarter of the volume and dose gave equivalent coverage with good uniformity.', 'IPDM', 'Set the spray volume from the canopy, not the hectare', 'confirmed'),
	(1048, 63, 81, 'Begin the powdery mildew fungicide programme at the tight cluster bud stage and carry it until the new shoots stop growing, or about the fourth cover spray. Both ends of that window are set by the shoots rather than by the calendar, because it is the growing tip that is susceptible.', 'Disease management', 'The mildew programme starts at tight cluster and ends when shoots do', 'confirmed'),
	(1049, 63, 81, 'Count flower clusters per tree at green bud. The count taken before the flowers open is the one that sets the crop-load target and the thinning plan, and it is the last point at which the number can be established without the bloom itself getting in the way.', 'Thinning', 'Count the clusters before the flowers open', 'confirmed'),
	(1050, 66, 81, 'The pre-harvest calcium programme runs from the T-stage to harvest, and on a bitter-pit-prone cultivar that means repeated applications across the whole window rather than a single spray before picking. Cultivars differ in how many they need, so the number is decided per block on its disorder history.', 'Nutrient management', 'T-stage opens the pre-harvest calcium run', 'confirmed'),
	(1051, 66, 81, 'Assess scab on leaves and fruit around this stage, sampling ten leaves from each of twenty shoots and a hundred fruit per block, taken from both sides of the tree. Scoring incidence here, once the fruit is big enough to show symptoms, is what tells you whether the primary programme worked while there is still a season left to respond in.', 'Disease management', 'Take the scab count at T-stage', 'confirmed'),
	(150, 64, 81, 'Apply nutrients based on spring tissue analysis to support bloom and early fruit development.', 'Nutrient management', 'Pink stage nutrient application', 'confirmed'),
	(734, 64, 81, 'Removing flowers at pink on young non-bearing trees is the most effective fire blight measure available for them: in trial it took infection to zero, against 71 to 77 strikes per 100 clusters where the flowers were left on.', 'Disease management', 'Flower removal on young trees', 'confirmed'),
	(1053, 6, NULL, 'Weed control before and just after planting is treated as its own problem, separate from mature-orchard floor management. The guidance covers clearing persistent perennials and tilling before trees go in, then holding the tree row clean through the establishment months when young trees are most easily injured by spray contact and by herbicide reaching roots through unsettled soil. A large set of entries names specific tree-row herbicide products with their use restrictions, tank-mix partners and dormant or pre-emergence timings, while soil organic matter and a soil bioassay for residues are given as checks before soil-applied materials. Rates and tree-age restrictions stay with the labels and the source guides.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Pre-plant and first-season weed control', 'confirmed'),
	(1054, 6, NULL, 'Almost all the work described here happens before a tree goes in the ground. Soil testing well ahead of planting drives liming, pH correction and any phosphorus and potassium base dressings, because these are far easier to place while the ground is still open. Entries also cover physical preparation, including grading the subsoil before respreading topsoil, checking drainage, and building organic matter, along with the caution against fertilising at planting itself. Target pH values and a drainage test method are given in the sources.', 'Nutrient management', 'Pre-plant soil correction and amendment', 'confirmed'),
	(1055, 6, NULL, 'Pollinizer choice comes down to overlap: the pollinating variety has to be shedding viable pollen while the main variety is receptive, judged from several years of local flowering records rather than one season. Entries set a minimum overlap period, describe planting ratios and row layouts for crabapple pollinizers, and recommend more than one pollinizer so the main variety is bracketed on both sides of its bloom. Pollinizers are treated as a crop in their own right, needing pruning, water and nutrition. Pollen performance at low temperature is flagged as a selection criterion for cool districts.', 'Pollination', 'Matching pollinizer to main variety', 'confirmed'),
	(1056, 6, NULL, 'Site selection is framed as a decision with costs attached rather than a survey exercise, with the cost of fixing a site counted into where to plant in the first place. Drainage is the recurring disqualifier, and entries cover digging a deep profile pit, tile draining wet ground, and raised terraces where drainage cannot be corrected. Frost behaviour shapes much of the rest: avoid low ground, match variety hardiness and bloom timing to the site, and keep the slope workable for machinery. Previous land use and season length also narrow variety choice, and contaminated ground is treated as a reason to bring in clean soil.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Choosing and improving an orchard site', 'confirmed'),
	(1057, 50, NULL, 'White rot is the subject, and the central point is timing: fruit can be infected well before anything shows, with decay developing only as the fruit approaches maturity. Because symptoms surface close to harvest, control decisions have to be made long before the problem is visible. Descriptions cover lesion appearance and how it differs on red- and yellow-skinned cultivars, the soft watery texture that separates it from black rot, the lesion shape in cross-section against bitter rot, and the mummified fruit and cankers that carry inoculum into the next season. Cultivar susceptibility differences and the behaviour of latent infections in cold storage are also recorded.', 'Disease management', 'White rot on maturing fruit', 'confirmed'),
	(1058, 34, NULL, 'Thinning at and just after bloom is covered from two directions. Chemical blossom thinning appears as a programme of one or two sprays applied at or close to full bloom, with the material and concentration varying by situation and with cultivar-specific cautions about when treatment is justified at all. Hand thinning follows, with a working window running from bloom to a few weeks after and a fruit-size cue for the best moment within it. Concentrations and the cultivar notes sit in the source guides.', 'Thinning', 'Blossom thinning and hand thinning', 'confirmed'),
	(1059, 64, NULL, 'Pink is a fixed reference point in the spray calendar, and most entries here anchor an application to it. Monitoring starts at the same time: pheromone traps for San Jose scale males go up at pink, and a cumulative capture count for tarnished plant bug drives an action decision by late pink. First-generation spotted tentiform leafminer is treated at pink, or split between pink and petal fall. Thresholds and product choices are in the linked production guides.', 'IPDM', 'Pink-stage sprays and monitoring', 'confirmed'),
	(1060, 6, NULL, 'Trouble that arrives with the site is the theme. Replant disease gets most of the attention, with stunting, leaf yellowing, discoloured feeder roots and in bad cases tree death in the first few years where apples follow apples, together with a frank account of why replacing the soil in the planting hole disappoints over time even where it lifts first-year growth. Other entries flag dagger nematodes as widespread and hard to eradicate, cotton root rot as severe on alkaline soils, and a virus for which removal is the only stated option. Regional fire blight pressure is noted as increasing.', 'Disease management', 'Replant disease and soilborne problems', 'confirmed'),
	(1062, 53, NULL, 'Everything between the picking bin and the storage room sits here: cleaning and ranking rooms by intended storage length, getting warm fruit into cold storage quickly, checking that cooling capacity matches the volume coming in, and managing airflow, humidity and atmosphere once the room is closed. Harvest maturity decides how long fruit can be held, and blocks with doubtful storage potential are flagged for shorter terms and closer monitoring. Post-harvest treatments and their application deadlines are recorded, together with atmosphere limits that apply to treated fruit and staged temperature regimes. The source manuals carry the concentrations, temperatures and day counts.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Cooling and controlled-atmosphere storage setup', 'confirmed'),
	(1063, 30, NULL, 'Blossom thinning decisions start with whether to thin at all, with frost damage and frost forecast the gate. Where it goes ahead, the guidance sets spray concentration and water volume according to air temperature and humidity, since drying speed changes how hard the product works, and describes a follow-up spray a set interval later aimed at axillary blossom on one-year-old wood. Cultivar cautions, wetter additions, interactions with growth regulators and products that must not be applied during bloom are all recorded, as is mechanical string thinning as a non-chemical option. Rates and timings belong in the source guidance rather than memory.', 'Thinning', 'Chemical blossom thinning at bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(1064, 6, NULL, 'Soil problems are settled before trees go in. A soil assay count drives the treatment decision, and per-species action thresholds are recorded for the plant-parasitic nematodes found in apple soils, with some species noted as not economic to treat. Where fumigation is warranted, the cell lists the available products, injection or broadcast methods, the seasons in which fumigation works, and the germination test repeated at intervals to set a safe planting date. Old orchard ground being replanted gets particular attention, and the rest of the guidance is about starting clean: certified disease-free and virus-free nursery stock, removing infected trees, and rootstock choice as a fire blight defence.', 'Disease management', 'Pre-plant soil treatment and clean stock', 'confirmed'),
	(715, 6, 81, 'Where crabapples carry the pollination, a common arrangement is one between every third tree in every third row. Crabapples can also be interplanted into an existing block that was laid out on the older solid-row pattern, without pulling anything out.', 'Pollination', 'Crabapple placement', 'confirmed'),
	(1065, 30, NULL, 'Bloom is the critical infection window and fire blight dominates the guidance. Risk is judged from daily average temperature through pink to petal fall and from accumulated warm degree hours starting at first open flower, with named prediction models used to call the spray. Antibiotic and copper applications are directed at open blossom and timed ahead of an expected wetting event, repeated as the bloom period extends, and there are entries on combining treatments and on orchards with a history of the disease. Running alongside are scab and powdery mildew monitoring, fungicide programmes that start at bloom and continue through the cover sprays, a growth regulator applied from bloom, and a caution against irrigating during bloom.', 'Disease management', 'Fire blight and fungicides through bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(1066, 41, NULL, 'Fertiliser in this stage is applied as split dressings rather than one application, with nitrogen, phosphate and potash each going on at flowering and again at fruit expansion. The entries come from a single irrigation and fertiliser trial and record only the timing pattern; application rates are not what this cell carries. Go to the source for the treatment levels and the yield and water-use-efficiency results that sit behind the schedule.', 'Nutrient management', 'Split fertiliser dressings during fruit growth', 'confirmed'),
	(1067, 52, NULL, 'Fungal pathogens are the main cause of post-harvest loss, and most infection traces back to what happens during picking and handling. Wounds, cuts, cracks in the cuticle and possibly lenticels are the entry routes, so bruised and damaged fruit is the fruit that decays in storage. Risk rises with the spore load carried in the orchard at harvest, with fruit picked riper than usual, with wet harvest conditions, and with spore concentration building up in hydro-handling water. Some infections are inconspicuous at harvest and only declare themselves later, and biocontrol products will not eradicate an infection already established, although one fungicide is recorded as still effective some hours after infection occurs.', 'Disease management', 'Harvest injuries and storage rot risk', 'confirmed'),
	(1068, 56, NULL, 'Autumn leaf fall is the treatment window for both canker and next season''s scab inoculum. Copper and tebuconazole sprays are timed against how far leaf fall has progressed, and are aimed at orchards with moderate to high canker incidence, with a further fungicide applied after pruning. Cutting overwintering scab inoculum is a sanitation job: blow leaves out of the row middles and shred them, mulch prunings, and time a urea application just before leaves drop. The mechanical and urea or biological treatments are repeated in early spring, a few weeks ahead of bud break.', 'Disease management', 'Leaf-fall canker and scab sanitation', 'confirmed'),
	(1069, 7, NULL, 'The aim in the first few seasons is a dominant central leader carrying wide-angled branches, reached with as little cutting as possible. Competing shoots below the leader are rubbed off while still short, or removed later if missed, narrow-crotched shoots go, and low branches near the ground are not retained. Remaining limbs are bent or tied out towards a flatter angle, and vigorous trees need this repeated annually until they start to carry a crop, before branches get too thick to move. Dwarf trees are tied to their support early, and the block needs intensive attention through these years, including keeping young trees free of weed competition and other stress.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Training young trees in early years', 'confirmed'),
	(1070, 15, NULL, 'Zinc is supplied around bud development, early enough in the season to work before root uptake takes over. Zinc sulphate is the form used at the silver-tip to green-tip stages, applied annually, with chelated formulations kept for in-season sprays once fruit are on the tree. Foliar analysis showing low zinc is the stated trigger for the spray, and rates appear in the source for both dilute airblast and per-area application. An entry on post-harvest basal fertiliser rounds out the cell.', 'Nutrient management', 'Dormant and early-season zinc sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(1071, 10, NULL, 'The dormant season is when zinc and copper are applied to the tree as foliar sprays. Zinc sulphate goes on in the late-dormant window ahead of the oil spray, and there is an explicit caution against substituting a chelated zinc product at that timing, since chelated forms are for later in the season. Both nutrients can also be applied after harvest while leaves are still green and functioning, which gives a second chance if the dormant application was missed. Copper may additionally be applied to the soil at any time of year.', 'Nutrient management', 'Dormant zinc and copper applications', 'confirmed'),
	(1073, 41, NULL, 'Summer pest work is timed off models and traps rather than the calendar alone. Pheromone traps combined with a degree-day development model are used to hit small larvae of a leafroller-type pest, starting after the thinning period and again in late summer, while apple maggot sprays fall around mid-summer and pear slug monitoring continues all season with extra attention late. Routine summer jobs double as scouting opportunities, with weed assessment done during hand thinning. One spray-application restriction limits spraying to the earlier part of the morning in summer.', 'IPDM', 'Midsummer pest monitoring and sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(1074, 59, NULL, 'These are the first fungicide applications of the season. Copper goes on before bud burst in orchards carrying canker risk, followed by a dithianon-based spray at bud burst and mouse ear. Scab protection starts when a degree-day ascospore maturity model reports mature spores, and defaults to bud break or green tip where no model output is available. The same model, combined with orchard monitoring and rainfall, also decides when the primary season is over and spraying can be wound back.', 'Disease management', 'Bud burst scab and canker sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(1075, 53, NULL, 'Postharvest decay is described here as mainly fungal, with blue mould ranked the most common and destructive. These fungi are largely wound pathogens, entering through cuts, stem punctures and bruises, and infection usually traces back to spores picked up in the orchard even though symptoms only appear once fruit is in store. Cold slows their growth rather than stopping it, growth resumes when fruit is warmed, and CA gas levels have little direct effect on the fungi themselves. Several entries deal with how inoculum carries over on field bins and in packhouses and cold rooms.', 'Disease management', 'Storage rots and how they start', 'confirmed'),
	(716, 6, 81, 'Check the specific pairing rather than assuming a crabapple will do. Manchurian crabapple is unsuitable for some modern cultivars on both counts that matter -- around 50 per cent pollen incompatibility, and flowering too early to overlap -- and some cultivars need roughly ten per cent more pollinizer trees than the standard layout provides.', 'Pollination', 'Check compatibility for the cultivar', 'confirmed'),
	(1076, 50, NULL, 'This cell is about holding fruit on the tree through to picking. The materials recorded are the ethylene inhibitor AVG (ReTain), the auxin NAA, and sprayable 1-MCP (Harvista), used alone or in combination, with timings expressed as weeks before anticipated harvest. Programs differ in placement: some begin about a month out, others are placed in the final week or two, and NAA is sometimes tank-partnered with or sequenced after AVG. Harvest colour standards for the retail market also appear. Rates and label conditions are in the source documents.', 'Harvest management', 'Preharvest fruit drop control', 'confirmed'),
	(1077, 53, NULL, 'Decay control is framed as a continuous program that starts in the orchard and carries through packing and storage rather than a single treatment. Recorded practices include sanitation of bins, flumes and cold rooms, chlorine treatment of fruit soon after harvest, reducing the spore load before fruit is hydro-handled, careful handling to avoid wounds, rapid cooling, and holding at recommended temperatures. Registered postharvest fungicides appear with their timing at harvest or at packing, as does postharvest heating, alone or with ULO storage, as a non-fungicide alternative. Registration differs by country, so check current label and industry guidance before applying anything.', 'Disease management', 'Controlling decay in storage', 'confirmed'),
	(1078, 7, NULL, 'Herbicide options for the strip beneath young trees are listed here, covering residual and postemergence materials, useful tank-mix partners, and the season or temperature window each is applied in. A recurring constraint is orchard age: several products cannot be used, or cannot be used at full rate, until trees have been in the ground a stated number of years. Non-chemical measures appear alongside, including cultivation out to a set distance from the trunk and wrapping young trunks for protection. Registered products and their restrictions vary by region, so work from the current label.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Tree-row weed control, young trees', 'confirmed'),
	(1079, 42, NULL, 'Two threads run through this cell. The first is tissue testing: fruitlet samples taken early in the season and again a few weeks before harvest, with results read against published optimum values and nutrient ratios such as potassium, nitrogen and magnesium relative to calcium, then used to adjust the following season''s program. The second is in-season correction, including boron applied around bud break and flowering and again during rapid fruit growth, and repeated nitrate-form calcium and potassium sprays starting a few weeks after full bloom. The optimum values held are tied to a specific cultivar, so check they apply to yours.', 'Nutrient management', 'Fruitlet analysis and foliar nutrition', 'confirmed'),
	(1080, 6, NULL, 'Layout is the main subject. Solid single-cultivar blocks are advised against; instead pollinizers are distributed so that no tree sits beyond a stated distance from a pollen source, whether as individual trees on a regular grid, whole interspersed rows, or compatible limbs grafted into existing trees and clearly marked. Minimum pollinizer share and maximum spacing figures are given, and a triploid variety needs two diploid partners rather than one. Where bloom weather works against cross-pollination, supplementary pollen through hive inserts or direct application is described as a fallback.', 'Pollination', 'Placing pollinizers in the block', 'confirmed'),
	(1081, 6, NULL, 'Decisions taken in the first season are collected here. Entries cover what to take off at planting, including low branches below a set height, dead and broken wood, feathers too large relative to the leader, and branches with narrow crotch angles, and what to keep, since retaining feathers brings the tree into crop sooner. Whether the tree is headed depends on the system: an unbranched whip is cut back to force branching, while a feathered tree in a high-density planting is generally left unheaded. Branch tying to a pendant position is also done at planting, and spur-type cultivars are left with more branches because they branch sparsely.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Cuts made at planting', 'confirmed'),
	(1082, 30, NULL, 'Weed competition matters most from around bloom until roughly a month after petal fall, and the tree-row program is aimed at that window. A range of residual and postemergence herbicides is listed with mixing, surfactant and application notes, and several carry an explicit instruction not to apply during bloom. Pollinator safety shapes the timing as well: flowering ground cover is mown off or removed before and during bloom, and mowing blooming cover is avoided from the week before bloom until petal fall. Product availability and restrictions are region-specific.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Weed control through bloom period', 'confirmed'),
	(1083, 50, NULL, 'Two activities run through the preharvest period. The first is structured scouting: terminals are examined around each sample tree along with a fixed number of fruit, lesion counts are recorded, and even a very low level of infected fruit per tree is treated as damaging for the fresh market. The second is fungicide cover, with named preharvest products. For white rot the guidance splits by disease phase, combining fungicides with sanitation against the fruit rot phase while managing cankers on stressed or winter-injured trees through annual pruning and balanced fertility based on soil and leaf analysis.', 'Disease management', 'Preharvest scab and rot checks', 'confirmed'),
	(1084, 10, NULL, 'Chill accumulation is to be tracked with the Dynamic Model in chill portions, calculated from hourly temperatures logged in the orchard itself rather than taken from a regional weather station. Where chill is marginal, as at low latitudes, dormancy break may need to be assisted. Two late-summer practices are listed as things to avoid after a stated cut-off date: pruning and fertiliser application.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Measuring winter chill accumulation', 'confirmed'),
	(1085, 10, NULL, 'The early-season calendar is set out by bud stage rather than by date. Oil goes on at dormant or delayed dormant before pink, copper at delayed dormant, followed by the green tip, prepink and pink sprays covering both insects and diseases. Individual pests are pegged to stages, with the first leafminer generation treated at pink or at pink and petal fall, while leafhopper in young plantings is handled later, through the summer months as numbers build.', 'IPDM', 'Dormant and early-season sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(1086, 64, NULL, 'Between tight cluster and pink the knowledge base holds a foliar programme built around boron, zinc and low-biuret urea, often applied together as a single cocktail. Whether boron is needed at this timing depends on what was applied the previous autumn, and a repeat at pink or full bloom is described along with a caution against overdoing bloom-time boron. Foliar sprays are treated as a supplement to soil application rather than a substitute for it, and leaf analysis decides whether a second combined application is warranted. Also recorded: the zinc source to avoid when frost or oil sprays are close, and the need to check tank compatibility on the label.', 'Nutrient management', 'Pre-bloom foliar boron and zinc', 'confirmed'),
	(662, 6, 81, 'Where flowering crab apples are the pollenizer, place them about every 15 m in each row and offset them between adjacent rows, and choose cultivars resistant or immune to fire blight -- a susceptible pollenizer becomes the inoculum source that reaches every tree it was planted to serve.', 'Pollination', 'Crab apple pollenizer spacing', 'confirmed'),
	(1087, 6, NULL, 'Two decisions run through this cell: which variety supplies the pollen, and where it goes in the block. Compatibility rules are explicit, requiring fully compatible diploid pollen sources, ruling triploids out as donors, naming partial-compatibility pairings to avoid, and warning against relying on a self-fertile clone to pollinate others. Layout options range from solid pollinizer rows and regular tree-and-row spacings to interplanted crabapples, with pruning noted so pollinizers do not crowd the commercial cultivar. Cross-pollination is recommended in every planting regardless of stated self-fruitfulness, and hand-pollination steps are recorded for small-scale or breeding work.', 'Pollination', 'Pollinizer choice and orchard layout', 'confirmed');
INSERT INTO "public"."operation" VALUES
	(1088, 8, NULL, 'This is a herbicide selection cell for the tree row, drawn from a commercial production guide. Each entry names a product and formulation and sets out where it fits: preemergence residual versus postemergence burndown, which weed groups it covers, and which tank-mix partners broaden the spectrum, particularly for annual grasses or non-selective knockdown. Constraints run through consistently, including dormant or winter-only windows, bloom exclusions, minimum tree age, soil type limits, surfactant or crop oil requirements, and temperature or rainfall conditions around application. Registration differs by region and labels change, so treat this as an orientation to the available classes and confirm against the current label.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Tree-row herbicide options', 'confirmed'),
	(1089, 24, NULL, 'Pre-bloom micronutrient work concentrates on boron and zinc applied foliar between tight cluster and pink. Boron guidance separates maintenance sprays from corrective ones where last season''s status or the absence of an autumn application suggests a shortfall, and states plainly that foliar sprays should not substitute for soil application every year. Zinc appears as chelate and oxide formulations intended to supplement rather than replace sulphate treatment, with a caution about applying sulphate near frost events or oil sprays. Combined nitrogen, boron and zinc tank mixes are covered, along with using low-biuret urea to avoid leaf damage and checking mineral-pesticide compatibility before mixing.', 'Nutrient management', 'Pre-bloom boron and zinc sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(1092, 52, NULL, 'The framing here is that storage decay control starts in the orchard rather than at the packhouse door. Fungicide programmes running from early season or petal fall through to harvest on a regular interval are recorded, with particular weight on the later cover sprays closest to picking. At harvest the emphasis shifts to handling: avoiding wounds, cooling quickly, holding fruit at the recommended temperature, and keeping over-ripe fruit out of storage. Postharvest fungicide use appears in two forms, as a drench combined with an antioxidant treatment straight after harvest and as a line spray or wax application at packing.', 'Disease management', 'Decay control from orchard to pack', 'confirmed'),
	(1093, 6, NULL, 'At the design stage the questions are how many pollinizer trees to plant and how to distribute them. Several arrangements are recorded, from whole trees placed at a regular tree-and-row interval, to crabapples set between trees within the row, to grafting pollinizer limbs into main-cultivar trees so no planting position is given up. Using more than one crabapple cultivar, chosen so their bloom periods bracket the main variety, is preferred over relying on a single one. Pollinizers are recommended on dwarfing rootstocks, and growers are advised to settle the ratio with a local advisor and trial any new cultivar in a small block before planting it at scale.', 'Pollination', 'Planting pollinizers in new orchards', 'confirmed'),
	(1094, 35, NULL, 'Magnesium leads this cell, applied as Epsom salts or a proprietary product once leaves are well developed or at petal fall, and repeated annually as a preventative rather than held back until deficiency shows. Foliar urea for bearing trees runs from petal fall into early summer, with more than one application sometimes needed. Both are maintenance timings, not rescue treatments. The same few recommendations repeat across the sources, so the coverage is narrow but consistent.', 'Nutrient management', 'Petal-fall magnesium and urea sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(1095, 35, NULL, 'Spray scheduling across bloom and petal fall is the focus of this cell. Protectant applications are placed at blossom and petal fall when fruit is most susceptible, with several named product options, and a regular-interval programme then continues from petal fall through harvest. Early-season mildew sprays are timed from tight cluster to petal fall. Fire blight guidance keys off average daily temperature during pink through petal fall, both to judge how severe the risk is and to decide whether a full streptomycin programme is warranted.', 'Disease management', 'Bloom to petal-fall spray timing', 'confirmed'),
	(1096, 30, NULL, 'Frost management at this stage splits into avoidance, physical protection and assessment. Avoidance covers choosing later-flowering cultivars and deliberately delaying bloom, including evaporative cooling with sprinklers run through a defined window between rest break and bloom once air temperature passes a stated point. Physical protection entries are simpler, covering trees with various materials and painting bark white. For assessment, cutting flowers open a few days after a freeze is described, with the colour of the interior separating surviving flowers from killed ones.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Frost avoidance and damage checks', 'confirmed'),
	(1097, 52, NULL, 'Entries here cover how a block is judged ready and what happens to the fruit once it is off. Maturity release criteria are held cultivar by cultivar, with starch, firmness, soluble solids and acid each carrying release and over-mature figures in the source tables. A second group deals with the handling chain: careful picking and bin transfer, roads and equipment prepared before harvest starts, and how quickly fruit must move into cold storage, controlled atmosphere or an ethylene-inhibitor treatment. Cultivar-specific cautions about picking too early or letting a block get away also sit in this cell.', 'Harvest management', 'Picking decisions and post-harvest handling', 'confirmed'),
	(1098, 35, NULL, 'Post-bloom thinning is organised by fruitlet size rather than by calendar date. The metamitron product has an application window opening at petal fall and closing in the high teens to about 20 mm, with a narrower band where it works best, and with rates held per acre and per hectare in the source. Its use as a standalone thinner and the surfactant added to slow drying are both recorded, as is the parallel start of the benzyladenine thinners at petal fall or small king fruit. A growth regulator applied at late bloom or petal fall for shoot control shares the same window.', 'Thinning', 'Post-bloom chemical thinning window', 'confirmed'),
	(1099, 30, NULL, 'The rule running through this cell is not to spray insecticide over open blossom, including where drift could reach a neighbour''s bloom. Products are sorted into bee-toxicity classes, from those that must never go on a blooming crop through to those safe at any time, with the middle classes restricted to late evening or early morning and qualified by high temperature. Where a hazardous product cannot be avoided, keeping bees off for the residue period is the stated condition. Coordination is treated as part of the job: notify neighbouring growers and their beekeepers before spraying, and agree hive placement and removal dates between orchards.', 'IPDM', 'Protecting bees during bloom sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(1100, 30, NULL, 'Around bloom the guidance separates three nutrients by timing. Nitrogen goes on well ahead of bloom at a stated amount of actual N per tree, is withheld from fertigation in the weeks either side of bloom, then resumes daily as calcium nitrate for a set period after full bloom, with foliar urea at bloom listed separately. Boron appears at bud break and bloom as a foliar or drip application, with soil application possible at any time and an autumn foliar option while leaves are still green and active. Foliar calcium starts at pink or early bloom and carries on through the early cover sprays.', 'Nutrient management', 'Bloom-period nitrogen, boron and calcium', 'confirmed'),
	(1101, 59, NULL, 'What the grower sees at bud break is the theme. Trees with Phytophthora infection show delayed bud break, leaf discoloration and twig dieback as the first spring signs, and apple union necrosis and decline produces a similar late, weak push with small, sparse, dull pale-green leaves. Both present as a tree that is behind and thin rather than as an obvious lesion, so the early-season walk is where they get picked up. One canker entry notes a copper material that gives prolonged protection but is phytotoxic and restricted to before bud burst.', 'Disease management', 'Spring symptoms of root and union disease', 'confirmed'),
	(1102, 65, NULL, 'Two products define this cell. The ethylene-generating thinner is labelled from king bloom but recorded as having more effect on larger fruitlets, applied with a non-ionic surfactant, with dilute and per-area rates held in the source. The other is a gibberellin-plus-cytokinin material applied as king blossoms open to improve fruit shape.', 'Thinning', 'Late thinning and fruit-shape sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(1103, 30, NULL, 'Timing is what this cell is about. Pruning sits in late winter or the few weeks before bloom and can run into bloom itself, bud notching falls within a few days either side of full bloom, and girdling vigorous trees belongs in the fortnight after bloom begins. One entry marks a window to avoid rather than to use, since the NAA sucker treatment is kept out of the period from bloom to four weeks after. The purpose behind the cuts is recorded too: thinning cuts open the canopy so light reaches fruitful spurs throughout the tree.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Pruning and girdling around bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(1105, 6, NULL, 'Spacing recommendations here are organised by rootstock and matched to variety vigour and soil strength, so the same rootstock carries more than one spacing depending on the site. The planting instructions cover hole preparation, keeping roots unpruned and fitting the hole to them, setting graft union height so dwarfing is retained, firming the backfill and watering in. Support is treated as part of the design decision rather than an afterthought, with staking or trellis specified for fully dwarf trees from planting onwards. Site preparation is expected to be done the season before planting, leaving only row marking for planting time.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Tree spacing and planting practice', 'confirmed'),
	(1106, 42, NULL, 'Thinning guidance at this stage is built around timing windows rather than fixed recipes: chemical thinners are tied to days after full bloom or to fruitlet diameter, and hand thinning has a cut-off size beyond which it stops working. Weather in the post-bloom period changes both the response and where in the window to spray, so the entries pair product timing with temperature and light conditions. Before any hand thinning, a representative sample of trees is counted to decide whether it is warranted at all. Product names, rates, surfactant requirements and the diameter and temperature figures are recorded and should be read from the source before spraying.', 'Thinning', 'Post-bloom and hand thinning', 'confirmed'),
	(1107, 30, NULL, 'Fire blight dominates the observations for this stage: open flowers are the main infection site, bacteria enter through the nectarthodes after being washed off the stigmas, and pollinating insects spread the pathogen rapidly between blossoms once the first infections start. Wetting events after enough warm weather has accumulated are described as the trigger, and late or rat-tail bloom is singled out as a persistent source of severe damage. Other entries cover rain-driven spore release in Marssonina blotch, bitter rot under prolonged wetness, collar rot following cool wet pre-bloom seasons, and the russeting risk that copper products carry once bloom begins. Latent fruit infection from late-bloom events may only show up in cold store.', 'Disease management', 'Bloom-time infection pathways', 'confirmed'),
	(1108, 30, NULL, 'Pollination is framed here as something to plan rather than leave to chance. The actions cover hive stocking rates scaled to orchard density and bloom load, when to bring colonies in relative to king bloom, how far to move them from earlier stone fruit, and how to keep competing weed bloom out of the block. Backup measures are also recorded for poor seasons: cut flowering bouquets from a compatible block, hand pollination, stored pollen, and hive-entrance pollen dispensers. Checking that cultivar bloom periods overlap, testing pollen viability after frost, counting bee activity per tree, and coordinating spray plans with beekeepers round out the set.', 'Pollination', 'Managing bees and pollen at bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(1109, 10, NULL, 'Dormant work in this section is sequenced by tree age, from first-year heading of the leader through scaffold selection in the first dormant season to leader securing and branch removal in the second. Whether a branch or scaffold comes out is decided by its diameter relative to the leader or central axis, and large removals are made with a bevel cut. Also recorded: clearing low branches, dealing with root suckers and their regrowth, tying down weeping limbs, and keeping a conical shape for light penetration. The diameter ratios and leader heights are specific numbers in the sources, and the guidance expects the leader treatment to be reconsidered each dormant season on that season''s vigour.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Dormant pruning and leader training', 'confirmed'),
	(1110, 35, NULL, 'Almost everything recorded for this cell anchors pest management actions to petal fall, when the flower petals drop from the blossom, followed by the first cover spray. Two specific pests are named with their own timing: first-generation spotted tentiform leafminer is targeted at pink or petal fall, and potato leafhopper is treated in young plantings across the summer months while populations build. The material here is timing scaffolding rather than product selection, and the entries are heavily repetitive. Consult the linked production guides for which materials fit each of these slots.', 'IPDM', 'Petal fall and first cover sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(1111, 35, NULL, 'Petal fall marks a turning point for several diseases, and the observations here map that shift. Apple scab lesions on leaves often only become obvious after petal fall, black rot ascospore release peaks around the same period, and fruit susceptibility to Neonectria is highest through blossom and petal fall before declining over summer. Fire blight''s window is characterised in two ways: open flowers remain susceptible until petal fall, while the period from petal fall to terminal bud set is when limb and tree losses are greatest. Additional entries describe wetness requirements for quince rust and sooty blotch, the later-starting Brooks fruit spot cycle, and fungicide group effectiveness against bitter rot.', 'Disease management', 'Disease pressure around petal fall', 'confirmed'),
	(1112, 52, NULL, 'Fertigation programmes recorded here are defined by calcium nitrate applied at a set annual rate and split in different proportions between pre-harvest and post-harvest timing, including all-post-harvest and zero-nitrogen comparisons. Potassium is applied pre-harvest as either sulphate or nitrate, by foliar spray or through the fertigation line, and foliar mixtures combining potassium and calcium nitrate are applied as repeat sprays. On the assessment side, fruit mineral analysis starts shortly before the first expected pick and the resulting mineral contents and ratios are used to rank blocks by storage potential. A consultant or service provider is recommended for that ranking.', 'Nutrient management', 'Split nitrogen and pre-harvest potassium', 'confirmed'),
	(1113, 10, NULL, 'Floor management options here split between the system and the chemistry. At system level the choices are a herbicide or cultivated strip in the tree row with seeded or naturally established alleyways, cultivation rotated with a dormant-season cover crop, or repeated post-emergence sprays leaving a sparse ground cover over winter. The herbicide entries list residual pre-emergence products for the tree row, most with conditions attached: application windows, temperature limits, rainfall needed for activation, and tank-mix partners that extend control to annual grasses or add a non-selective knockdown. Product labels govern rates, soil-type restrictions and whether a material is cleared for young plantings.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Dormant tree-row weed control', 'confirmed'),
	(1114, 10, NULL, 'Winter is when fire blight cankers are dealt with, because the bacteria are inactive in the tree. Actions include cutting limbs well below the visible canker margin, scraping infections from trunks and major limbs and dressing the wound, and disinfecting tools between cuts. Alongside the cutting work, trees carrying heavy dead wood and canker loads are located and recorded so intensive pruning can be scheduled, and bark lesions are counted on a set number of shoots per tree as a monitoring baseline. Dormant and summer pruning to open the canopy is also listed, while scab monitoring is explicitly not required during dormancy.', 'Disease management', 'Dormant canker removal and mapping', 'confirmed'),
	(1116, 50, NULL, 'Several preharvest growth regulator treatments are recorded for this stage, aimed at different outcomes: ripening and drop control on one hand, red colour development on the other. Ethylene-acting materials and ethylene inhibitors both appear, applied in the weeks before anticipated harvest. Colour enhancement has its own timing rules, including a window measured back from expected harvest, application in the cooler parts of the day, and a maturity trigger read off a DA meter rather than a calendar date. Product choice and rates are cultivar-specific in the sources; confirm registration and rate locally.', 'Thinning', 'Preharvest growth regulator options', 'confirmed'),
	(1117, 42, NULL, 'For trees that are too vigorous, this stage records trunk scoring: a single cut circumscribing the bark on the trunk, made in a narrow window measured in days after full bloom and repeated annually where needed. Alongside it, thinning cuts are used to open the canopy so light reaches spurs throughout the tree rather than only at the outside, which is what keeps them fruitful. Late summer pruning is also listed for this period. The exact days-after-bloom figures differ between sources, so check which one applies to your system.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Scoring and summer canopy work', 'confirmed'),
	(1072, 6, NULL, 'Root protection runs through most of this guidance: keep roots covered with tarps, wet down any that look dry, and do not let them dry out between delivery and planting. Trees go in while completely dormant, and planting depth is set by holding the rootstock shank a recorded distance clear of ground level so the graft union stays above soil. In-row spacing has a stated upper limit for the system described, and the expected tree quality is a well-feathered nursery tree. Fall planting is treated as conditional, working only where nursery trees entered dormancy early and dropped their leaves before lifting.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Planting day handling and depth', 'confirmed'),
	(951, 45, 81, 'Treat thinning with extra caution in a block that had a dormancy-breaking treatment. The compressed, uneven bloom that follows means the fruitlets are at different stages when the spray lands, and the response spreads accordingly.', 'Thinning', 'Thinners after a dormancy-breaking treatment', 'confirmed'),
	(101, 34, 81, 'Apply contact insecticides when fruit damage or feeding is detected.', 'IPDM', 'Western flower thrips', 'confirmed'),
	(107, 46, 81, 'Maintain fungicide coverage for scab, mildew, and fruit rots during cover sprays.', 'Disease management', 'General disease control', 'confirmed'),
	(108, 46, 81, 'Rotate insecticides for pests such as codling moth, aphids, and stink bugs in late spring and summer.', 'IPDM', 'Insect pest control', 'confirmed'),
	(969, 6, 81, 'Mechanical hedging substitutes speed for selection: it cuts to a plane rather than to a bud, so it suits a canopy already built as a wall and produces regrowth at every cut. Decide whether the system is being hedged before it is planted, not afterwards.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Mechanical hedging', 'confirmed'),
	(110, 61, 81, 'Use PGRs like prohexadione-calcium to control excessive shoot growth in summer.', 'Plant growth regulator', 'Vegetative growth control', 'confirmed'),
	(793, 6, 81, 'Where flowering crabapples are used, use ones with proven compatible pollen for the variety being grown. Ornamental value and flowering time are not evidence of compatibility.', 'Pollination', 'Crabapples need proven pollen', 'confirmed'),
	(797, 8, 81, 'Where pollinizers are grafted into trees already in the ground, place the grafts low in the tree. High grafts get shaded and pruned out, and the pollen ends up above the flowers it was meant to serve.', 'Pollination', 'Grafting pollinizers into an existing block', 'confirmed'),
	(794, 6, 81, 'Place pollinizers at 15 to 20 metre intervals in each row and offset alternate rows by half that interval, so no part of the block is at the far end of two gaps at once.', 'Pollination', 'Pollinizer spacing pattern', 'confirmed'),
	(712, 6, 81, 'Every planting needs cross-pollination, from pollinizer varieties whose bloom overlaps the main cultivar. Plan on no fewer than three pollen-compatible varieties, or at minimum two to three cultivars with slightly staggered bloom, so a short or wet bloom does not leave the block without overlap.', 'Pollination', 'Pollinizers in every planting', 'confirmed'),
	(713, 6, 81, 'A block containing triploid cultivars needs three pollinizer varieties: the triploid contributes no viable pollen of its own, so the pollinizers must also pollinate each other.', 'Pollination', 'Triploid blocks', 'confirmed'),
	(792, 6, 81, 'Beyond compatible pollen and bloom overlap, choose a pollinizer with little biennial tendency -- one that skips a year leaves the block without pollen in that year -- with fruit visibly different from the target so it is not picked into the line, and compact or spur-type so it takes as little of the block as possible.', 'Pollination', 'What else the pollinizer has to be', 'confirmed'),
	(955, 17, 81, 'Notch above a bud two to four weeks before full bloom to force it out. Notching much later than that reaches a bud that has already decided what it is doing this season.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Notching before bloom', 'confirmed'),
	(1014, 45, 81, 'Where the two conflict, time chemical thinning by temperature rather than by fruit diameter or days after full bloom. Diameter and days describe where the fruit is; temperature decides what the spray will do when it gets there.', 'Thinning', 'Temperature before diameter', 'confirmed'),
	(1104, 66, NULL, 'Coverage here is indexed by tree size. Entries step through standing trees of increasing height and spread for late-season dilute spraying, with the matching application held in the source. Nothing is cultivar or product specific; the point is putting enough volume onto a full canopy of a given size.', 'IPDM', 'Late-season dilute spray volumes', 'confirmed'),
	(1115, 66, NULL, 'From mid-season through to preharvest, scab monitoring shifts to terminal shoots and fruit, with a defined sampling protocol: a set number of terminals taken from each compass direction and a set number of fruit per tree, including fruit from the interior canopy, with lesion counts recorded. A low count of infected fruit per tree is treated as a damaging level where the crop is destined for fresh market. On the spray side, cover sprays are maintained into the later part of the programme and a fruit-rot material is added to the final cover. Blocks on susceptible rootstocks are also checked after fruit set for further crown or collar rot symptoms.', 'Disease management', 'Mid-season scab and rot scouting', 'confirmed'),
	(1090, 66, NULL, 'Symptom recognition is what this cell provides for the summer disease complex. Sooty blotch and flyspeck are described as surface blemishes strongly favoured by frequent rain and poor drying, with a predictive model driven by wetting hours accumulated from a fixed point after petal fall. Canker diseases get the fullest treatment, from early-summer blisters that enlarge, sink and fill with fluid, through fusion of cankers that can girdle and kill large limbs, to the cracked, sloughing bark of older cankers. Brooks fruit spot is noted as a latent leaf infection that surfaces late in the season, and graft union necrosis through its reddish foliage, poor vigour and abnormally heavy bloom and set.', 'Disease management', 'Summer disease and canker symptoms', 'confirmed'),
	(1061, 66, NULL, 'Sunburn protection here is handled with protectant sprays applied to the fruit surface, and the entries name specific products along with their application rates and spray volumes. Timing is keyed to fruit size or to the approach of a burning event, with the first application going on ahead of damaging conditions rather than in response to them, and a follow-up to maintain cover. Summer pruning before harvest also appears in this cell. The rates themselves are held in the source.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Sunburn protectant spray programme', 'confirmed'),
	(775, 47, 81, 'Limit shoot and leaf removal in summer pruning: the leaves being removed are the ones filling the fruit. Take whole shoots out with thinning cuts after terminal buds have formed rather than heading everything back, and note that summer pruning timing also affects fruit calcium.', 'Pruning & Training', 'How much to take in summer', 'confirmed'),
	(552, 66, 81, 'Schedule a reflective sunburn protectant off full bloom rather than off the first hot spell: the first application goes on seven to nine weeks after full bloom on susceptible cultivars.', 'Environmental stress management', 'Sunburn protectant timing', 'confirmed'),
	(1003, 52, 81, 'Where a fruit-attacking population is high going into harvest, the pre-harvest cover is applied to the point of leaf saturation across trees and fruit. It is the one application in the programme where coverage matters more than material choice.', 'IPDM', 'Saturation cover before harvest', 'confirmed'),
	(1091, 56, NULL, 'Nutrition after harvest is treated as the chance to refill tree reserves while leaves are still green and functioning. Boron, zinc sulphate, copper and nitrogen all carry postharvest or dormant timings, and the guidance repeatedly conditions the spray on leaves remaining active on the tree. Copper and boron are also listed as soil applications with no timing restriction. Calcium spray programmes are cultivar-specific, and the number of sprays for a given orchard is set from fruitlet mineral analysis sampled at a stated point after full bloom.', 'Nutrient management', 'Postharvest and dormant nutrient sprays', 'confirmed'),
	(700, 56, 81, 'Put post-harvest nitrogen on through fertigation or as a foliar spray rather than broadcasting it. The tree is taking up into reserves for a short window, and broadcast nitrogen mostly does not reach it in time.', 'Nutrient management', 'Post-harvest nitrogen placement', 'confirmed'),
	(266, 50, 81, 'Apply a fruit-retaining bioregulator -- an AVG or a 1-MCP product registered for pre-harvest use -- to reduce drop and hold the picking window open.', 'Harvest management', 'Pre-harvest drop control', 'confirmed'),
	(891, 52, 81, 'Start maturity testing about a fortnight before the expected harvest date and repeat weekly, or more often as the date approaches. Track it by objective measurement rather than by how the block looks.', 'Harvest management', 'When maturity testing starts', 'confirmed'),
	(899, 67, 81, 'Check and treat bins at regular intervals through harvest. A bin carrying rot inoculum or a broken board undoes the handling care taken everywhere else in the chain.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Bins through harvest', 'confirmed'),
	(854, 67, 81, 'Handle fruit carefully picking and transferring from bag to bin. Most postharvest rot enters through an injury, which makes handling a disease control measure rather than a quality one.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Handling as disease control', 'confirmed'),
	(666, 8, 81, 'An established block can be changed to another variety by grafting over the existing trees, using the step-graft method, rather than pulling and replanting -- which keeps the root system and the support structure and brings the new variety into cropping years earlier.', 'Pruning & Training', 'Changing variety by grafting over', 'confirmed'),
	(703, 7, 81, 'Match the tie to the trunk and change it as the trunk grows: tape while the trunk is under about 10 to 12 mm, 14 mm staples up to about 20 mm, and 18 or 19 mm staples once the trunk passes 20 mm. A tie left too small girdles the tree it was installed to support.', 'Modify Landscape', 'Tie and staple size by trunk diameter', 'confirmed'),
	(807, 9, 81, 'Test for nematodes before replanting, particularly on light soils where populations build and the replant response is sharpest. It is the test that decides whether the site needs a treatment or only a rest.', 'Disease management', 'Nematode test on light soils', 'confirmed'),
	(825, 6, 81, 'Match the production system to the horticultural skill available to run it. A system that needs precise annual renewal pruning and gets approximate pruning produces less than a simpler system run properly.', 'Pruning & Training', 'System against the grower''s skill', 'confirmed'),
	(1052, 50, 81, 'Reflective mulch laid a few days before picking does not have time to act. Where it is being used to lift colour it has to be down well before ripening begins; a last-minute laying is an expense against fruit whose colour is already set.', 'Orchard floor management', 'Reflective mulch laid days out is too late', 'confirmed'),
	(494, 53, NULL, 'Consider dynamic controlled atmosphere as an alternative to a chemical scald inhibitor. Dynamic CA guided by chlorophyll fluorescence, and repeated low-oxygen stress followed by ultra-low oxygen, suppressed superficial scald for ten months of storage plus seven days of shelf life, and held higher flesh firmness and soluble solids.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Dynamic controlled atmosphere', 'confirmed'),
	(750, 53, 81, 'Apply 1-MCP within seven days of harvest for scald control, and within five days on the earliest-picked Granny Smith. It also needs fruit picked at the maturity that would normally be stored under controlled atmosphere -- late fruit does not respond to it.', 'Post-harvest handling', '1-MCP application window', 'confirmed'),
	(752, 53, 81, 'Where diffuse flesh browning is the risk, hold the fruit at 3 C rather than colder, and market it within about five months of harvest where the block''s risk index is high. Both are decisions made at intake, not when the disorder appears.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Storage temperature against flesh browning', 'confirmed'),
	(753, 53, 81, 'Monitor fruit quality and maturity through the storage period, not only at intake and outturn. The decision the monitoring supports is when to market a lot, and it can only be made while there is still time to move it.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Monitor through storage', 'confirmed'),
	(855, 53, 81, 'Latent rots such as bull''s eye rot are decided before the fruit is picked: select a preharvest material against them, and follow with a postharvest treatment applied as a drench at harvest. Nothing applied after symptoms appear in store affects the outcome.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Latent rot programme', 'confirmed'),
	(902, 53, 81, 'Restrict 1-MCP to fruit designated for intermediate or long-term storage, picked at correct maturity. It should not be relied on in a block treated to hasten maturity, and the pre-harvest form of the treatment needs cool, slow-drying conditions to be taken up.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Where 1-MCP applies', 'confirmed'),
	(903, 53, 81, 'Where the cultivar needs it, cool in steps -- down to about 5 C for two weeks before reducing further -- rather than straight to the storage temperature. The staged drop avoids the chilling injury that the final temperature would otherwise cause.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Stepwise cooling', 'confirmed'),
	(904, 53, 81, 'Modified-atmosphere box liners extend the life of marginal lines, but the first decision on at-risk fruit is to market it early rather than hold it. Storage does not improve fruit; it only slows what is already happening.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Marginal lines and at-risk fruit', 'confirmed'),
	(905, 53, 81, 'Bring fruit into the correct temperature band before drenching -- roughly 15 to 30 C -- and allow drainage time of several minutes after any chlorine or water pre-wash, so the drench is not diluted by what is still on the fruit.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Preparing fruit for a drench', 'confirmed'),
	(906, 53, 81, 'Run the pump and agitator for at least five minutes before the first bin goes through, so the solution is mixed rather than settled, and hold each bin in the drench for a defined minimum contact time. Buffer the pH where the co-applied fungicide requires it.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Running the drench', 'confirmed'),
	(907, 53, 81, 'A drench is consumed as it works. Test the strength after a set number of bins, top up on a bins-per-volume basis, and replace the solution outright rather than topping up indefinitely, or once it passes its age limit. A drench past that point is applying water and colour.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'When a drench is spent', 'confirmed'),
	(908, 53, 81, 'Use a scald forecasting system to decide whether the antioxidant treatment is needed this season before deciding how to run it. In a low-risk season the treatment is a cost with no return, and the forecast is available before the fruit is picked.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Whether the drench is needed at all', 'confirmed'),
	(991, 53, 81, 'Size the drench plant to the harvest rather than to the bin. Published systems run a reservoir of one and a half to two thousand litres with a pumping rate around a thousand litres a minute for a single tier, doubling and tripling that rate for two- and three-tiered systems, and hold each bin in solution for at least ten seconds. Undersized, the plant becomes the bottleneck that keeps fruit standing in the yard.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Sizing the drench plant', 'confirmed'),
	(992, 53, 81, 'Set the drench concentration by variety rather than running one strength for the season. Varieties differ in how much they need and in how much they will tolerate, and the difference is larger than the year-to-year variation the standard rate was chosen for.', 'Post-harvest handling', 'Concentration by variety', 'confirmed');
